      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>The LIST will be CLOSED on or before Wednesday, the 18th January, 1893. the El Shares of the Dunlop Pneumatic Tyre are now stand- ing at about £l5. The El Shares ,of the Seddon's Pneumatic Tyre are now standing at about 452. The £1 Shares of the Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre Sydicate Limited, are now standing at about 355. THE " PERSIL" FLEXIBLE WHEEL TYRE SYNDICATE, LIMITED. Incorporated under the Companies ACts of 1862 to 1820, which limit the liability to the amount of the shares. Capital, £170,000 divided into 170,000 shares of Ll each. Issue of 16,000 shares of El each, at ss. premium, being the balance of the company's working capital, payable as follows :-10a. on application, and 15s. on allotment. DIRECTORS :</p>
        <p>Jacob K. Marshall, Esq—...-11;:(lifeesrs. Marshall and Son's. Victoria Mills, Great Grimsby), chairman. Forster, Esq. (Keenlyside, Forster, and Forster, New- castle-on-Tyne). vice-president, Harrogate Camp. Arthur Illingworth, Esq.. 33, Charles-st.. Bradford. Isaac .Molloy, Esq. (Messrs. Molloy and Molloy), Eustace-st.,</p>
        <p>Dublin. _ Eugene Persil, Esq.. Paris. Smithett Brinsley-Sheridan, Esq., 54. Piccadilly, London, Managing Director. BANKERS : The York City and County Banking Company, Ltd., and</p>
        <p>Branches. Messrs. Thomas Nash and Sons, 68, Cross-street, Manchester Commercial Bank of Scotland, Edinburgh and Branches. The Bradford Commercial Joint Stock Banking td. _ _ _ . _ _ . , . ... ..• Co..''</p>
        <p>The Belfast Banking Co.. Ltd.. Belfast and Dublin. AUDITORS—Messrs. W. 0. Clough and Co., London and Leeds.</p>
        <p>SOLICITORS:</p>
        <p>Messrs. Dix and Warlo-w, 4, Danes Inn, Strand, London, and Newcastle-on-Tyne. OFFICES: Portland House, Dasinghall-street. London, B.C. 31 and 35, Royal Exchange, Manchester. SECRETARY—Mr. Walter J. Creaser. GENERAL AGE,T—Tom Taylor, Esq. PROSPECTUS. _ _ -</p>
        <p>This Syndicate Comi;an-y—w-as incorporated on August lath 11032:.to purchase the invention for Great Britain and Ireland a Mons. Persil's Flexible Wheel Tyre. The Syndicate has acquired snace that date other inven- tions in connection with flexible tyres. This tyre is suitable to all kinds of bicycles and tricycles, and can be axed to existing wheels at a comparatively low coot.</p>
        <p>It has been long felt that for cycling purposes a new tyre is necessary to obviate the disadvantages, which are admittedly annoying to all cyclists. The "Persil" tyre not only abolishes the cardinal objection of *" puncturing ` and " skidding," so common to pneumatic tyres. but it reduces vibration to a minimum ; in addition it is claimed that it increases the speed over roads even of the roughest character. • Unlike all or any existing tyres. which are only suitable for bicycles and tricycles, it is claimed for the •' Persil" that it , is adaptable to every description of carriage, such as bro sag- I hams, victories, hansom cabs, omnibuses, carts, peramulat ors bath chairs, invalid and other similar vehicles. The Syndicate entered into an agreement for the manufac- ture of their wheels and tyres with Messrs. George Salter and Co., the well-known spring makers, 0 West Bromwich, after this firm had exhaustively tested the durability and strength of the tyres. The Syndicate therefore are now in a position to execute their numerous orders. Agents and travellers are being appointed all over the United Kingdom, and orders are being received from all parts. The following are a few extracts from the many reports which the -directors have received upon this tyre. From these it will be seen that speaking of the " Persil' Tyre Mr. ?red Wood says : "It is the tyre of the torture." Mr. Radcliffe says : •• I consider it the best yet sub- mitted to the cycling world." Mr. W. B. Kirsop says :—" Your patent, in my opinion, has a big future." Mr. W. Howitt says --'• It contains the best principles as a touring wheel I have yet ridden." Mr. Jerry Cleminson says:—" It is perfect in every res- pect and is certain to bea big success." Mr. Edward Clerk says : -" It is as nearly as possible perfection." Mr. George R. Palmer BITS A great success awaits ite adoption through the world of road riders." Mr. D. G-. Anderson says : —" There is a great future tor this wheel-, and no road rider in 1893 will be without it." The " Newcastle Daily Journal "says :—Our own minion Is that for roadster 'purposes the new tyre is the best thing yet introduced to the cycling world." Mr. Koenan says -" It has fairly surprised me." Mr. Staveacre says : "I was particularly struck with its hill-mounting qualities, and with the fact that 'skidding 'was an almost unknown quality." Professor Steuart says:—" I confess it more than sur- prised me by its good qualities and great promise." The directors believe that large profits will be made from the sale of these tyres, there being an ample margin between the manufacturers' price and that received by the Com- pany. The profits which are likely to accrue may be estimated from the fact that there are in existence thousands of solid tyres, and other practically obsolete types of cycles, which are lying comparatively useless, but which, by the adoption of the" Persil," can be converted to modern machines at a moderate cost. The purchase price which was paid by this Company for the patent was £150,00-, which was accepted by the vendors entirely In fully-paid shares. £2 00 remained for working capital, which sum, as the Company does not itself manufac- ture, is deemed amply sufficient. The balance of the work- ing capital, £l6, 0, is now offered to the public at ss. pre- mium.</p>
        <p>The following agreements have been entered into ": Bet ween Eugene Persil and S. B. Sheridan. and the Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre Syndicate Limited 15th August. 1893. Eugene Persil and S. B. Sheridan, and the Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre Syndicate Limited, 16th August, 1892, The Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre Syndicate Limited, and S. B. Sheridan, January stb, 1893. Tom Taylor, S. B. Sheridan, and the Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre Syndicate Limited, Jan., 1893. The Persil Fb•xible Wheel Tyre Syndicate Limited, and Messrs. George Salter and Co., 7th November, 1892. S. B. Sheridan and the Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre Syndicate. Limited, 15th August, 1892. T. Taylsr, S. B. Sheridan, and the Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre, Syndicate, Limited. 26th November, 1892. T. Taylor and S. B. Sheridan 24th Nov.. 1892. Letter from E. Persil and S. B, Sheridan to the Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre Syndicate, Limited. 16th August, 1892. Thnmas Nash and &amp;Ins and the Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre Syndicate Limited, Nov. 7th, 1892. Application %ill be made for a settlement upon the Stock Exchange....</p>
        <p>Copies of the memorandum and articles of Aa.ociation may be Been at the offices of the Company's solicitors, or at the offices of the Company.</p>
        <p>Prospectuses and forms of application may be obtained at the bankers, solicitors. and offices of the Corn pany. Where no allotment is made, the deposit will be returned in full, and where a smaller number of shares is allotted than is applied for, the application money will be appropriated on account of alladdnent mon.ey, and.cy excess returned to the applicant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>COUNTY OF CHESTER. CONTAGIOUS DISEASES (ANIMALS) ACTS 1878 and 1888. ANTHRAX ORDER, 1892. OTICE IS HEREBY `GWEN, that at a Meeting of 1:11 the Executive Committee of the Local Authority County of Chester,held at the Crewe Arms Hotel,Crewe, on Friday, the 30th day of December, 1892, IT WAS ORDERED that the following regulations, made pur- suant to the above Order, shall, on and after the 11th day ctf January, 1883, come into operation, and take effect within the District, under the jurisdiction of this Local Authority, until otherwise modified or revoked.</p>
        <p>REGULAHONS AS TO MOVEMENT OF ANIMALS, FODDER, Stc. S.. No diseased or suspected Animal .shall be moved into or out of any shed, stable, building, field, or other place, or any part thereof, except only for the purpose of isolation on the same premises. 2. No Animal shall be moved into or out of any shed, stable, building,, field,or other place, or any part thereof, in which there is or has been any diseased or suspected Animal. provided, nevertheless, that this regulation shall ope*ate so long only as any diseased or suspected Animal remains in the shed, stable, building, field, or other place to which•the regulation refers, and in case of a shed, stable, building, or other like place, until the same has been cleansed and disinfected in accordance with the order.</p>
        <p>3. That all fodder, litter,or other thing, that has been in contact with, or used for or about any diseased or suspected animal, shall not be removed out of any shed, stable, building, field, or other place, excepting in the presence of an Inspector of the Local Authority, and in the case of fodder until the same has been disinfected to the satisfaction of an Inspector of the Local Authority.</p>
        <p>CLEANSING AND DISINFECTION. 4. All those parts of any shed, stable, building, or other place, in which a diseased or suspected animal has been kept or has died or been slaughtered ; and every utensil, pen hurdle, or other thing user for or about any diseased or suspected animal ; and also every van, cart, or other vehicle, used for carrying any diseased or suspected animal on land otherwise than a railway, shall be cleansed and disinfected at the expense of this Local Authority and under the direction and supervision of one of itslnspectors by the Owner or Occupier thereof. The mode of the cleansing and disinfection of such shed, stable, building, or other place, or the part there- of, shall beam follows : (1) All those parts aforesaid of the shed, stable, building, or other place, shall be swept out, and all litter, dung, or other thing that has been in contact with, or used about, any diseased or suspected animal,shall be effectually removed therefrom ; then (ii.) The floor and all other parts of the shed, stable, building, or other place with which the diseased or suspected animal or its droppings or any discharge from the mouth or nostrils of the animal has come In contact, shall be, as far as practicable, ' thoroughly washed or scrubbed or scoured with water: then (iii.) The same parts of the shed, stable, building, or other place, hall be washed over with limewash made of freshly burst lime and water, and contain- ' ing in each gallon of limewash four ounces of chloride of lime or half a pint of commercial car- bolic acid, the limewash being prepared immediately before use : (iv.) Except that where any place as aforesaid is not capable of being so cleansed and disinfected, it shall be sufficient if such place be cleansed and disinfected as far as practicable. Tha mode of the cleansing and disinfection of such utensil, pen, hurdle, or other thing, and such van, cart, or other vehicle aforesaid shall be as follows :</p>
        <p>(I.) Each utensil, pen, hurdle, or other: thing, van, cart, or other vehicle shall be thoroughly scraped, and all litter, dung, sawdust, or other thing shall bo effectually removed therefrom : then (ii.) It shall be thoroughly washed or scrubbed or scoured with water : then (iii.) It shall be washed over with limewash made of freshly burnt lime and water, and containing in each gallon of limewash four ounces of chloride of lime or half a pint of commercial carbolic acid, the limewash being prepared immediately before use. All litter, dung, or other thing that has been removed from any such shed, stable, building, place, van, cart, or vehicle as aforesaid, shall be forthwith burnt or otherwise destroyed or disinfected to the satisfaction of an Inspector of the Local Authority.</p>
        <p>MILK. OF DISEASED .OR SUSPECTED COW NOT TO</p>
        <p>BE REMOVED.</p>
        <p>5. Where anthrax exists or has existed in any shed, stable, building, or other plan,: it shall not be lawful to remove from such shed, stable, or building, or other place the milk of any cow which is affected with anth- rax.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>DISPOSAL OF CARCASES.</head>
        <p>DISPOSAL OF CARCASES.</p>
        <p>6. The carcase of every animal that was at the time when it died or was slaughtered, affected with Anthrax shall be disposed of by the owner thereof, tinder the direction and supervision of an Inspector of the Local Authority, and at the expense of the Local Authority, as follows :</p>
        <p>(1) By causing such carcase to be buried as soon as possible in its skin in some proper place, at a depth of not less than six feet below the surface of the earth, and to be covered with a sufficient quantity of quicklime or other disinfectant, and before re- moving such carcase for burial as aforesaid by covering the same with quicklime, and in no case shall the skin of the carcase be cut, no shall anything be done to cause the effusion of blood. INTERPRETATION. - - -</p>
        <p>Animals includes,.Vit—h—lhe--a;fr;-:aii. specified in ,the</p>
        <p>Act of 1878 (tha't is cattle, sheep and goats, and all other ruminating animals, and swine), horses, asses, and mules : Disease means anthrax, and diseased animals means an animal affected with anthrax, and suspected animal . . . .</p>
        <p>means an animal suspected of being affected with anthrax: Carcase means the carcase of an animal, and part of such a carcase, and the meat, flesh, bones, hide, skin, hoofs, horns, offal, or other part ot an animal, separ- ately or otherwise, or any portion thereof. Fodder means hay or other substance commonly used for food of animals : Litter means straw, or other substance commonly used for bedding or otherwise for or about animals. PENALTIES. .</p>
        <p>Any offence against the above Regulations is deemed an offenve against the Act of 1878, whiah renders the offender lsable to a penalty of not exceedi, Twenty Pounds.</p>
        <p>Lng DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>The District of the Local Authority of the County of Chester does not include the city of Chester, nor any of the Boroughs, either wholly or partly within this County.</p>
        <p>REGINALD POTTS, Clerk to the Cheshire County Council. Ch,este,r, 10th January, 1893.</p>
