      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>Sarasate has had thirty-two watches given him at various times, several in the shape of violins. He could use a different watch every day if he fancied it.</p>
        <p>The last day of the frost was taken advantage ot by Captain Ronald Scott to give a skating party at the Elms, Acton-hill, What makes The Elms skat- ing parties so delightful, apart horn the fact of the perfectly kept ice and the pleasant surroundings, is the charming novelty of skating at night by daylight. It is even better than daylight, for Cap- tam Scott's powerful electric searchlights give a much better illumination than foggy London has been treated to 14ely.</p>
        <p>Professor E. S. Beesley is the editor of the " Positivist Review," which has just been issued, and Mr. Frederick Harrison leads off the first num- ber with a strong article on " Home Rule for London," Dr. J. H. Bridges writes a thoughtful article of" Experiments on Living knimals," and the editor has an article on payment of members. There is nothing directly refering to Positivism in the review, but then Positivism covers the general progress of humanity on an orderly and altruistic</p>
        <p>Whisiler, who.has now taken up his abode in Paris, is having a great artistic success over there, painting several portraits, charging £l,OOO each. The portrait he painted of his aged mother years ago is now hanging in the Luxembourg Gallery, and is extremely admired by the French painters. Mr. Frank Dicksee, R.A., is painting an important picture for the next Royal Academy, "The Death of a Viking." Sir Frederick Leighton has a large picture in hand, " Rizpah Defending the Dead Bodies of her Sons from the Tigers and Vulters." A big stuffed tiger is now to be seen in Sir Frederick's studio.</p>
        <p>Literary men are notoriously careless of their attire, and the present address of the Authors' club —which is to be changed in the course of the month, when their new club-house in Whitehall will be opened—happens to be near to Arthur's,the members of which give more attention to the details of their costume. A distinguished young Scotch writer was the other day made the subject of an amusing experience. It is not commonly known that the Authors have their quarters in the Hotel round the corner of St. James's place, and the popul4r writer having asked a porter to direct him to the Authors' Club was mortified at finding him- self directed to "the kitchen entrance to Arthur's."</p>
        <p>Miss Frances E. Willard, president of the World's Women's Christian Temperance Union, was born in New York in 1839, and is therefore fifty-four years of age. Although only farmers in Wisconsin, her parents were cultured and refined, and most of Miss Willard's education was received from then, only two years being spent at college. For many years a schoolmistress, it was not until 1873 that she commenced her more public career. In that year began the great temperance crusade, which re- sulted in the almost total suppression of the liquor traffic in ratvly states of America; and it is not, perhaps, too much to say that a very large share of the success achieved by that movement was due to Miss Willard's energy and enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>Miss Frances Willard:t home is at East Cottage, in the prohibition town of Evanston, 111., about a dozen miles from Chicago. It is a kind of bunga- low residence, opening on to a smooth lawn by French windows, and almost hidden from view by tall fir trees. In the same town is located the North Western University, of which Miss Willard is a graduate and former professor. The charter of the university contains a stipulation that " no spiritu- ous, vinous, or fermented liquors shall be sold tin- der any license or otherwise within four miles of ths university, except for naPdicinal, mechanical, or sacramental, purposes, under a penalty of twenty- five dollars." " This." says Miss Willard, " has kept Evanston free from saloons and hotel bars for well-nigh forty years."</p>
        <p>Mr. Samson Fox, who has come in for such hostile ritieism lately in connection with his water-gas schemes, is a big burly Yorkshireman, who like a good many other money makers started life at the bottom of the ladder. He is a well-known figure in commercial circles at Leeds, while in the town of Harrogate he was a leading light, being Mayor for two successive years. Ile lives in a roomy old house in that quarter of the town known as " High Harrogate," where he has entertained from time to time on a very large scale. Mr. Fox is chiefly known in London as the donor of the munificent sum of £15,000 towards the Royal College of Music. There are those who say that he had set his heart on getting a baronetcy, but recent events will not help it.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Campbell-Praed has just lost her father, the Ron. T. L. Murray-Prior, who died in Brisban&amp; a few days ago. lie had been Chairman of the Com- mittees of the Legislative Council since July, 1889. Previously he had been Postmaster-General in three Queensland Ministries: He may be decribed as one of the pioneers of Queensland, for as a young add adventurous Englishman he settled there more than half a century ago, when it was a vast, uncivilised, neglected province of New South Wales. He was a native of Wells, Somerset, and had attained his seventy-third year. His daughter, Miss Rote Caroline Murray-Prior, is, of course, a native Of Queensland, and the Australi3n scenes and incidents that figure so prominently in nearly all her noveis are transcripts from her girlish life and experiences in that tropical colony.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harrell, the new Under Secretary for Ireland, is not a politician, but a tried administrator. He was for many years a police inspector in Belfast, --where he took an active part in suppressing some of the riots which have brought such disgrace on the -" loyalists "of that city ; and afterwards, as a resi- • dent magistrate 'in the West of Ireland, he suC- ,ceeded in gaining the respect of all classes alike. But his greatest success has been as chief of the Dublin Metropolitan Police, a body Which he has divested of its military pretensions and transforroo ,into what the constabulary in the rest of Ireland should be—a purely civic force. The result of his policy is shown in the fact that the metropolitan police acting as the servants, not the nasters of the masters of the people, have become distinctly popu- lar, whilst the constabulary in the rest of the coun- try are generally execrated. Mr. Harrell is a C.B.</p>
        <p>There were two notable women on the platform --at Exeter Hall on Monday night—Lady Henry .Somerset and Madame Antoinette Sterling. Lady Henry is the daughter of the third Earl Somers, at whose death she became owner of gastnor Castle. 'The principle which animates her' in her temperance and social purity crusade may be found in her own words :—" The profit of humanity should, the absorbing enterprise of our life." MadaniecAn- tainette Sterling is known and loved by thousands for her bounteous gifts of song to the poor at the Victoria.B.all, in Lambeth. It is no unusual thing to see her respond four or five times to the demands -of her entirely working-class hearers, which is a favour she does not always grant to her more favoured West-end audiences. She was born in the village of Stirlingville, in the State of New York, and claims direct descent from William Bradford, who was one of those who went out in the historic Mayflower.</p>
        <p>The Ranee of Sarawak, who will shortly pay a visit to this country, though she bears an Oriental title, is an Englishwoman, a native sf the West Country, and a relation both by blood and marriage of the celebrated Sir James Brooke, who was born at Bath, whose great service to the Sultan of Borneo, were recompensed by. the Rajahsbip of the province of Sarawak in 1841. Rajah Brooke's stern ' repression of the slave trade was highly appreciated in England, and a small estate, Barrator, on wild and breezy Dartmoor, near Sheepstor's granite height, was purchased for him by public subscrip- tion. Here he retired when he gave up his active career in Borneo, and here he died in 1868. The lady, who is uow in Paris, and will ere ling be in London, married her cousin, the nephew of the late Sir James, whose po.tronymie was Johnson, but who smined the name of Brooke on receiving his uncle's title of Rajah, and succeeding to his posi- tion in the East, when the great Sir James took up bis residence in Devonshire.</p>
        <p>Adare Manor, whet.° the Earl of Dunrayen has been entertaining Lord Randolph Churchill and numerous other guests, is one of the finest seats in the south west of Ireland. It stands a few miles south of Limerick, close by the river Maigne, which here glides past the iuius of three abbeys and an c;por ivy-mantled castle. The manor, which has th.,• n the plar.e or an older edifice, was built at the .b.tzinning of the century, and is a picturesque and steetely stmcture in the Tudor style. On the south•</p>
        <p>" CAPITAL" CHAT. 1 side a broad flight of steps, adorned with vases, • leads to the river, which reflects the oriels, orna- mental chimney stacks, and grey limestone walls of the mansion. The lofty entrance hall contains an organ,and is decorated with armour and the horns of old Irish deer. The picture gallery is a hundred and thirty-two feet in length. There is also a museum. Among the ruins is the family mausol- eum. Much of Adare Manor was built in famine times to give employment to the men, while the village girls were set to work the tapestry which covers some of the furniture.</p>
        <p>Three miles south-west of Selkirk is Bcwhill House, a favourite seat of the present Duke of Buccleuch, who is residing there just now for the purpose of entertaining a party of friends. It is one of five .fine mansions which the Duke possesses in Scotland, the other four being Aal- keith House, Edinburgh, The Lodge, Langholm, Drumlanrig Castle, so Splendidly situated in fertile and beautiful country, and Eildon Hall, Rox- burghshire, where ibrd and the Lady Dalkeith, that is to be, will live after their honeymoon. To set against all these, the duke has only one residence south of the Cheviots apart from his London house. Bowhill is a modern hunting seat, situated near the confluence of the Ettick and the Yarrow. The grounds,which are beautifully wooded, extend for two miles along the latter stream, and are reached by the General's-bridge, constructed by General Mackay. The principal object of interest is Newark Castle, a massive square tower with adjoining buildings, celebrated in "The Lay of the Last Minstrel." The Duke of Buccleuch owns over sixty thousand acres of land in Sea- kirkshire.</p>
        <p>THE WELSH LAND SYSTEM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>DEMAND FOR REFORM.</head>
        <p>A DEMAND FOR REFORM.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. E. Ellis, M.P., speaking on Monday night at Harlech at a meeting of the Western Merioneth Farmers Society, gave a very depressing description of the state of agriculture in Wales. The conviction was, he said, overwhelming that the English land system in that country had broken down. Rents had gone up to an unreasonable height, and landlords often spent the money which should be devoted to labourers' dwellings and farmsteads upon pheasantries, rabbit warrens, and battues. Almost every civilised community in Europe had regulated the relations of landlord and tenant so as to save the latter from the capricious and ruinous exac- tions of the former, and Mr. Gladstone had done the same in Ireland and Scotland. Now the question had been taken up in Wales, and they must not be satisfied until they had secured the establishment of a land court to fix rents and assess compensation for improvements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CORPSE-CANDLE.</head>
        <p>THE CORPSE-CANDLE.</p>
        <p>A THEORY OF THE WHITECHAPEL MURDERS.</p>
        <p>The corpse-candle is a weird implement of the robber's trade, which, although common through- out Europe, seems to have been more general in Germany than in any other country. Not so long ago it may be remembered how four peasants in the South Russian government ot Kursk murdered a girl in order to make candles of her body; the notion being that such a hideous light not only renders the perpetrators of robberies invisible, but actually throws the victim, or victims, into a state of somnolency. In the German Criminal Codes of I the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries we find express mention made of penalties against a crime, the motives of which was the making of " thieves'," or "sleep-pro- ducing oandles." This superstition has figured in many a trial, and seems to be, even at the present day, to be implicitly believed in by the criminal classes. Indeed, it was suggested that the Whitechapel murders were in- stigated by this idea—a theory which the celebrated Dr. Bloch, a member of the Austean Reichsrath, was in- duced to support. An instance or this piece of criminal lore occurred during the trial of the German thief Theo- dore linger, who was executed at Magdeburg in 1810, when it was brought out in evidence that 3 regular traffic existed for the supply of these gruesome instruments of the burglar's trade. But, instead of making the criminal 'invisible, this grim light has more than once done the reverse, and caused him to be brought to justice.</p>
        <p>THE VETO.</p>
        <p>Of all the bugbears which the opponents of Home Rule have conjured up, the biggest and the silliest is the scare about the loss of the supremacy of the Imperial Parliament. It is difficult t 3 understand how the agita- tors who have raised their alarms on the platform and in the Press have failed to see that they were fighting a shadow. What man, or what body of men, can des- troy the supremacy of the Imperial Parliament ? Its very essence is its indestructibility. So long as Great Britain remains free, so long will the grand Parliament at Westminister remain absolutely supreme. The Im- perial supremacy must be maintained • in other words, there must be a power of veto vested' in the supreme authority of the United Kingdom. How is it to no ex- ercised ? Without pretending Ito 'conjecture the deci- sion arrived at by the Cabinet on this point, we may say that the simplest form of veto is also that which is most likely to work well and to be acceptable to both coun- tries. In other words, the veto might be placed in the hands of the Lord-Lieutenant as the,representative of the Sovereign. There would be no need to state expressly in the bill that his excellency would exercise this veto on behalf of the Imperial Government. The fact, which would be self-evident, depends upon that supremacy of the Imperial Parliament which nothing can take away.</p>
        <p>ONE OF THE RICHEST WOMEN IN THE WORLD.</p>
        <p>She is the wealthiest woman in America. It is not unlikely that she has as much money as Jay Gould has left behind him. She comes of a Quaker stock. Her ancestors were fugitives from England They were Quakers. Among her property is a house where she was born, at New Bedford, near Boston, the land upon which it was built having been bought direct from the Indians. She inherited money, married a man of means; left a widow, she has by personal effort and ability multiplied her capital until it is estimated at upwards of forty million dollars. Her name is Hetty Howland Robinson Green. She has two children, a boy and a girl, the boy a cripple, and she "works for them." with daily persistence. She dresses very plainly, and lives in the humblest way. She has saved much by spending little ; but her investments are well judged, and sive attends to them. In Chicago she owns a large carriage factory. Before the fire she had lent money upon it. She and the fire foreclosed, and in a year or two she built, reorganised, and worked the factory her- self. " Your investments are pretty well scattered ? said a friend to her the other day. "Yes," she said, "coal, iron, copper, lead, tin, cotton. land,—especi- ally on margins of cities and growing towns, villages and their plants, railroad, shipping in fact, everything it is prudent to put money in, especially real estate. I ewe a square mile west of Chicago." She has had a big fight to clear the title of this estate, and is known in nearly every court in America. She says "you can't trust anybody, especially in Chicago," and she ie more particularly down upon lawyers, judges,and juries. "There is only one judge in America whom you can't buy." But her friend says, " she spoke rather hastily in the matter, smarting, no doubt, under the worry of some unsuccessful litigation." This remarkable woman—about fifty—above the medium height, of a stately carriage, was pointed out to me lately, in Wall- St. She 13 a typical American, and what strikes me about the typical American Is that he resenibles the pictures of the early English settlers, and yet, oddly enough, is a good deal like the finest exanaple of the North American Indian. There is an incongruity in this which philosophy can no doubt straighten out. Mrs. Green suggests the physiognomy and firm, steadfast manner that must have characterised the planters of Massachusetts. She has a long, bony face, the large, searching nose of the pioneer ; the calm, grey eyes of courage, and the self-restrained manner of the Indian. Her lips are thin and welt-shaped, and when young she must have been a handsome woman—not pretty, but striking. She speaks with a strong accent, wears no jewellery, has a sufficient sense of humour to decry speculation, and to declare that she " hates business— wouldn't attend to it only for the sake of my children " —and " would rather be a society woman " than "anything else in the world."</p>
