        <p>BY MRS. HARRIETT LEWIS, Author of "The Sundered Hearts," "The Bailiff'. geheme," -"-The Double Life," Ste.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER HI. • A STRANGE INTERVIEW. All through the night te old housekeeper sat by the fire in that sick chamber with the baby on her knee while the mother slept. And while the faithful old nurse was watching through the dark hours by the bedside of her young mistress a strange scene was being enacted in the old squire's room. About an hour after the return of the wretched wanderer to her father's house another visitor made his appearance at the gate, and on sending up his</p>
        <p>nane; sto Vie master f the house was at -one( admitted. He divested himself of his outex</p>
        <p>clothinp% which WAS wet through, removed a muiller'from his neck and a slouched hat, and re•</p>
        <p>veated a pale face with sharp features, and keen piercing eyes. He followed the footman into the presence of Squire Markham without a word, and left that functionary in a state of wonderment and curiosity bordering on distraction, especially as his master, in a cold, stern voice, bade him make no mention to a living soul of his visitor's presence on pain of instant dismissal. As soon aS tbe door was closed the master of Lonemoor, who had, been Standing near the fire- place with an apparently calm demeanour, and a face as rigid as if carved in marble, sprang I orward, and said, in low but impressive accents—</p>
        <p>" Well ! What news 44 No e," was the reply ;" that is, noneworth the telling. I have left no !tone unturned to do, your biddlz,, but have failed—failed completely.' I thought so," returned the old man but I have been more successful. Would to heaven I had not ! Come hither, man, and listen—closer, do you hear? The very walls must be deaf to what I have to tell you:" His strange visitor came close to the squire, who clutched his arm, and leaning forward, whispered a few Words in his ear. The other sprang back as if he had been stung by a snake, while the squire silently pointed in the direction of the room in which his daughter lay hovering between life and death. The stranger, when he had recovered the shock the squir...'s information seemed to have given him, spoke again, this time in a calmer and more hopeful tone than he had used before. - "Poor girl—poor child !" he said. "Here—alone, and ill almost unto death. Knowing this, do you wish me to go on ?" "Aye! If I wished it before, I desire it now with</p>
        <p>ten tiMes more ardour. Go on even to the bitter end."</p>
        <p>-- "Mr. Markham, have you no mercy, no pity— ao human feeling left?' The old man gave a low cry of agony. "Mercy I What mercy have they shown to me?" he said bitterly. "What pity have they had for my outraged honour? What human feelings gave them the slightest consideration for my life ? Is my existence to be blighted, all my fondest hopes dashed to the ground and the few short Years I have to live made a curse instead of a blessing to me for nothing? No, no! lam no longer a boy, to be turned from my purpose by the breath of every fresh breeze that blows. They should have thought of all this before. They will find me as inflexible as fate. And you—if you fail me in this —if you grow chicken-hearted—l must find other agents. You are too well paid, I should think, to be sentimental." "But, Mr. Markham, she is your only child." The squire's features became, if possible, more rigid and his eyes more cruel as he impatiently waved his hand. "My daughter is dead to me for ever. Hence- . .. .</p>
        <p>These questions troubled the housekeeper night and day. She resolved to make a last appeal to her employer, and if possible revive his former love for his child. One night—a night the very counterpart of that on which Clare Markham had returned to her home, a night wild with storm and pitchy in its darkness—the old squire passed along the hall off which his daughter's room opened. Mrs. Quillet heard his step and resolved to make a bold, effort for the reinstatement of her darling. Laying down the child, she hurried into the hall and intercepted her master, leaving the door open behind her, that he might see the slim, drooping figure curled up on the couch in such a helpless attitude. "Master," said the old woman, speaking quickly lest she should lose her desolate courage, "will von come in and see Miss 7" The girl raised her head, listening. The squire halted. "Sec whom did you say he asked In a harsh</p>
        <p>and strident voice.- - " Miss Claret" faltered the woman, "your</p>
        <p>daughter, sir." "My daughter is dead !" said the</p>
        <p>uire in a</p>
        <p>t. Aghter _ sqn. voice that rang clear and cold as metal. "She died a year ago. You mean the woman in there, perhaps," and he shot a glance into the chamber. Yes, I will see her, and now." He strode to the entrance as he spoke, and motioning Mrs. Quillet to wait, entered the room and shut the door after him. A sight met his gaze as he entered that room which might have melted a heart of stone. Old Mrs. Quillet had striven hard during the time poor Clare had been there to restore it to something of its old splendour, and had succeeded, at least, in making it cheerful and bright, and now . .</p>
        <p>, it was looking at rts best. The young-mother was n reclining on a couch, and the infant born idst so much sorrow was nestling in a cradle b her side, tranquilly sleeping, unconscious happily of the storms of grief and passion which hovered around Its hapless head. Clare was intently watching it as it lay with a sad wistful expression, and so deeply wrapped in thought that she did not hear the opening of the door, or her father's footsteps as they approached the couch. - On his part he made no sign, but stood for a few minutes contemplating the mother and child in silence; but there was no softening of the hard features, no unbending of the brow, or of the set compression of the lips. Suddenly Clare looked up, and an expression of fear—nay., horror—sprang into her eyes as she recognised who it was that stood before her; but she uttered no sound. Finding that she did not speak as he had ex- pected, and hardly knowing whether or not she would understand him, though he guessed from her ' she</p>
        <p>Lgi evident recognition of him that A had recovered her reason, the squire addressed, her in harsh,</p>
        <p>grating tones. "I am come," he said, "much against my hetter judgment, to listen to any explanation you may have to give me. Whether I shall believe you or not is another matter." Clare did not answer; but the hot flush on het cheek told that she understood the taunt too well. The squire waited a short time, but receiving no reply began again. Understand me. I shall take your remaining silent as a proof of your guilt." • Still no answer. The squire had paced up and down the room, his steps growing shorter and quicker each time as he glanced from under his brows at the pale, mournful face so tenderly bowel over the sleeping child. At length he stopped and strode up close to her couch, his face crimson with passion. " Girl," he said, in a guttural voice, "I will knoit the truth, even at the peril of my soul. You have returned unbidden, unsought. You shall now con fess, and aid, not thwart me in my just revenge." Still silence fell. _</p>
        <p>.LVly lamb," said the housekeeper tenderly, ne will torgive you yet, whatever your faults. speak to me, Miss Clare; tell me that you under- stand me. Your old nurse loves you, darling." But the girl's look of despair did not lighten. 3he only sighed, and maintained that peculiar silence that so puzzled her attendant. Mrs. Quillet soon afterwards went downstairs to ler supper, the young lady having been served, uad being well enough to be left to herself. As she passed through the front hall, she opened ;he door and peered out into the night. The air vas full of whirling drifts of snow: The sky was lack as ink. A wide, wild waste of snow, track- !ess and terrible, stretched out upon the moors. Ile December wind was fierce, and keen, and shill. _ _</p>
        <p>"An awful night !" thought the housekeeper, thivering, and closing the door. "heaven help the poor to-night! It was a night less wild than this when Miss Clare came. Heaven pity ker."</p>
        <p>She went down to, the warm rcom ; her supper ;Was ready, her husband waitinz. The pair lingered sver the meal. Suddenly the crash of a door damming startled them both. "The housemaid," said Mrs. Quillet ; "she grows more careless every day. As I was saying, these are dark days for this old house, John. I wonder if the master will ever be reconciled to Miss Clare? She never speaks to me, but I know the has her mind again. I know she heard and anderstood every word her father said to-night, whatever it was." Aye, she had understood only too well. When him Quillet returned to that up,- chamber the girl had gone. The baby lay in the cot crying most piteously, but the young mother had vanished.</p>
        <p>'With wild terror, the housekeeper explored the adjoining rooms—the entire mansion. She re- membered the door that had been slammed so heavily, and the truth came home to her soul in one piercing conviction. Clare Markham had gone forth into the storm even as she had come—gone into the darkness, and whirling snows, and fierce Decernber blasts—gone In her weakness and despair—gone with a father's bitterest turn uponler head—gone to what fate? (To be amtinued.)</p>
        <p>...,y es.. forth lam alone, and live but for one object. It is Once Clare turned her eyes with a mute, tsp. for you to help me or not; but beware how you pealing glance to her father's face, and he almost play me false. felt his resolution give way, for it told him fa 3 His companion bowed his head. more powerfully than words, what a heartless, un "You have known me too long, I should hope, to manly part he was acting. It told him that In doubt me," he said, "and therefore will believe me should have faith in the honour of his danghtet when I tell you that I will do your bidding simply who had borne his name, and have believed in het as a machine, no matter what the result. But innocence till he had at least some evidence of her mark what I say; if you persist in your course you guilt. It told him that he should not have utterly will live to repent it most bitterly, and to curse the steeled his heart against her till he had the most hour you sent me out again on this fatal mission." convincing proof that he had nursed a viper in hit The squire laughed scornfully, bosom that had turned and stung him. It brought "I will riik all that," he returned, "and accept home to him all the folly, all the wickedness of hit your warning for what it is worth. lam resolved haste to disbelieve, but so far from softening hirt to know all that has been attempted to be con- it only made him inexorable. cealecl from me, and to wreak my vengeance re- "So," he muttered between his set teeth, "you morselessly on those who have so unscrupulously are determined to defy me to the last. Is it to is wronged me. I will not rest till I have avenged war, and not peace? So be it. I will give you five my honour, and restored my tarnished name to its minutes to consider whether you will reveal all tc old position. Then, perhaps, I can lift my head me, or whether you will take the consequence oi and face my fellow men." your obstinacy.' "I have nothing to do, then, but await your Still the same terrible silence. further commands," was the reply, in a cold, bus!- He took out his watch as he finished speaking nesslike tone, noting the hour, and then replaced it, looking ,at The squire bent his shaggy brows, and fixed his poor Clare's white face as he did so. Tears were glittering eyes on his companion, who met his sun- coursing down her cheeks, and she was rocking tc tiny unflinchingly. I and fro and sobbing as if her heart would break. "How soon can you be ready for a long journey ?" but not a syllable either as a demand for forgive began Mr. Markham. ness or in explanation escaped her lips. "At once." Her father stood by the fire watching hes The Squire made no answer; he went th a:desk on eagerly, and as he found the minutes slipping bj a side table. He unlocked it, toucted a secret and still the silence unbroken, the gathering start( spring, and revealed a drawer carefully hidden in upon his rigid features grew terrible to witness. the back of it. From this he took a small packet, He took out his watch from time to time, and without any superscription, and handed it to his at last, when the time was up, approached the visitor. The latterbtook it in silence, and placed couch slowly, and in a voice of concentrated fury, it in the breast pocket of his coat. The squire re- the more fearful on account of its apparent calm. fastened the desk, and went to an old-fashioned once more addressed the miserable girl. cabinet which stood in the corner of the room, and 'You are more reckless than I gave you credit from this he took a small canvas bag, which he for," he said. "No doubt you think it clever and, also handed to his companion, brave to remain silent; but you have yet to kno* "In that bag," he said, "you will find five hun- what you have drawn upon yourself by your die. dred pounds. Spend it freely, but to good pur- obedience and obstinacy. Whether you stay here, j pose, and when it is exhausted draw on me for a or go forth, wherever on the face of earth you may similar sum as before. I shall know whether it is be, whether rich or poor, in prosperity or adversity, applied properly. The packet will tell what to ('0 in sickness or health, you will be accompanied by 1 and where to go. Now leave me. Send me word my curse, and that curse shall fall upon your the moment you glean what I am in search of. I children's children I You shall not know a will deal with yonder difficulty—do you be as suc moment's peace; and as you have brought sorrow cessful with the other." and shame on my grey hairs, so shall you be For reply, the stranger simply bowed his head, steeped to the lips in sorrow and shame as well as and buttoning up his coat left the room, turning guilt to your dying day, should you live to your his head as he left it to see that the squire had hundredth year. ' sunk into an arm-chair, as if exhausted by the in i With this awful imprecation on his lips, and tei vi.- w. without waiting to see its effect upon his daughter, I The strange visitor made his way to the hall, he hurried from the room. Clare listened. half I resumed his wrappinks, wet as they were, shut the tainting to his fearful words, but dimly compre- outer door atter him as quietly as he could, and /tending their meaning at first; but as she grasped wont forth once more into the night to battle mith their purport, she uttered a faint cry of agony, and , the storm. . sank back on her pillow in a swoon. When the morning broke, Mrs. Quillet, who Mrs. Quillet returned to her charge as soon as , knew nothing of what had transpired between the the squire had quitted the room, and was horror- I stranger and her master, brought the child and stricken to find that she had fainted. She was I placed it in its mother's arms. Clare smiled faintly soon, however, able to restore her to consciousness, as she received it, but the smile was still vacant. kensnd with quick feminine inine tact saw that there had The girl's memory still slept; her mind was still neou unhinged. • I Still the squire did not speak, nor did he avert The old woman stole out into the hall. Her his stern compelling gaze from tee old wentan's husband was lying on the rug before the door. He worn and troubled visage. rose up as the door opened, and the faithful old [ "She—there's a little baby, sir," faltered Mrs. pair, to whom the honour of that old house was ! Quillet. "It's a girl--" almost dearer than their own, gazed into each other's The squire pointed with one long and bony fore- eyes through tears, finger towards the door, his countenance so terrible " What will the master say now ?" whispered the I that the woman was appalled. She retreated butler. I silently, and swiftly returned upstairs. "Who knows. Perhaps, as the doctor says, they'll both die. It will be best so, John, If the , There her breakfast was ssrved to her. She did child were a boy, he might make his way through not show herself again in tlie squire's presence throughout the day, nor for days afterwards, keep. the world in spite of its shame and sorrow. But it 1 , a girl, born to a hard fate, John, and let us pray ing herself shut up with her patient. that heaven will take her now in its mercy." The doctor was not sent for again, though for "Who will tell the master?" days both mother and child lay at the very gates ,"I will," declared the housekeeper. "He must Of death. know. Be sure the servants think our young lady 1 I The old housekeeper was a good nurse, and had a mere vagrant woman. I must go back to her. The•reared a family of children. S'ae took this puny child is ciying." babe to her heart, thinking with every morning She returned to her charge in haste, and remained that at nightfall the tiny creature would be on duty till her husband knocked at the door, some dead. three hours later, and informed her that the squire But the mother and the child, feeble as they was in his sitting-room, and that his breakfast was . "re' maintained their hold on life. The mother ready. i lay silent as death, watching her nurse for hours "Set the tray on the hall table and I will carry I together, and the old woman never knew whether it in," said Mrs. Quillet. "She is asleep now," and mind stil wandered or if reason had seated her mi . , she glanced towards the bed. "1 can be spared had lately herself again upon the throne shesoleft for a few minutes." vacant. The poor mature never caressed it, but Soon after, the butler announced to his wife that ' Mrs.ou et noticed that she stared at it often the tray was waiting. Mrs. Quillet, with a last fath able -sitirlange and steady gaze, which was un- glance at her sleeping charge, descended the stairs i oma. and carried the tray into the sitting-room. The old squire was there alone, before the bright hearth, looking a hundred-fold more haggard than on the previous day: He did not turn round at the housekeeper's entrance, and she put down her tray and lingered, not knowing how to oommenee bet story, and trembling in anticipation of a burst of his fury. "Here is your coffee, sir," she said at longth, moving slowly away towards the door. "And the eggs and toast and bacon." The squire slowly turned his head. He looked graver and ten years older than on the previous night. His eyes asked sternly the question his lips Could not frame. "She's in her old room, sir," said the house- keeper, obeying his mute command. "She is very ill—like to die, I have had a doctor to her."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER IV. MT IN THE WoRLD. . A week—two weeks—thus passed. Clare sat up now, thin, and shrunken and wan, never speak- ing, never smiling. Mrs. Quillot often longed to question her, but something in the girl's look of btter desolation and despair held her silent. November glided into December. A month had gassed since Clare Markham had so singularly re. mrned to her home, and still she had not passed Ilie threshold of her chamber, still the old souk° lad not looked upon her face. By this time the girl was able fo sit up for most of the day, and the nurse beg,,an to think of her future. What was to become of her? Could ths equire be indu.ed to pardon and reinstate her in *er home? Would he grant her a father's pxy- iection, or would he send her forth with her child ta wander homeless and friendless</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>GENERAL NEAL DOW AND THE MAINE LAW.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEAL DOW AND THE MAINE LAW.</p>
        <p>General Neal Dow, writing srom Portland, Maine, to Miss F. E. Willard, says :—" There is no tendency in Maine towards the repeal of our prohibitory law. It is generally, if not universally, conceded here to all parties that has become a fixed policy in our State. 2 was en- acted in 1851 by a majority of 18 to ten in the Senate and sa to 40 in the House, and in 1834—after an experi- ence of thirty-three years of its benefits—it was added as an amendment to our Constitution by a majority of 47,075 of the popular vote, the affirmative being three times larger than the negative. And now, after trial Of it for forty-one years, it stands in public opinion, as we think, stronger than ever before. Prohibition in Maine, has reduged the volume of the liquor traffic to on- twentieth of its former proportions. In more than three-fourths of our territory, containing more than three-fourths of our population, the traffic is practically unknown. An entire generation has grown up there never having seen a saloon or the effects of one. The drink habit is nearly or quite unknown among the people there. I have seen many of them, men. and women grown, who have never seen an intoxicated per- son. This is among the villagers and in the rural di9- tricts. Liquor is yet sold more or less on the sly in some of our cities, owing to certain defects in the 141,*, which will be corrected in the near future. In Poll- land, the largest city in the State—it is far within this fact to say the quantity of liquor now sold is not on+ hundredth part so large as it was before the law, tile city being now twice larger than it was at that Ulna. In all the region around Portland with many large</p>
        <p>.„ villages, no liquors are sold. I think a successl attempt will be made in our Legislature this winter passe resolve to submit to the people a proposition amend the Constitution so as to admit women to t</p>
        <p>franchise on equal terms with men, including the quali- fication of reading and writing, which is now required of men. Such a rwoposition, I think, would be cordial r responded to by our people."</p>
        <p>THE RISE OF TO-idS.</p>
        <p>The romance of manufacturing towns which I modern times have achieved greatness possess less of personal interest than the story of the wate ing places; but it has been in many cases far mo sensational. Huge populations have sprung up on the coalfield, and around ironworks and cotto mills, as though, like Minerva, they had but awaite Jobe's fiat to stand forth full grown. It is since th days of the railways that all these swift growth have occurred, though the most remarkable on th list is that of a place still some few miles beyon the railway. The twin towns of Barry and Cado ton, on the great. South Wales coalfield, show a incease more American than British in its sudden ness. Ten years ago they were little villages, wit a united population of 494 souls ; their population ie now over 13,000. Near at hand are Cardiff and Merthyr Tydfil. Cardiff has long had a name in history, but its enormous commercial development is a thing almost of to-day, and is greatly owing to the enterprise of the Marquis of Bute. Fifty years ago its population Wag 10,000, ten years ago 82,000, to-day it is nearly 129,000} Merthyr was only a village when the first railway loco- motive—a precursor of No. 1 and the Rocket—was, it is said, run there in 1204 ; now it has hB,OOO inhabitants. Other Welsh towns have likewise made great strides, notably Penarth, Op.fmore and Garw, and Newport, the two first-named having doubled themselves in the 'Bl-91 decade. Among English manufacturing towns none can boast a more startlingly rapid rise than those of Barrow- in-Furners and Middlesborough. Barrow was an insignificant hamlet made up of a handful of fishermen's huts, until the discovery of a rich•vein of hematite ore some 40 years ago transformed it into one of the most importment centrts of our great iron manufacture. Its population is now 51,000. Where the fishing cables were hauled on to the quiet strand there now flares many a huge blast-furnace, fed with hundreds of thousands of tons of ore yearly, while jute works and one of the largest steel works in England add further, not to the beauty, but to the commercial prosperity of Barrow. The makers of the place as it now is were the late Duke of Devonshire and the Duke of Buceleuch. Middles- borough began even in a smaller way. Sixty-five years ago a solitary farmhouse stood where furnaces and pud- dling mills make the land hideous to-day. Its life was revolutionised by the find in 1840 of a thick vein of iron in the Cleveland Bills, running from Middlesborough to , Whitby; the Darlington and Stockton Railway was already at hand to help in the work. Iron shipbuilding , and Bolckow and Vaughan's great steel works came next, and " Ironopolis " is now an incorporated borough with an M.P. and over 75,000 people, where 40 years since there were not as many hundreds.—From " Corn- hilt Magazine."</p>
        <p>THE r9PITLAR BEVERAGE tor Breakfast, Luncheon, Tea and Supper in all seasons is CADBURY'S COCOA. —Comforting, strengthening, nourishing—for old and young, robust and feeble. CADBURY'S COCOA is absolutely num.—Advt.</p>
