      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>JUDICIARY AND POLICE.</head>
        <p>THE JUDICIARY AND POLICE.</p>
        <p>These questions. though troublesome because of the personal interests involved, must rank among the minor difficulties of Home Rule. Probably the essential point will be secured by the retention for a fixed term of years of the appointment of judges in the hands of the Imperial Government, acting through the Lord-Lieutenant. The question of the police might be settled in a similar man- ner. The raison d'etre of the Royal Irish Constabulary will cease when Ireland becomes a contented and self- governing nation, but the disbandment of that semi- military force, and the substitution for it of an ordin- ary body of police,under the control of the local authori- ties, will necessarily be a work of time. If a term of five years were definitely fixed for the completion of the transformation, the c i-istabulary remaining in the mean- time under the control of the Lord Lieutenant, we be- lieve that Irish public opinion would be fully satisfied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>With regard to finance, the writer does not see how the provisions of the bill of 1886 can be improved upon. The Receiver General will be retained,but with reference to the Irish contribution to the Imperial expenditure, it is hoped that the English people will deal liberally with the question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>INTER.VIEV WITH TOM CANNON.</head>
        <p>INTER.VIEV WITH TOM CANNON.</p>
        <p>THE FAMOUS WRESTLER'S CAREER.</p>
        <p>_ In view of the approaching wrestling contest between I Torn Cannon, of Liverpool, and D. C. Ross, of Glasgow, which is being awaited with a considerable degree of interest in athletic circles, our representative says the " Liverpool Daily Post," presented himself at the Athletic Vaults, Great Howard-street, on Monday morning, and was accorded an interview with the champion. Cannon, who combines a powerful physique with a frank and engaging manner, was ehatting with S. Dixon, of Mel- bourne, his seconder when in Australia, as our reporter entered, and he smilingly acquiesced to the process of " pumping " to which he was immediately subjected. "I was born," said the famous wrestler, " at Tyldesley in 1852, my parents both being Irish, and, as a boy,was put to work in the coal mines which, as you must be aware, are plentifully besprinkled thereabouts. It was while engaged unearthing the " black diamond," that I acquired a knowledge of wrestling, and began to develop muscle with a view to trying my strength and skill. "And what was your first serious engagement P" "With Henry Lord, the (at that time) light weiaht champion Of England. We met at Manchester in 1889, and though only seventeen years of age. I easily dis- posed of my opponent in two straight falls. In the same , year we again encountered each other, and I was once more victorious. In 18701 met and defeated David Bentley at Ince, and the next match was with Frank Robinson for the middle weight championship of Eng- land: The contest proved a draw, but at a subsequent meeting I was declared the winner after six hours' wrestling. In 1872 E. Bibby defeated me at Manches- ter, but 1 had my revenge two years later at Fara- worth."</p>
        <p>"What was your next move ?" " After defeating Bibby I went to London, and there joined the Metro- ' politan Police Force." " And how long did you serve in that capacity ?" For fifteen months, when I received a good offer from Hen,gler's Circus to give wrestling exhibitions, which accepted." " That is to say you threw over the State for the Stage ?" " Well, scarcely," replied Cannon laughingly', "but wrestling was more to my fancy than walking." After touring through England and Scotland I gave a challenge to the worm n for a mixed match, catch as catch can, Gmco-Roman, and back-hold styled, which was accepted by Joseph Acton. We met at Lillie Bridge grounds, London in March, 1879, for £2OO, and after losing the first fan I got the better of the second and third, and so won the contest. Two years later Acton and I again had a bout, and this time he proved sue- zessful. My next achievement was the carrying off of a £5O tournament for the mixed championship of London, and then I decided to cross over to America.</p>
        <p>And how did you fare with our Yankee cousins ?" "Even better than here," was the reply. "I first met and beat Andre Christol in 1883, and afterwards de- feated the great Whistler at Louisville in the same year. the following fall saw ma in San Francisco, and here John O'Hanlon and I tried conclusions, with the most favourable result for me. It was at the same place that I encountered Tomioko, the Japanese Sack Carkeek, Theo Baur,and Max Perchia, and in each ease I met with the most gratifying success. " Did you meet Ross at all during your American tour ?"—"Yes, that is just what I was coming to. We encountered each other for the first time at Louisville in 1885, when I gained the better of the Scotchman in a mixed wrestle. Several months later, I went to Cin- cinnati,anci there beat Greek George and Antinio Pierre, and at 11inneapolis a wrestler named Mutsadi Sora- kicho."</p>
        <p>" Who was your next victim ?" our reporter asked somewhat timidly.—" The last and perhaps stiffest tussle which I had during the American tour," Cannon answered,. "wag with Evan Lewis, known as the strangler, at Cincinnati, but I gained two falls in the quick time of two minutes and four minutes respective- ly, and thus beat him completely." "From America where did you go ?"—" To Australia, where I met and beat William Miller at Melbourne in 188 e. I also gained a victory over Donald Dinnie at the same place, and in the next year I defeated Jack Connor, and so won the championship and £500." " Did that conclude your Colonial career ?"—" Yes, I returned home, and in 1889 went over to the Paris Exhibition, where for two months I had to do battle with all corners." " Rather warm work, wasn't it ?"—" It was, but the pay was princely, and my success unbroken, so much so, that I carried off the championship of France from Felix Bernard. After another trip to the States, two years ago, during which I had several tough bouts at Cincinnati, I returned here and met Antonio Pierre at Manchester. I then went to Brussels, and toured throggh most of the principal continental town, and in November of last year met my old opponent Ross at Glasgow, with what result you are probably aware." " And are you content to meet Ross here in a week or so ?"—" Perfectly. I have no doubt the lengthy Scot is anxious to have a decisive encounter, and for nav part I am perfectly willing to meet his wishes." -" Now, with regard to training ?"—" Oh, it won't take long to put me in trim, and now that the match is practically arranged I intend to buckle down at once.</p>
        <p>It may be stated that Cannon, though powerfully built, is only sft. 81n. in height, whilst D.S. Ross measures fift. lin.—Liverpool Post.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>-- RED HAIR.</head>
        <p>-- RED HAIR.</p>
        <p>Respectable persons whose hair is red cannot be distressed by the remark that the vulgar entertain a prejudice against that hue. From the day they went to school, if not before, the disagreeable fact must have been too familiar with them, and doubt- less they have wondered fiord that time to this how such a feeling arose and why it survives. It is, in truth, an interesting theme,. One may say outright that among every species of human'being at the present day which counts red-haired individuals among its members, that complexion is more or less disliked. But the reason—so far as a reason can be found—is by no !beans the same. The very reddest than we ever saw was a: E.roo boy, serving aboard a man of war on the West African station—that ig, he was enlisted on the ItroO Coast, but he himself told us he was a Maori. The fact is as strange as well could be, but some of our readers may possibly recollect this gigantic fellow in the Naval Brigade. He informed us that the Kroomen did not like him, nor did his Maori kinsfolk either—on this account he had left home. Two red-haired negroes we have seen, and in both cases they were outcasts, though strong and healthy; the Albino black is quite another sort of creature. These instances, however, are cited only in passing. l'hey do not prove anything, we imagine, beyond the obvious fact that rude people object to any departure from the common type. Also, they suggest; at least, a mixture of white blood, though erroneously so far as we could ascertain in these cases. Red hair is not uncommon among Arabs, or, of course, among Afghans. It is a grand virtue of the Moslem faith that it teaches charity towards men who may be afflicted with abnormal peculiarities of the kind, and among these people red hair is no dis- advantage possibly. But they regard the Rufus Jew with special animosity. And•the donkey boys of Egypt —who have lost all the best part of their creed, whilst keeping the worst, and have taken no other in its stead, —jeer at and maltreat a red-haired comrade. No one asserts that this complexion implies any mental or physical weakness. Some of the greatest men in history have been red-haired. Cato is frankly described as red —hair, beard, and all else. Like Cromwell, who was decidedly sandy,he would have no flattering compromise, Cresar himself, the greatest of mortals, has been sus- pected of red hair. Suetonius is silent on that point, though he tells us his skin was vssry white and his eyes dark. Sir Philip Sydney, noblest of gentlemen, and Bayard, the model of chivalry, are described as "auburn "—we know what that means. Among poets were two of the first rank conspicuously and superla- tively red—Ben Johnson and Alfieri. The colouring of Shakespeare's portrait at Stratford-on-Avon hints that he inclined that way ; Milton had the suspicious golden looks, But it is enough, for, as has been said, nobody questions that the highest qualities may accompany red hair. Painters also have done their best to exalt it.. So has fashion at variouS times. The great women of history show more than their due share of this complexion. To take the names as they recur to memory—Branca Capella, Lucrezia Borgia, Queen Elizabeth, and her rival of Scot- land were all red-haired. In truth, the great Italian artists portrayed a majority of the beauties and grande dames of the Clinque Cento with hair more or less red ; but then, we must take account of the fashion, There is no need to tell how Russian ladies envied the ruddy tresses ef their German slaves, and cropped them to make wigs, or dyed to match them. When the bar- barians from the North established themselves as kings and nohles in the Empire, their descendants naturally regarded this complexion as evidence of pure blood. There are old families in Italy which still look askance at a dark-haired scion. Every King of France in the long line from Holdowich, whom we call Clovis, to the very last of them, was blonde or red, like Francis I. Their people loved the hue no better for that.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC NOTICE.—Not one but HUNDREDS of people of both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constitutions are yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson, 3 and 4, Fitzallau Square, Sheffield. Write to them for advice free when suffering from weakness, loss of flesh, appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches, indiges- tion, pallid skin, nervousness, and general debLity. Posi- tive cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases, acquired or consti- tutional. Sixty years' experience. One address (as above) for over 30 years. The" A. 1 Mixture," 4s. 9d. the case. The Magic Mirror in envelope, four stamps, Sufferers disap- pointed with Electric Belts should write to us at once.—Advt. KAY'S COMPOUND, a demulcent anodyne expectorant for Coughs and Colds, 81d., 130. Of all Obernists.—Advt.</p>
