      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MEETING OF BOOKMAKERS.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF BOOKMAKERS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of bookmakers was held in London on Monday to discuss a proposed system of registration— in other words, a prop,;sal to tax themselves on a gradu- ated scale to provide a special force of police who should be employed to keep racecourses free from welshers. pickpockets, " talepitchers," and other mmbers of the thieving fraternity. Mr. R. R. Fry, one of the leading bookmakers, spoke in support of the proposal, aud moved that" this meeting favours the registration of bookmakers." Considerable opposition was at once manifested, and it was evident that the idea of taxing themselves was regarded by many in the meeting as the height of absurdity. The resolution was lost by 16 voteS to 11, only about a third of those present voting.</p>
        <p>WESLEY'S THEORY OF EARTHQUAKES. " The cause of earthquakes," said John Wesley, " sin."</p>
        <p>How he reasoned it out is not easy for the average sinner to see. The idea of such a tremendous physical convulsion as an earthquake resulting from the violation of moral law is nonsense in the eyes of modern science. The reverse is more often true. "The cause of a deal of sin," said Hannah Mere, "is bile." That we can see through. Bile poisons the brain, and the brain is the organ of the mind. It is certain that all the earth- quakes that ever shook this wicked world never did half the damage that is done every year by sleepy stomachs and lazy livers. Generals have lost battles, statesmen have been beaten in diplomacy, workmen have Veen thrown out of jobs, clergymen have preached poor sermons, and husbands and wives have quarrelled for ..o reason under the sun but a " touch of liver com- plaint." The crust of society can never lie quiet with such a farce as biliousness under it. This is not a run- away metaphor ; it is hard, cold fact, and the man who doesn't know it has never tried to do business with an- other man when the second man's skin looked yellowi or asked a loan from a friend when that friend was labouring under a sharp indigestion. Writing of a time six years ago a lady says her skin became first yellow and then of a saffron line. Her breathing was difficult and short, and she felt much pain in the chest and sides. Her appetite failed, of course, for Nature never calls for fond when she is not in condition to use it. Still a trifle of sustenance must be taken. The lady took it, digested a bit of it, and suffered great distress from the presence of the rest in the torpid stomach. We scarcely need say that her sleep was broken, and mind and body weary, weak, and Out of tone. Now what sort of life is this to lead ? What is anybody good for while in such a state as that? What wages:would you give a servant who was always So ? What would you wager on your own success in business if you had to pit yourself against other people's sharp' 'ness, while you had to fight with a poison-soaked head ' and a stomach that refused its breakfast? Not a•</p>
        <p>crooked sixpence. . Our correspondent continues : " At last I took to nil bed. The doctor said my liver was wrong ; that I had the jaundice. As his medicine did no good, he advised me to go to the hospital. I objected to this, and he saki Try a change.of air then, and see what that will do for you.' So I went to my old home in Fairford, Glouces- tershire. This did no good, and I consulted another physiciap, who attended me for some time, but failed te help me. My friends now thought I was in a decline. "I didn't eat enough to feed a bird, and begot t° despair. Gradually growing more feeble and miserable. with no expectation of tbetter days, I lingered on until July 1890, when an acquaintance urged me to trY Mother Seigel's Syrup. The confidence my friend seemed to have in this medicine made such an inuPree- sion on me that I sent my niece four miles to get it. After taking the first bottle I felt better. A weiglit appeared to be lifted from my chest, I began to relish my food, and felt better in every way. I will conclude by saying that when I had used two bottles more returned to Birmingham quite well, and have had au attack of the disease since. lam only sorry I did 00° know of the Syrup years before. (Signed Serail gel" icES, of the Lion Inn, Longmore-st., Birmingham." flere is certainly a lesson for the day. Probably there, is not one person in a thousand who has not sufferee from what is called a" bilious attack," and maul tire more or less bilious all the time. The symptoms ere these:. Furred tongue, headache, dulness, and sleePi- ness, yellow eyes and akin, spells of dizziness, hot hand° and cold feet, bad taste in the mouth, loss of appetit°. broken sleep, nervousness, loss of inclination to ecer- tion or work, low spirits, irritable temper, the gulping, of a nauseating wind or gas, distress after eating, an° wandering pains and uneasiness all over the body. These things signify liver complaint ; and the eV° is indigestion and dyspepsia. If long neglected there are plenty of worse consequences to follow. The suc- cess of Seigel's Syrup in curing this malady fact that that it goes straight as an arrow to the ver7 root and source of it, the paralysed digestion. Whatever may be Q true theory of earthquakes may be sure of one nketk-on-Ite.-Tfamely, that in the blood, arising bile I a daughter. in the digestire function, is the hot ITARIItow, pain, and death than all the powers at -the interior of the earth ever scattered over its surface.</p>
