      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>-ALLEGED KICKING TO DEATH AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>-ALLEGED KICKING TO DEATH AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE PRISONER RELEASED.</p>
        <p>CRITICISING .THE CHIEF CONSTABLE. - la Saturday morning saw a crowd of unusual tilltbers at the Borough Police Court. Michael ,eo.n 'Molly was again to appear on a charge of cans- jokt,he death of James Hosty, by striking and tmeg him on the 27th ult. The incidents con- 'with the affair which had gained publicity !Zcited the interests of a certain circle of per- and the curiosity of many others. In Irlam- ‘2,, at the entrance to the court, there were gathered °rue forty or fifty persons, chiefly women, being b!the most part, the same individuals who are to 'Thseen there any time during a sensational case. e "hen the magistrates—Messrs. Burgess and Voisey— ntered the court, they would be surprised with the retacle, for the whole room was packed. Mr. J. Lyon ~hittle, Town Clerk, appeared as public prosecutor,and • Percy Davies was at another table, as solicitor for the defence. Behind the box where the prisoner stood, th,e!ll was a group of the prisoner's friends, peering the massive iron railings at him, some of them a9°' whispering ring for him to "keep your pecker But the prisoner seemed to realise the serious- • of his predicament, and never had sufficient tto respond. to Ihe facts of the case which .have been published be- ase Were detailed to the Bench, and were to this effect : e rniking in a public-house in Mersey-street on the Ight of the 27th ult, were the deceased, his brother, two men named Collins and Cain. The four ilenrrelled, and were in consequence ejected by the land- Later in the night the two Hostys were passing Cor.i• .Warrington Bridge, when their two antagonists, '1213 and Cain, accompanied by the prisoner came t4LIV. A. scuffle then took place, and James Hosty sus- cll-iled the injuries that culminated in his death three pora later. How he got those injuries was the baffling th;ll,,t,_. The deceased's brother John swore to the Bench koZge, distinctly saw Collins rush up to his brothd, t,ll'c bins down, and kick him several times ; whilst on tae other hand Collins told such a tale to the police as to the arrest of. Connolly, who swore that he was -l• or within arms reach of the deceased. •itin'Lr. Davies and the Town Clerk pointed out that the th4l,est would take place during the ensuing week, and g,,a-Laccordingly asked for-another remand. This was tlas,7edi and the prisoner was allowed bail, until Tues.</p>
        <p>PRISONER RELEASED. The ease was resumed again on Tuesday. before a full ''Of spectators. Mr. It. Garnett was in the chair. Of 6,4 r. E. J. Guest was also present. In consequence evidence adduced at the inquest, and the lack of hee's for a prima facie case Mr. Whittle said he felt that ,was not lustified in offering any evidence for the pro- uite agree with you "said Mr. Davies.-Whereupon aief Constable remarked cot' am rather surprised at Mr. Whittle. I think that thew Dithstanding the evidence at the inquest yesterday, .rtisoner ought to be.-sent for trial:" ain Sorry to hear you •say that" retorted Mr. ha„ le,a indignantly. '"Mr. Hunt in making that remark, the given way to an opinion which is quite improper on aatipart of a chief constable. If the magistrates are the thit there is not sufficient evidence to. sustain Wite°secution they are doing what is in accordance e; th Inaft • aw if they dismiss the case. J.know a police- thin,2B necessarily prejudiced for the prosecution ; but I evid• that had Mr. Hunt listened more attentively to the uence given yesterday—" `A",t; Runt I did listen attentively—thoroughly so. "er further discussion the 'Chairman said the evid- -4011'4 adduced only justified them in releasing the. pri- Abe". 'But if the police could at any time bring fur- l' evidence to sustain the charge it should be heard.</p>
