        <head>INQUEST..</head>
        <p>THE INQUEST..</p>
        <p>AN OPEN VERDICT.</p>
        <p>(CENSURE ON DR. SPINES. at„The ad jou= ed inquest on the deceased was held by ;Z. Brighouse, coroner, at the Police Station on Monday p„ rning. The Town Clerk &lt;Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), was „'esent as public prosecutor, and Mr. P. Davies ap- pear,d 41tabl to represent the prisoner. The Chief Con- ti, e was also present.—John Hosty, brother of the hr-eased, said that cn Tuesday. the .27th ult., the day 4:llrother received his injuries. theyavere in the. Leigh 417 Public-house, Mereey-street, between four and ita 14 the afternoon, along with Tim Cain. Stephen 447, trick Coll* - came in soon afterwards, and Cain P.,Z,the, former had a dispute. Stephen Collins struck 104 we., the mouth and then ran out. Two minutes, kee fif,Y left, and tWalked tewards Warrington Bridget sh4litween near the Packet House they met seven or tit,3' roughs. Stephen Collins was among them. Wit- hs as knocked down, and, when getting up, hie 4ctt,"t!r was knocked down. He did not recognise the ktsc,'",„d but he might have been among the roughs who, Itht.-e(1 them. His brother did not stir hand or foot t the the ground. A man came up and assisted 9e4,e'5 to take the deceased into hie lodgings. The h'd on the following night spoke to him as he lay 4t but eimply asked him to tie a handkerchief about ~ ei..**'• He died on Thursday the 29th ult.---By Mr. 'Ay „,`° Witness did not tell the magistrates on &amp;ter- "re,ek that Stephen Collins went up to his brother T4itkedxtiocked him down, or that he almost to death before his very eyes. "re magistrates the deceased was kicked, but L'bilL,',,ea 3' by whom, because he did not know. Stephen not spoken to him since he gave his evidence Oteps-LrdaY week. He had not seen him since. ,`"•,•,),,",,"en Collin's, labourer, living in Walsh's Yard, off ,•"teskt-es's-et., Latchford, corroborated the previous `'"eh'• as to being in the public-house with him in tio ,Y With the men he had mentioned. There 11 was '4lk:4'l'o there, he said except between him and Cain. as to the paying for a pint of bee ,and at gave Cain a" clout "on the nose, upon turned them out. Witness vient out 4,44„1aetfr2.11t _ managerdoora t nd Cain at the back. Witness went t,2clii,`"e Bridge Foot, and then turned back. He 671 f; eotisin, Patrick Collins and Michael Connolly, ttlethk-e`ert.Wa2ds Cain, and James and John Hosty anal_ Ile saw Connolly "hit James Hosty with his 04olist.le'Ck him to the floor." Pat Collins said to was a shame to strike a man like that. Con- Va. 4410.. ot say anything. Witness then went to the Le, th. ", and Connolly came in and said, " Did you lj'lt lift I gave that chap in Mersey-st. ? ' and acivt.° be rather proud of what he had done.—By Witness heard John Hosty give his evidence day week. He did not say that witness (Collins) Ltid knocked James Hosty down or that he before hie eyes. He did not say who 11511:h Witness (Collins) had been before the tiZl•ll,;',rs eight or nine times, twice for felony,but had penal servitade. He had had hard labour for t4gchh,;llths• He had never been up for assault, only til`2h in -self-defence. He did not see any lii'Olit„"Y Connolly should have struck the deceased. )ta: cirkfv`,..tatelY walked across the street and knocked tot' "• Witness was perfectly sober. The deceased aterY sober. Vtio,7 Collins, 12 Friar's Green, labourer for the Cor- lp", reel;' said he went into the Leigh Arms with Michael llltStephen Collins, the Hostys, and Cain were end Cain quarrelled, and were turned out. Lec• Cae, ?•od witness went out, and saw Stephen Collins • la a ;11 the street, in front of him scrimmaging. ao es 'ated them and on turning round saw the bee••,,°l the "no, or" on his back. He could not 14,e iLknocked down by Collins or Cain, as they '‘), nt • of him. The two Austine and Connolly ..a51,-,asetthal him. John Hosty was standing above the Vitl "r, nen witness saw him on the ground. He 1144, 'Knocked down either by his brother or by 05 he must have fallen accidentally. He saw Witness and Connolly went into the gfta-", and b C ollv said to witness " Did you see Cave that fellow?" anah, gad he replied "I`lo, ' tk- V v hack was turned to you."—By Mr. Davies tho rd the deceased's brother ' tell the maigstrates ueeeased was followed by a lot of roughs, esti" ho knocked him down.—Mr. Davies said gabevidence to prove the contrary.—Tisnot hy • th;eterroborative testimony. He stated that cht b „. y- "'mays were knocked down, he could not 11`,1 t whom, but Stephen Collins was among Hosty got up, but James could - There were five or six at 41 ari ' but none of them were navvies. attack o an at n him.—Frederick Wallington eado_ss k e ing along Mersey-st., and saw two men ,tln,` thei," by roughs, and assiste d a man to help A'5114'17 ee-"thliP• A man just behind this man gave him t,hq htllniouth,and he dropped to the ground. The aZ,°llt, "Who's next ?" and made for him, tink-"•`-' off round the corner. (Laughter.)—Peter ",?thee to Stephen, and cousin to Patrick SilaB4l‘iliti°dll the 28th ult. Michael Connelly came up 9E-Bt., and said, "I knocked a man sense- fl kitg ?' a Mersey-s t. " Witness said, "Had you 4th ell oh, Zlel he replied, "We had a drop of drink, EiteZ Witness did not then know that his '"en was accused of doing this.—Wilham</p>
        <p>Potter manager of the lodging-house where the de- ceased said he was brought in about? 3a p.m. on Tuesday, the4.7th ult. He was unconscious, and was' placed on a squab in the kitchen, where he remained. until 2 30 p.m. on Thursday. Witness first saw hisW about eight o'clock on the Tuesday night. Inspector; Church, the nuisance inspector, called in the ordinary course at 130 on Wednesday, and sesiines the man lying in the kitchen reported it to Dr. Gomel'''. medical officrer of health. ,Dr.. Gornall immediately called thinking it to be a case of smallpox, but findingit was not, said it was a matter for Dr. Spinks. Witness went for Or. Spinks, and meeting him in the streetsthelatter returned with him. 'The doctor paid the deceased was in a drunken sleep, and should be put Wised and have some tea given to him. Dr. Spinks did not see him again until 2 p.m. on Thursday, two aud a half hours before he died. 4Witness had called on ' the doctor at 10 a.m. otirThursday, and was told to get a subscriber's ticket fossadmission to the Infirmary. Instead of doing so witness went for the relieving officer. Nothing had been done for the deceased except that his brother seve- ral times gave him some tea.-ealfred Bruchemite, pro- prietor of the lodging-house, said he informed Police- Constable Barnett shortly aftercraidnight of the day the man was brought in. Witness instructed three men to carry the injured man upstairs, and • because they could not do so they brought his bed downstairs and placed it on three forms, and laid him on it. In- spector Church came and thought the man had the small- pox. The deceased was ultimately carried up to bed. It was not very easy to get 1/nBj:sinks down when they wanted him. There were no women on the premises. Dr. Spieks did not examine the deceased until he was told that the man's side was much swollen.—James Harrison, deputy at the lodging-house, said he saw the deceased about half-past eight the morning after he was brought in. Dr. Spinks came in and saw the deceased about noon. He did not open his shirt and waistcoat to see whether he had some wounds about him. He gave him no medicine, only ordered him to be put to bed and bare some tea and sunk. When the doctor came a second time—on the Thursday—the deceased was in bed. The doctor asked witness where the de- ceased was hurt, and he replied, "behind the head." The deceased's brother told him he had no money for medicine, and Dr. Spinks said a thousand pounds would not cure him.—By Mr. Davies :—The doctor saw him two hours and a half before he died. His brother asked trine to give him some medicine, but the doctor said it was no use giving him anything.—Dr. Spinks was then sworn. He said he was first informed about noon on the Wednesday, that the deceased required his attention. The Relieving Officer called and told him about the case. He went to the lodging-house and got there about half-past one. When going into the lodging-house he was informed that he was drunk. He touched the deceased and asked him his name, .. and he opened his eyes arid looked at him. He saw some blood on his hand, and found there was a slight abrasion of the skin. He thought the man was seffering from drunkenness. Be had no ilea that he was so seriously hurt as he was. He did not prescribe any medicine. The deceased did not seem as though he could drink anything. He pre- scribed some tea, and that he should be left alone for a short time, then got to bed. On Thursday afternoon he went again, and found that the man was dying.' Subsequently assisted by 'Dr. ' Braide, witness made ,a post mortals examination of the body. He found no marks of violence except a slight scalp wound on the back of the head. No bones of the body' were broken.except the bone of the bead. The vessel of the brain was very much congested, and clotted with blood. His opinion was that death resulted from fracture of the skull,, and rupture of the blood • vessel.—By the, Chief-constable : He received no information en the Wednesday as to the man having met with •violence from anybody. Nobody I said anything to him about abusing the deceased.— The Coroner said if it had been his case he should have I expected the doctor to come back before Thursday afternoon. That was,the way he looked at it.—Mr. Davies asked the witness how it was he made such a terrible mistake, and he replied " Because somebody told me he was drunk."--r The Coroner : It is remark- able at all events they should tell you he was in a drunken sleep, when about 1130 they were very anxious to tell the police-there had been a row.— Witness in answer to Mr. Davies said he did not consider that neglect had anything to do with the death. I Nothing would have saved him. In reply to a similar ques- tion by the Coroner witness gave a like answer.—Police Inspector Bowen gave evidence as to • the arrest of the prisoner, and Inspector Rowbotham deposed to his being informed late on Wednesday night about the attack on the deceased. The jury deliberated privately for some 20 minutes, when the foreman (Mr. Albinson) announced that their verdict was that the deceased met his death through violence, but on whose part there is not suffidl- ent evidence to show. They also unanimously requested that the Coroner would censure Dr. Spinks as having been guilty of great remissness in the performance of his duty in the case.—The Coroner, addressing' Dr. Spinks, said: The jury have asked me to convey this note of censure to you. It is a.very unpleasant duty, because you and I frequerstly come into contact with one another in the course of these ihquiries, and I oonvey it to you by reading this %verdict to you. But I think it would be cowardly on my part if ,1 did not express an opinion one way or the other, an&amp;l think after carefully considering the matter, that the jury are justified in their verdict.—Dr. Spinks : Tam very sorry. It is the .I.rst time you have hadoccasion to doso.—The Coroner: I should be wantine in courage if 4 did not say it, because this man was in an unconscious state on Wed- nesday afternoon, and according to yo.ar own evidence you were told that he had been in that state from 8 o'ciock the previous night. That means4B hours, and I thick you might have come to the conclusion that there was something else than drunkenness.</p>
