      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>;FORMERS AND OLD AGE ;PENSIONS.</head>
        <p>;FORMERS AND OLD AGE ;PENSIONS.</p>
        <p>THE ABOLITION OF NV.ET RENTEL</p>
        <p>kreport has been issued by the Executive Council of the,Foresters Friendly Society to the 700,006 members of the society with respect to old age pensions and other questions. With respect to old age,pensions the Execu- tive say that in their opinion the policy of complete freedom from any financial association with the State is the only safe policy. They were all desirous that something should be done to make the lot of the aged industrious poor pleasanter than it is, but they fee! that the acceptance or extension of the system of State pen- sions would deal a heavy blow at the very prine;ples which their order had been working for tie years to ex- tend and perpetuate. Self-help, self-reliance, and self- dependence had been potent factors for the improvement in the condition of the workers in the past, and they were convinced they were the true factors for the suc- cessful solution of the problem of help in old age in the future. When the proposed Royal Commission to inquire into the treatment of aged paupers was mooted it was felt that the working people of this country should be represented on the Commission,and that one representa- tive of friendly societies should be appointed. The Friendly Societies Sub-Committee met at Birmingham, and appointed the London members of the committee a deputation to wait on the President of the Local Govern- ment Board and press for a favourable reply. The re- quest had been granted, and Brother J. J. Stockall, P.G.M., and Parliamentary Agent of the Manchester Unity of Oddfellows, was appointed. The Council heartily congratulate both Brother Stockall and the societies. The subject of wet rents for clubrooms had excited considerable attention by reason of their remarks in the last report. They believed the evil to be of com- paratively small compass in their order, but none the lees it did exist. No complaint had been made to them, and, therefore, they had beta unable to reach any of the transgressors, but it was within their knowledge that in certain dis- tricts the officers were moving In the matter. If their efforts failed they invited them to report the delinqu- ents to them, when they should not hesitate to take the severest measures to stop the evil. With reference to the opening of courts for females, the Council were gratified to be able to report that the greatest interest was being taken in the matter in all parts of the United , Kingdom. Many such courts were in process of forma- tion, and some only awaited the necessary preliminary step of the sanction of the district being given. It might be necessary to point out that in instituting these coutts precisely the same formalities would have to be observed as in opening courts for male members. The same form of application for a dispensation would have to be filled up and sent to the Council. The contribu- tions for the benefits given must be those in the general law tables. Female courts, like the male courts, might establish other benefits suitable to themselves at their discretion.' Special rules would, of course, be required, and they were drafting a code of guide rules for female courts which would be found useful. They strongly commend the furtherance of this extension of their work to every member of the Order. Court Star of Nelson, formed for females in October, 1891, in connection with the Nelson district, New Zealand, had now 47 financial members, and a fund of £5O. In the preparation of the compiled financial statement for 1891, numerous in- stances occurred where districts and courts had illegally borrowed money for management purposes for the bene- fit funds, and in the case of no fewer than 31 districts and 399 courts adverse balances were shown in the management funds amounting in the aggregate to £2,871, but it was feared that these do not entirely re- present the actual extent of the practice, which was a most pernicious one, and absolutely illegal under the Friendly Societies Act and the rules of the Order. It was imperatively necessary that the benefits funds should not be used for any other purposes than those authorized in the rules, and any violation rendered the district or court offending liable to specified penalties.</p>
        <p>ADVICE TO MOTHERS I—Are you broken In your rest by a sick child suffering with the pain of cutting teeth P Go at once to a chemist and get a bottle of MRS. WINSLOW'S SOOTHING SYRUP. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is Perfectly harmless and pleasant to taste, it produces natural, quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the little cherub awakes 'as bright as a button." It soothes the child, softens the gums, allays all pain, relieves wind, regulates the bowels, and is the best known remedy for drentry and diar- rhcea, whether arising from teething or other causes. Mrs. Winslow's soothing Syrup is sold by Medicine dealers every- where at is. lid. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>111 E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 14, 1893.</head>
        <p>111 E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 14, 1893.</p>
        <p>WAItRINGTON risITON )30ARD OF GUA.RDIANS.</p>
        <p>Ths fertnightly meeting of this Board was held at the Town Hall on Timrsday morning. There were present Messrs. R. 'Heaton (chairman), E. Auk- land, Pilling, A.J.lolley, ie. Jolley, Gill, W. J. Shaw, Clarke. Davies, W. 'Houghton, J. Muir, .1. Turton, Ratcliffe, 5. Houghton;the Rev. G. F'. Roberts, J. C. Sutton (clerk:), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), Messrs. Ward Workhouse master, and Mr. Mines, the super- intendent of Padgate Schools), and B. Shearer relieving officer.</p>
        <p>WATCWIELIZING IN LA,NGAItIIa.</p>
        <p>INTEIIESTINR (CE! EMONY AT PRESMT.</p>
        <p>The recent revivill of the watchneeking industry at Prescot was marked on Wednesday by an interesting celebtatinn, when the first watch that has beet made in all its pertain the largest watch fattory in Europe—no less a claim is made for the 'Present works—was pre- sented to Lord ,Derby, through this representative, the Earl of Camperdown. For =ay generations Prescot has made its living in thawateh trade, betnintil within the last year oetwo it has ,never attempted to make a complete watch. The internalffittings ofeehat in Pres- cot are believedto be the hest "watches in existence have been nranufactured there three out of mind. Prescot, indeed, did that part of theework so well that the watch. makers, thinking they could travel no further in the erathof.pelfeotion, were cotetent to rest there. Resting 'was speedily' found to mean retrogression. More enter- prising mantlfacturers both at home and abroad, and especially intim United States, begirt. where Prescot left off, and pursued the competition to ..such purpose that not more than five years 'ago the industry, so far as 'Lancashire was concerned; had almeet died of inanition. At that critical juncturathe bold proposal was put forth that Present could only-nave itself' 'by making watches from beginning to end—from the faze to the case. The movement, which was warmly eupported by the towns- :people, generously backed by tbe Earl of Derby, the late Mfr. Evans, and other gentlemme led to the formation of the Lancashire Watch Company./Limited, who are al- ready eowell assured 'of success that they hope to be able in the near future to tura out fifty thousand complete watches a year, and by that means give employment to not 'fewer than three thousand hands. The first section of the new factory was opened exactly three years ago, and is-devoted to the old trade —that of manufacturing interior movements; while the second section, which will be utilised for finishing the watches, was opened yesterday practically for the first time. Two other sections are in contemplation. The new building, which, like thefirst, has been erected by Menem.. E. Taylor and Saes, # Littleborough, from the designs of .Messrs. E. Stott end Sons Manchester and Oldham, takes the form -of a three-storey workshop, 300 ft leng and 2.2 ft. wide. The first storey contains the I flat steel and stem-winding ,departments, the second is used'for jewelling, gildint, said escapement and balance making ; and the third as an assembling and dial-making room, -with accommodation altogether for about 900 workpeople. When the scheme is complete the factory will consist of a handsome front building 440 ft. +long and 28ft,wide, and either three or four storeys high, the central portion, which will be suitably treated in eleva- tion' 'forming the administrative department.' Behind thiabuilding, running its whole length but separated from it by areas of light, Neill be the main works,corridor from which the different workshops will be entered. , The latter 'will be placed with their length at right • angles to the corridor like the teeth of a comb, and are intended to be seven in number, connected together at the outer ends by a rope-race, in the centre of which 'and at the end of the middle workshop the steanrengines, boilers, and electric lighting plant will be placed. The. buildings Which have so vfar been completed consist of one of the three storey teeter workshops and 'two one- storey workshops, and occupy a space of 300 ft. by26oft. Cloakrooms and lavatories are attached to each depart- ment. The rooms are excellently warmed and ventil- ated, and during the day they are flooded with light.. Aht Preseot took part in the ceremony with which the achievement of the completed watch was signalised. Triumphal arches brightened the factory corner of the somewhat 'dingy .town, and the highest arm of the Preseot windmill held at.g against the watery sky. A band played cheerful, music in the factorremadrangle, where the townspeople assembled until the doors •should be thrown open to them. The great room on the ground •flooeof the three-storey workshop was gayer in appear- ance than it may ever be again. Courageous mottoes in' Latin and English were displayed on a- profusion of, brilliantly coloured shields, and one could scercely see the ceiling for bunting. When the chair was taken by Mr. ,W. D. Houghton the place was crowded. The Earl of Dertenwho had•promised to attend in person to receive the first watch, was unable to be present in consequence of his recent indisposition and the inclemency ot the .• weather, In his place the Earl of Camperdown attended, and there were also present Lord and Lady Kelvin! Lady Margaret. Cecil, LadyeArthur Cecil, Lady Carnegie, the Hun. Miss •Crrosvenor, 'Miss A. Bere,sford-Hope, Mr. T. P. Hewitt. (managing director), the Rev. H. Mitchell ;(vicar of Trescot), Mr. 'W. Clark:on, Mr. A. A. Menzies, -Mr. Alderman F. Smith. Mr. G. Hale, Mr. J. W. Raley, \Mr. W. ?leer, Mr. George Banner, and others. The Chairman, in opening the proceedings, expressed regret at the absence, owing to the inclement weather and the express orders of his medical advisers, of 'Lord Derby. who. had, however, asked Lord Campordown to represent him. (Applause.) They had mac to cele- brate the third anniversary of the Lancashire Watch Company. f,(llear, hear.) He could assure them it was •eseeedingly gratifying to 400 so large an assemblage, which he toak as an indication of the sympathy and in-. terest manifested in the welfare and progress of the moo;pany. De had everyneeson to believe that the com- pany had now turned the tide of affairs (hear, hear), andthat witha continuanoe of the same careful and in- telligent working, which at present prevailed in every department, they might reasonably expect allthe sue- cess they anticipated. (Hear, hear.) The company fronnits commencement had shown an earnest faith in that law of political economy which demanded that , cheapness of production and excellence of quality-Mould be matters which could not be overlooked by any branch of inductry if anything like success was te be realised. and accordingly, whilst they bed given attention to the cheapening of labour by the most improved machinery, they had at the same time kept distinctly before them the fact that excellence of quality must be a sine slim ;nen of everything they produced. (Hear, hear, and anplause) Under the guidance of this policy they were fast arriving at the position when they womld be I alf4 to compete with the very bcst firms in the manu- facture of watches,* remarkable for their excellence and cheapness. (Hear, hear.) He -thought they enigirt fairly admit that it was owing to anon-compliance with this policy that the watch trade cf Prescot for some years was found to be rapidly declining. (Applarear.) I it coiled not withstand the vigorous ,competition which 1 was brought to bear against it by ,countries like Swit- zerland and America. In both these countries economy of production had been carefully studied and carried out. In America, in particular, advantage of all the mast improved machinery had been taken and an almest unlimited amount of capital had been invested in the businese, with the object of producieg a cheap watch.. This they had accomplished with very great sucenos not only from a manufacturing but also from a commercial point of view, and to-day there were eeveral firma in America whirl; were respablesach of turning outover 2,000 watches a day all the year round. Under such compe- tition it was not to be wondered that the watch trade of Prescot, remaining on the old lines, and not being in accord with the spirit of the times, was fast being left high and dry; but thanks to the enterprising spirit of</p>
        <p>several of the leading townsmen, that undesirable result had been, he hoped, entirely averted. Those who had watched the company from its commencement could not but have been impressed with the unfaltering energy and perseverance which had marked the general man- agement. They had availed thtmselves of every known means for cheapening production, but at the same time they had employed all the most expert and skilled work- men that could be procured. 'Therefore they might look with some confidence to Prescot ; instead of becoming a deserted town, it would become a hive of industry, where useful and profitable employment would be pro- vided for the people, and the welfare and prosperity of the whole district increased. (Applause.) He wished to congratulate Prescot on having established a technical school, where the science of horology and attendant sub- jects were being systardtically and scientifically taught to about 150 pupils. That showed that Prescot was at last determined to be in touch with the times, and if the instruction supplied were only appreciated and utilised as it deserved to be, very great good would be achieved not only to the young people, but to the com- pany as well. Such an institution could not fail to create increased interest in the local industry, and he hoped it might be the foundation for a continued supply of good and intelligent workmen. Througheut the country in every branch of manufacture there was a growing de- mand for more intelligent and more skilled workmen, who not only were expert with their hands, but who were in possession of a fair amount of technical and scientific knowledge—in fact, knew the why and the wherefore of every detail of the work in which they were engaged. The age of the rule of thumb was pass- ing away; they lived in times of very great change,both as regards improved machinery and improved methods an&amp;processes of manufacture, and they might rely upon it that those only who could grasp that situation and take 'advantage of all the improved intelligence of the da:,a, whether they were workmen or employer, who would be able to take a front place in the race of com- petition which now so widely prevailed. (Applause.) _</p>
        <p>1- Mr. T. P. Hewitt made a statement with regard to the development of the works in which he stated that there was now a floor space in the factory of a little over 59;000 square feet, while there was accommodation for son:m.1;500 hands. Since the company took over the old movement making businessess of the town they had manufactured something like 300,000 watch movements, which they had sold for about £lOO,OOO making some- thing Hilo 15 per cent, on the turnover. He claimed that the company had been the means of arresting the decay 'which had been rapidly overtaking Prescot, and con- fidently believed that it would be instrumental in restor- ing England to her old position as one of the first of watch Making nations. (Applause.) The Ghairtnan then handed Lord Camperdown the first complete watch (cased in gold) made in the factory,and desired him to present it to Lord Derby, from the share- holders, as a token of their respect and esteem.</p>
        <p>Lord Camperdown, who was received with applause., 1 saidif .Lord Derby had been able to make an exception to the course which he was now pursuing under medical advice, that exception would certainly have been made in •favour of Prescot, in the prosperity of which he was deeply and warmly interested.—(Hear, hear.) Generally speaking, patriotism and neighbourly feeling, however excellent and admirable they were in themselves, were not the principles which it was most wise to call into counsel when you were embarking in speculations. Their factory in Prescot was the exception which proved 1 • the rule. They had shown that it was worth while to • bat* your neighbours when they knew how to back 'themselves, and they had done it without resorting to Any of the devices of the company promoter.—Hear. hear.) They had collected the money chiefly among themselves, and had been assisted in the first place by business ability drawn from themselves. In the struggle which preceded the tormation of the factory they were powerfully assisted by the Church militaht—(laughter And appl(tuse);—and, further, they formed their com- pany by amalgamating those private interests which were to be found among themselves, so that instead of continuing to compete against one another they had 'chosen tht i better course of uniting to compete against Coventry; mid against America. They had now met to- gether not to lay a foundation-stone or talk about the , . formation ; )fa company,or even to open a factory, but to commemor ate the actual completion of the watch they had set thei nselves to make. (Applause.) He congratu- lated them, then, on recovering their watch trade. It was one thi eg to establish trade and another thing to lose it., but the most niffieult of all was to recover it. That was what he was glad to think they were in a fairway' of doing now. Five years ago Prescot was moribund, a nil they were told—he had no doubt quite truly—that t his was their last chance, and that if it lid not succeed t hey must decamp and go eemewhere else. To recover a trade that had been. lost both courage and brains were r squired. He was one of those who believed that brains w ere just as good on this side of the Atlantic as on the othe r ; but the difference was that on this sido be thought we i were a little more inclined to•economise our brains, an d not to have recourse to them until we saw by experi( me that there were no other means of carrying on bu winces successfully. In these days trade was very diffe rent from what it used to be. One would imagine that ii i these days of education.and cheapness of production i t would be easier to make atertune than in past times. It was, however, very much more diffi- cult, and-that d tifficulty was constantly increasing,while , the tendency of ' trade was more and more in favour of the union of c( mmanies. Instead of it being private manufacturer ag!ainst private manufacturer in the same town it was noo r becoming a question of companies. It was even, as in this case, a matter of one town uniting Against another, or of a whole country uniting against other countries, se that trade had become a larger,wider and more difficult; thing to manage than it used to be. In the meantime in Prescot they had taken the first step —a step often taken by England before in times of diffi- mtlty. All English trades had from time to time been in difficulty. At this moment the very greatest of our iniustries—the agricultural industry—was in a most critical condition, but he firmly believed that by one means or another the agricultural industry would extri- cate itself as it had done before, and as they had extri- cated the vratchinaking trade. (Hear, hear.) He had to.congratulate them on the results they had obtained and to express a hope that they would long continue to enjoy that prosperity which appeared to be now in prospect, and Which they had done so much to deserve. llt would be with the greatest pleasure that he should convoy the watch which they had handed to him to 'Lord Derby. (*pplause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Clarkson presented an address to the Rev. H. Mitchell (vicar of Prescot), in which the directors and shazeholders expressed their appreciation of the valuable assistance rendered by him during the promotion of the Company. The Rev. gentleman's parishioners also stated,in the address that they felt under a deep obliga- tion to him for his a:-,tivity in promoting the prosperity af the town.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. A. Menzies,lla,nded the first lady's watch made la the factory to Mrs. Mitchell, the Vicar's wife, and eersiarked that there were many lady shareholders in the ,Cornpairly who had given great help and very little itroublet. He mentioned that in connection with the factory .aeme hundrede of modern and convenient hNuses far workmen had been erected without any risk or cost to Use Company. The Rey. H. Mitchell acknowledged the double pre- sontationiVotes of thasks were given to the directors and the chairman, and the proceedings concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>'1101752 COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>'1101752 COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee recommended that the offer ,of Mr. Evans, eiMorter's Arms, of a parcel of numbers of the ' " Graphic " and that .of Mr. Frank Booth of maga- zines be accepted. The Committee also recommended that the Master be instructed to attend the alournecil conference at Maud:Mater on Monday the 9th inst., his expenses to be paid. The Committee further recom- mended that-the o'ller of the Kilburn Orphanage Author- ities te board and-educate two children be accepted.— I The minutes.wereiarlopted on the motion of Mr. Rat- , diffe with the exception of that as to the two orphans,as to which a discussion took place.—ln reply to the Chair- man the Workhousdhlaster said the children were those Of a blind woman who had recently died in the House. The matron having an interest in the children, and knowing of the institution, in London, wrote to the authorities desiring them tcl take the children in, which they eon- seated to do. Mr. Ward read a report from the institu- tion, which stated that of all who had left-it none had been returned on the hands of the anthorities. Alliwere earning their Hiring honourably, and availed themselves of an occasional hsliday to come and see their old -guar- diens and companlons a proof of the kindness shown to them in'tkeir childhoo:d. Mr. Ward read a'letterstating that children remained in the institution tilt they , were-21,the threellast j'ears of theirserviceteing given to i the orphanage for the advantages they had enjoyed. It stood to reason that some arrangement of this kind must be made, otherwise a work of the kind could not be carried out without t,he intervention of paid servants, most undesirable as • well as expensive. The girls them- selves reaped no sma benefit from this plan. By the time they left they v vere fairly experienced, and their minds and principles were fully formed.—Mr. Ward stated that there were no paid officials, at the • institu- tion. (Hear,: hear.) The girls had to -stay till they were 21, and helped to ke oip the house in order. The Society was a Church of England one.—Two women ..ap- peered before the B bard to (Meat to the children being sent to the instituti on, it being stated that they had previously declared themselves unable to -support the children, but now t hat it was proposed ,to send them away, wanted to take them. One of the women, an aunt of the thildrel a, said she would bake one -child's/id find a home for the other with a neighbour.—The'lloard, however, conerme I the recommendation of the-com- mittee as to 'the ch ildren.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>AVPLI :CATION HT litl2. AVAITHIN..</head>
        <p>AVPLI :CATION HT litl2. AVAITHIN..</p>
        <p>An application a Tas read from Dr. Watkin, medical officer for Newton, for an extra mllowance,for surgical operations perform ed by him on a child.—The 'Newton Relieving Commit tee had recommended that .110 be paid.—Mr. Ratelift e proposed that he be paid:43s. Dr. Watkin got MO a 5 rear as the Board's Medical Officer, £45 a year as vaccination officer, :MO a year ,for expensevfor M adicine. and Oor d2O a year from other sources. He (Mr. Ratcliffe) thought Dr. Watkin would be 'very WE di paid with £5, better paid than the ratepayers general ly who had to find themroney,—Mr. Muir seconded. Ile remarked that in Warrington and other places it seem ed to be the fact that they appointed officials as ornamen its, and directly they got into work and becamemseful they wanted something for it. He would give him £5 , as to do this was thelretterof two evils.—Mr. A. J. Jol hey proposed that no allowance be made. He said th‘ re were other medical officers Wi.lo devoted a large am ount of time and work to the Board and were not freg [neatly coming forward for extra remuneration. He thought Dr. Watkin was ecarcely justified in his application.—Mr. Clarke seconded Mr Jolley's proposition.. —Mr. Davies proposed that 2715.! be paid. (A voice: I lloonshine..)—This motion was not seconded.—The prop osition for the payment of .£5 was carried.</p>
        <p>•REISMONATI ON OF SLR. MOOD4WRIOECT. The Clerk read a letter frona Mr. Rood-Wright, resign- ing his seat as a,,Grua rdian for Newton, as he was about to leave the district.- —The resignation was accepted, and the Clerk instructed to take the necessary -StAVS for san election to -fill the sea t. NEW Cd eIISOLIC QNVERIIIVOR. A letter was read from the Rev. Father Feeney resign- ing his position :as Raman Catholic instructor at the Workhouse, andlreem nmending that the Rev. Michael Ryan of the nesv mission at Warrington, benppointed. —The resignaticuarwas: accepted and 'Father Ryan ap- pointed. SMALLPDX.</p>
        <p>The ()Lana nada lettter from the Cleftut° the Heydock Local Board, statiag that three more •cases of smallpox had occurred in their district. In view of the continued spread of thedisease in that district, he was instracted to request that the Guardians would grant inereased facilities for public evacoination, and thereby assist td stamp out the epidemic.—Mr. .tiarron -said he had written to Dr. VVatkin desiring him to do anything he could in the matter, and' read a reply from gum stating that the quarterly periodical attendances for gratuitous public vaccination were about to ne-commence.*The CLERK read a 'letter from the Aberstwyth Guardians stat- ing that a man had ;recently been admitted to their Workhouse sufering 'from smallpox, who said•haeame direct from Warrington, where he had spent .ane night in the Worklaouse, and KZ several inmates were-mitering from smallpox he nuisthave contracted the disesse there The writer desired to 'know what steps had been taken to isolatetbe disease in the Warrington Union and prevent its spread.—The Clerk said he had replied stat- ing that before the matter could be considered he must; be furnished with the name of the man, and the date on which be slept in the Warrington Workhouse,—Mr.' iIIATCLIFFR said the statement that he had caught the. disease there waequae untrne, as there were no cases of smallpox there as alleged. NORTH WORMERS; I'OOE LAW CONFRRENCE. The Cants read the report of this conference at Mos- theater, which the Chairman of the Warrington Guardians had attended as a deputation, and which dealt at some length with the question of .vagrancy.—The Outman thought the Warrington Guardians were dealing very properly with vagrants at present. Their meals cost. something like id. per steal, and their keep was less, he believed, than in any other Union. He thought the best pin was to go on as they had been doing.—On the, motion of the Bev. Mr. RIDER-TR, a vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman as deputation.</p>
