      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>ITS PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>ITS PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>Many portions of the work connected with the Ship Canal undertaking are completed, but there is an enormous amount of work still to be done, and which has practically been left untouched for some weeks. The new railway diversions and buildings constitute that portion of the undertaking which alone can claim to be anything like perfect. The excavating, the finishing of the slopes, and the con- creting,in this neighbourhood at least, stand pretty much as they did two months ago. The frost is much responsible for this, as it has prevented the men from working for some weeks richt The whole undertaking, Consequently, is a scene of desolate .desertion, and few outdoor workers are to be seen at their labours. _ .</p>
        <p>• The railway viaducts at Latchford and Acton Grange are certainly two of the most important pieces of local work. They are both likely to be I opened within a few days, for luggage traffic, which • will have to run over them for six months before passenger trains will be allowed to travel along them.. The new line running to Acton Grange, is a magnificent affair; in consequence of being made into a junction near the new Acton Grange Bridge it presents a width of metals and a complete set of signal and point arrangements, probably surpassing anything in the locality. With the exception of a few little details, the bridge is entirely finished,and as the cuttings in this neighbourhood are accord- ingly advanced, the majority of the little colony of workpeople who were engaged here have gone. The metals used on this viaduct are said to be sufficient to run 25 miles of single line. The number of men working about the section was 1,500. All anima- tion has gone from the scene. What one sees upon a visit to Acton Grange now is the large magnifi- cent gi rdered bridge, complete, and the cutting of a depth of 70 feet which runs beneath it. The ex- cavating on this section however, has probably been more difficult than in other places in consequence of the treacherous nature of the peat banks. Th work extending from Stockton Heath to Latchford has progressed fairly well, but it has been of a Character unobservable to a visitor's eye. The large iron bridge at Stockton Heath over which we pas sed has been well-nigh completed. The abut- tin property will be demolished shortly, and pro- bably within a few months the diversion of the road will be completed. From here down to Latch- , LI ford the chief work on hand is the finishing of the slopes, which will be of considerable extent, in con- sequence of the sandy nature of the earth. Near Latchford Station the new stone bridge, which is to convey Wash-lane to Lpmb Brook bridge on the south side of the Ackers pits, is in progress. But nothing more than the sub arches and the base of the grand arch have been done. There is three months' work about the structure yet. When it is finished and the new railway is , opened, the aspect of that part of the country will be entirely changed. The large high public bridge will stand out nearly upon the same elevation as the new station platform which now looks down upon the old station from a considerable height. It is said that the new bridge will cost £5,000. Leaving here and getting along in the direction of Thelwall, we come to the completed railway bridge. The lines over this will be opened for goods traffic in a few days. Hard by are the com- pleted but deserted locks with concreted bottoms and chained gates and sluices, all ready for utiliza- tion. On the far-off side of those locks, near to the hospital, is the hydraulic pressure house. It is nearly completed, and so far as the build- ers are concerned it is finished work. The engineers are laying their arrangements for supplying the hydraulic pressure which is to work the locks, and all the swing bridges down to Acton Grange. Reaching Thelwall huts there is little to be seen. The place looks like a deserted backwood village. It is all empty, the little chapel is closed, its windows are broken, the cold wind blows inside, and theatrical bills are posted outside. The huts are hard by. There are only one or two inhabited ; the remainder are empty and are not yielding the rental of fis. per week, which the compagy received for them some time ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SALTPORT AND RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SALTPORT AND RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Within about two miles of Saltport writes Mr. A. Rimmer T.J.R.G .A.T. in the" Manchester Guardian," the canal reaches Runcorn Gap. At Saltport the wharves and jetties for the transhipment of salt are well occupied accommodstion is also provided for timber, minerals, pigiron, and pottery, and a large warehouse has been constructed for the storage of any traffic requiring cover. The great rafts of logs that we shall soon see in the harbour do not, as has been said ,take any damage at all in the water. In Canada there are sometimes acres awaiting shipment, and for a year in the fresh-water lakes in the Far West no damage comes to the wood. Two steamers that are well known in Canada the Lake Ontario and Lake Nepigo n, have decided to ray up for the winter at Saltport Pool. They belong to the Beaver line, which was started som e 23 years ago by Captain Ayton, previously of the Allen line, and the late J;vhn Rimmer, of Liverpool. The present proprietors strong. 47 objected to the canal, but they And the advantage of it in a harbour for their two vessels. The following canals will be in communication with the Ship Canal :—The Bridgewater, the Leeds and Liverpool, the Bury and Bolton, the Rochdale, Ashton, Huddertfield, Stockport, Macclesfield,the Calder,and Hebble,the Aire and Calder, Trent and Mersey,and others. The importanee of the lona traffic that will be concentrated is very great, as may be seen from the list. Those who are acquainted with the , Rhine will remember the great barges that take up such , quantities of merchandise and bring down as rnuch,and it is proposed by the Ship Canal to form a syndicate to provide barges of from 200 to 1,000 tons "similar to those in use on the Rhine." This is a very important item, for barges can go to the side of a large steamer and take their cargoes to Manchester or elsewhere. There are many places of great interest round here, not only on the Cheshire but on the Lancashire side,and when there are passenger boats on the canal they will be an attraction to visitors. On the Lancashire side is the interesting old church of Farnworth, with its monu- ments. Bold Hall is near. On the Cheshire side is Hal- ,l ton, which has so eventful a history. It was at one ' time the residence of John of Gaunt and his son the Duke of Lancaster, afterwards Henry IV., and it is said to have been their favourite residence. Rock Savage, or Clifton, is in the township of Runcorn. The hill is pic- turesque, and on it was built a magnificent baronial mansion by Sir John Savage in 1565. It has been allowed to collapse, but part of it is still a farmhouse. It now belongs to the Cholmondeleys, who received the title of Cholmondeley and Rock Savage on the same day, Nov. 22, 1815. The chapel of the Savages in Macelesfield Ckurch has a number of monumental tombs finely carved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The following report of the work of the Runcorn Women's Temperance Association during the year ended December 31st, 1892, has been submitted to the General Committee. The work done during the past year was such has every member would re- gard as encouraging, as decided progress had been made, and numerous pledges had been taken. The most successful public meeting in point of numbers was the one held in January, when, after a tea in Bethesda Lecture Hall, Mrs. Eckroyd, of Liverpool, delivered a thrilling address to a very large audience, and nineteen pledges were taken. The annual meeting was held a few weeks later in Union Hall, when Mrs. Ward Poole, of London, distributed 150 certificates to the day scholars who had written successful papers on the lectures given in the various schools, a few months previously, by Mr. Chandos Wilson, scientific temperance lecturer, to the Band of Hope Union. Mrs. Eckroyd addressed the children in a most suitable manner before distributing the certificates. It was impossible to estimate the good that must result from such lectures. With the consent of the school managers, a similar course of lectures was delivered during the past year by Mr. J. Jakeman, of Chester, who also delivered a public lecture in Union Hall, on November Bth. There was a large audience, and the various experiments the lecturer made, showing the effect of alcohol on different kinds of food, were very interesting and instructive. The usual monthly meeting had been held, at which among others the following ladies delivered addresses :—Mrs. Green, of Liverpool ; Mrs. Williams and Mrs. Bird. of Chester ; and Miss Dutton, of Warrington. The change of the place of meeting, with a view to attracting people in different neighbourhoods to the monthly meetings. had met with a fair amount of success. The recital of Dickens's " Christmas Carol," by Mr. John Harwood, of Manches- ter, was a great suCcess, and the large attendance at what proved the last public meeting of the year was a happy augury of success for the coming year, when the secretaries hOpe to have the hearty co-operation of all friends of temperance. The financial statement showed i that the amount of receipts, including a balance n hand at the beginning of the year, £4 Cs. 2d.,was £3815. 1.0. expenditure £24 19s. lltd., leaving a balance with which to commence the new year of £l3 is. 2d.</p>
