      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>CHANCELLOR OF THE EXCHEQUER AND THE CYCLISTS.</head>
        <p>THE CHANCELLOR OF THE EXCHEQUER AND THE CYCLISTS.</p>
        <p>The following correspondence has passed between the Chancellor of the Exchequer and the secretary of the Oxford Cycling Club, Liverpool: Oxford Cycling Club, Liverpool, Jan. 5, 183. TO THE RIGHT HON. SIR WILLIAM HARCOURT, M.P.., CHANCELLOR OF THE EXCHEQUER. Dear Sir,—l am desired by the committee of the above club to draw your attention to a resolution passed yesterday by the Wirral Highway Board, in which they declare their intention to levy a tax of Ss. per head upon cyclists residing within their jurisdic- tion. My committee, which is representative of cyclists of the county, desire me to say that they regard such-s proposal as abortive and arbitrary, and we venture to hope that no sanction will be given by Parliament Ye such a suggestion, which would, we believe, prove de- trimental to what has become a very popular and. healthy sport, which is entirely free from the many temptations to gamble and indulge in alcoholic liquors which in so many instances characterise other pastimes. We quite agree that some steps should be taken to mid- gate some of the abuses which have unfortu mats ly arisen- through the recklessness of a very small minority Of.ioll those who do not pursue cycling as a pastime, but we.. CA believe that ttu very worst way to provide checks or'" safeguards would be to levy a tax upon cyclists gene- rally. We shall be glad to have an expression of your, views upon the subject, which is one of absorbing _ terest to cyclists who constitute no inconsiderable pro- portion of our population.—l am, dear Sir, faithfully ours,</p>
        <p>ARTHUR COHEN, Secretary.</p>
        <p>Malwood, January 7, 1893. Dear Sir,—l have no sympathy with the proposals which have been made in various quarters for levying a tax upon cyclists. In my view, cycling has afforded opportunities of healthy enjoyment, and facilities for business purposes which are highly advantageous t large classes of the community whose restricted mer exclude them from more expensive methods of locr tion. Many thousands of persons with small in, ,„7„,„,- are thus enabled to obtain fresh air, and to e the oppressive influences of large towns, and,e from carry on with less labour and fatigue their occupations. I see with satisfaction in the part of the country in which I live that vas* • deiv,ig,,ht,„,roul people are capable of enjoying its beautie t which in former times also t&amp; - was impractie to an exten. cycling as a cheap, a healthy, and a tow Able. I regiirl I have no difficulty in assuring you Aril pleasure,tni have any responsibility in the m that, 61.17,bnEi I should think of taxing.—Yours fe . Atter, it .ithfully, W. B. macoußT.</p>
