      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JANUARY 14, 1893.</p>
        <p>THERE are at present to be seen The Temperance many signs which indicate that Cause. the temperance question will be</p>
        <p>l'ery much to the front in the immediate future. Temperance reformers in various parts of the country are displaying a renewed enthusiasm in forwarding their great cause, and a buoyant con- fidence of approaching success. The Parliament- ary contest which has this week been decided in the West Derby division of Liverpool may not, at first sight, appear to augur well for progress, either in beuTerance or any other kind of reform. Mr. Loxo, the Conservative candidate, has been returned by a majority which, though not so large ss that obtained by the Tories in 1886,isyet larger than that which was secured by the late Hon. W. 14. CROSS in the General Election of last year. But it has been well said that the really notable and suggestive feature of the contest was not the largeness of Mr. LONG'S majority, but the unex- Pectedly numerous votes which were recorded for his opponent, Mr. D. S. Comarr. The last-named gentleman fought under pretty nearly every pos- sible disadvantage. In the first place, it was well understood from the beginning that there was, in reality, no possible chance of successfully oppos- i4g the Tory nominee. The political complexion rthe West Derby division has been for a "g time too pronounced to permit of even the most sanguine partisan indulging in any self- delusion in that respect. Even so late as July last, Mr• CROSS'S majority was no less than 1,182, and at the previous election the Tory preponderance Was even greater. Then, again, Mr. Comarr was not brought into the field till within a few days of tbo Poll, and this was a serious drawback to a man who, comparatively speaking, was unknown to he Constituents. Yet, in spite of all this, he hseeured no fewer than 2,275 votes. Mr. ComaN as been for many years an active worker for beniPerance reform, and reform of the liquor laws Was placed in the very forefront of his programme. liad it not been for the militant zeal of the tem- perance par ty of Liverpool, in all probability Mr. LoNo would have enjoyed a "walk over." In this election, as at the municipal contests in Novem- ber, that party have shown pretty clearly that they cannot be any longer ignored even in those Parts of Liverpool which have long been under the donnuation of the crudest Toryism. Another not- sble incident of the week, in connection with the temperance crusade, has been the enthusiastic g4tbellog held in Exeter Hall, London, on M0n- (1537 evening, to welcome Miss Wiimaine,President ,n4nd Pounder of the World's Women's Christian. -lemperance Union. Miss WILLARD is one, and certainly not the least, of those apostles of mperance in America, whose work, in spite of 1,111 interested detraction, has been amply justified 'Y its results. Of the operations of the Maine Prohibition laws, Mr. Stephen WILLIAMS, M.P., its recently given an inter esting and evidently tuPartial account of his own experiences. He 84trlit5 that at Portland, and one or two other i/laces, Ile found that the laws with regard to the tillthi.e of liquor were almost openly contravened, but was entirely due to the laxity or venality of .4711` local administration. In the towns of IL'gnsta. and Lewiston he, howo ver, found a very ~rent state of things. These are, he says, • sPerons manufacturing towns with an air of g:eat c‘unfort about them. "On that beautiful Ittit'unn evening the sidewalks were crowded with dl'ot.isans and their families. The shops were all ing a roaring trade. I did not see a drunken Ners „ On, nor was there a single drink shop to bring elirse upon any of the happy and contented 1)• ,1° I saw in their thousands." Hecame to the tloe'usion that, in the country districts, infrac- -118 of the prohibitory laws were almost unknown, 'ltilad that, for the whale of the State, it had been of clirnous benefit.</p>
        <p>THE January number of " Relief " 14t°xican • W terkhou ssem which is the organ of the Work- tc„, • house Drink Reform League, con- P w 4 a hich strikingly illustrates the ex- Deild• 11,3 'tura upon alcoholic stimulants in Work- during the year 1891, as given in the t Government return relating to the subject. (,Trial) shows each county shaded from white to Dtaek according to the aggregate average expen- torlditure upon all intoxicants in the Workhouses Iti:lprised within its area. All the counties ere the rate of expenditure is above the aver- are put in the " black list," and the smaller kr: "Penditure the lighter the shading. There lan-a°4l3' two absolutely white counties in Eng- t. 11' and, curiously enough, they are, geographi- sPeaking, the two extremes of the country, 4lnelY, Cornwall and Northumberland. The ' IsT erage yearly expenditure per head of inmates in rthninb • Workhouses has only been four- of rum Wal Cornwall, fourpence halfpenny. shows a considerably larger proportion of the4.te," there being four of its counties in which d"Penditure has been under sixpence per tvi,oh. Cardigan reaches so low a figure as 'ellee halfpenny, while the other three c°4ntie 40,a 8 are Anglesey, threepence three-f arth- Itiarti fivepence farthing; and Caer- /t i hen five and seven-eighths of a penny. tho ,P")rthy of observation that, in England, n°le. of the " black list,"—that is to say, the T is in which the expenditure of intoxicating ier • of IR 41)°ut workhouses has been above the average t} the Ire° shillings and elevenpence—are in epeate:uth, including the metropolis, and, broadly, the counties that are grouped around</p>
        <p>it. The diminutive county of Rutland has un- questionably earned the doubtful distinction of spending most money upon intoxicating liquor for its workhouse inmates. The expenditure there has been nothing less than twelve shillings and ten- pence per head, as compared with the fourpence per head of Northumberland and the twopence half-penny of Cardigan. The paupers of Rutland must surely be lavishly supplied with the contents of the cup that inebriates. Rutland, which is, let us be thankful, only a very little county, stands on a bold bad eminence in this respect, and its supremacy is not challenged. Hertford appears to come next, with eight shillings and twopence per head, and it is run pretty closely by Bedford, with seven and tenpence half-penny, Essex seven and sevenpence half-penny, Worcester seven and sixpence, and Kent seven and fourpence. Coming nearer home, we find that the consumption in Lancashire Workhouses has been at the rate of just a fraction over one shilling and ninepence half-penny per head, while in Cheshire it has been two shillings and a half-penny. The many-acred county of Yorkshire offers some re- markable contrasts. In the West Riding the ex- penditure has been two shillings and fivenence half-penny per head, and in the East and North Ridings only eleven pence half-penny. It is not difficult to form an opinion as to which Riding the liquor-loving pauper of that country would pre- fer to become permanently domiciled in. When the counties as a vcrlole differ so much, it may easily be guessed that there is even a greater diversity when comparing one Workhouse with another. At many Workhouses no intoxicants of any kind are used, and it is pointed out that the routine administration of intoxicants in Work- houses has been condemned by the Medical Officers' Association, by the Lords' Committee on Intemperance, and also by the Local Government Board, who have repeatedly penalised officials responsible for illegal or excessive consumption of alcohol.</p>
        <p>WE are told that the " Upper Ten" of Rome are, just now, suffering the pleasures of a "pain- ful sensation." This sensation is caused by an incident similar to one which fluttered the do ye- eotes of " Society" in England awhile ago. The story goes that a young nobleman, a " scion " of the Roman princely house, was observed to win money with quite a suspicious regularity when holding the bank at baccarat. Some of those who had become tired of losing wished to learn the secret of success which the young man seemed to possess, and a deputy—that is to say, a politi- cian—set himself to watch. A few nights ago tho "scion of the princely house " took his place in the banker's chair, and the politician had not long to watch before he saw the young man help- ing himself to some cards which were concealed under the bank notes. The deputy at once put his hand on the notes and cried out " Stop, cheat- ing is going on here." The bank notes were exam- ined, and two cards were founrl amongst them. This, we are told, was an unmistakable proof of cheating, though we cannot vouch for the accuracy of this assertion, as we are not versed in the intel- lectual intricacies of baccarat. The " scion" was, however, like another person who achieved a somewhat similar distinction, highly indignant at the imputation, and assumed an air of much- injured innocence. We are informed, however, in language which if ambiguous is suggestive, that his noble associates helped him from the room with their own hands. We may easily infer that the case was regarded as an urgent one, and that some excitement must have prevailed before Italian noblemen would do any kind of laborious work with their own hands.</p>
        <p>The report of Ma jor MAniNniN, the Govern- ment expert, on the Thirsk railway disaster, is a sensible document, and it is to be hoped that his concluding recommendation will have due weight with railway companies. Major MARINDIN comes, of course, to the conclusion that the signalman, Holmes, was principally responsible for the dis- aster, but he points out that Homing was at the time unfit for duty, and ought to have been ex- empted. Other signalmen and the drivers of the express and goods trains also come in for more or less of censure, but Major MA.EnTrax adds,—and the remark does as much credit to his heart as his judgment,—that" it must be remembered that it is comparatively easy to say after an event what should or should not have been done." His con- cluding recommendation is that ten hours' duty is the outside which ought to be allowed for signal- men on main lines. Railway directors who do not pay heed to this advice ought to be made much more responsible for railway casualties of the kind referred to than they have hitherto I been.</p>
      </div>
