      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>DISTRESS IN RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DISTRESS IN RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The severe frosty weather, which had continued for upwards of a fortnight, up to Saturday last, had caused considerable distress amongst the working population of Runcorn. Operations on the Man- chester Ship Canal Works had not been resumed since the Christmas holidays. Bridgewater canal was frozen up and outdeor workers generally were thrown idle. The Runcorn Relief Committee, formed by the Board of Commissioners,having upward of £6O in band determined to start a soup kitchen,on Saturdaylast, and it was continued until Thursday. The soup was of a most excellent description, and was distributed at the Town Hall each day at noon, by a band of volunteer workers. Mr. F. Wiswall, the chairman, Mr. Taylor, Mr. Grice,Mr.Hankinson,Mr.Abel,Mr.J.florsetield,and others taking a very active part. About 7,000 soup dinners have been provided, many of the recipients being men from the Ship Canal Works. A large portion of bread was given with the soup. The committee have also dis- tributed about 400 ticketa for groceries and butcher's meat, to needy families, and also about 250 tickets for coal. The Ship Canal Co., besides being the chief sup- port of a soup kitchen at the Ship Canal Mission room contributed £4 to the Commissioners fund and Mr. P. Speakman, C.C., gave three truck loads of coal. Mr. W .Shaw, missionary at the Seaman's Institute, has also provided soup particularly for the least children, and Mr. Jackson contractor for the Big Field section of the Ship Canal also commenced to provide soup dinners on Monday. The welcome thaw of the present week has enabled hundreds of men to resume work, and as the Bridgewater Canal was re-opened for traffic on Thursday the boating population were thus relieved of their necessities.</p>
        <p>POLICE INSPECTION AT WIDNES. The Widnes Division of the Lancashire Constabulary was inspected at their headquarters, Widnes, on Wed- nesday, by the Assistant Chief Constable, Captain Ibbetson, who made a minute examination of the new clothing and appointments • the men were also put through their drill. Captain Ibbetson expressed himself highly pleased with their smart appearance, and said they had greatly improved in their drill during the past 12 months. The inspector closely examined the books, and complimented Superintendent Keighley on the man- ner the books were kept.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>CLAIMING ANOTHRR MAN'S WATCH AT RUNCORN. .</head>
        <p>CLAIMING ANOTHRR MAN'S WATCH AT RUNCORN. .</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday, Joseph Johnson, a boatman, was charged with stealing by means of a trick, a silver lever watch, valued at £2. the property of Joseph Hopley. About the middle of December, Mrs. Hopley went to the shop of Mr. Thomas Carr, watchmaker, Regent-street, and left a watch to be repaired. The prisoner was in the shop at the time, leaving a watch of much inferior value. On the 24th December Hopley went to obtain his watch, but Mr. Carr could not find it, and inquiries revealed that the prisoner had previously been to the shop and claimed the prosecutor's watch as his own. When arrested, the prisoner had the watch in his possession. He had no excuse to offer, and was fined 203. and costs 223., or in default one month's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHAPEL, RUNCORN. On Wednesday evening the members of the Rev. J. Pulford William's class held a social eveMng, on its re- opening. A very enjoyable was specie.</p>
        <p>POST OFFICE, RUNCORN. The box at the Post Office will on and after Sundar next, the 15ts inst. be closed on Sunday nights at 8.50 p.m. instead of 9 p.m. as formerly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The Rev..CanOn Wood, vicar of Runcorn, has pro- mised to contribute £5O towards the cost of construct- ing this Institute. Mr. F. Wiswall, chairman r.f the Bot.rd of Commissioners, has succeeded in forming a committo to raise the aincunt required for the purpose. JOSEPH PRITCHARD keeps speeticles to suit all sightts from a shilling a pair.—Adrt.</p>
        <p>WILLIAMS, DBACON AND MANCHESTER AND SALFORD</p>
        <p>BANK, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The directors of this bank have declared a dividend for the half-year ended 31st of December last of ten shillings per share, being at die rate of 121 per cent, per annum. They have xis-) added £20,000 to the reserve fund, and £5.000 to buildings depreciation fund, carry- ing forward £7,469 Bs. 9d.</p>
        <p>JOSEPH PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- et.. and 4, Bridge et., Runcorn.—Advt. VICTORIA-ROAD WESLEYAN CHAPEL, WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Special services to the memory of the late Rev. W. Pritchard, superintendent of the Widnes Wesleyan Cir- cuit, were conducted in this chapel, on Sunday, by the Rev. J. W. Crake, of Liverpool, who delivered two im- pressive discourses. He dwelt upon the career of the deceased gentleman, and held up his life as a worthy example for all Christians. If you wantyour watch repaired by skilful workmen, take It to PRITCRLED'S.—Advt. FREE LIBRARY LECTURE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the course of lectures promoted by the Free Library Committee Mr. James Wilding, assistant town surveyor, on Thursday evening in Union Hall, Regent-street, delivered a highly interesting and exhaustive lecture on " the progress of Gothic Architec- ture in our Cathedrals." The Rev. Canon Wood pre- sided and there was a fair audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SMALLPDX IN RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SMALLPDX IN RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>During the present week three cases of infectious diseases have been notified in Runcorn, and there is also a fourth case not yet notified at. At Weston there are also three cases of the same disease. On. of the patients is under the care of Dr. MacLennan, who re- fuses to recommend him to be allowed to be removed to the hospital. A committee of the Rural Sanitary Authority are endeavouring to find a building at Weston in which to isolate the patients there. PRITCHARD'S have now stood the test for 50 years, and quality and first-class workmanship are still guaranteed.—etaMt DEATH FROM BURNS. AT RUNCORN:</p>
        <p>On Monday, at Runcorn Police Court, Mr. Worslev; coroner, held an inquest on the body of Bessie Balkwell, aged six years. who had resided with her parents in Bach Cooper-st. On Christmas eve the girl was warm- ing her hands, at the fire, when her pinafore became ignited, and before the flames could be extinguished she was terribly burnt on the body and arms. Dr. Mangan had attended the injured girl, who died from shock to the system, on Saturday. A verdict of " Accidental Death" was recorded.</p>
        <p>If you want a lucky wedding ring go to 51, Hrou-sT.,whers you will get a present with it.—Advt. - -</p>
        <p>TIIE VIEWING Or BODIES ET CORONERS' JURIES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of an inquest at Runcorn Police Court on Monday, held on the body of a young girl who had died from twins, the coroner, Mr. J. E. Worsley, said he did not like such inquests as that ; it was very sad. Mr. DeKorte, a juryman, said it would be a very good thing if juries had not to view the bodies ; it was anything but a pleasant sight.—The Coroner said it was quite unnecessary. There was an attempt to introduce into Parliament a Bill exempting juries from viewing bodies, but compelling the coroner alone to do so. He did not agree with that (laughter) because a coroner who knew nothing of the district might be called upon to view a body which might not be the one intended, whereas a jury, who did know the district, would be a guarantee that it was the right body. In his opinion it would be foolish for the coroner alone to view the body. Nothing further was said on the subject.</p>
        <p>When in want of It chaste and elegant wedding or birthday present go tO PRITCHIRD'S, 51, High-Bt.—Advt.</p>
        <p>ASTON UNITED DISTRICT SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the monthly meeting of this Board was held in the Board room, Aston. Present—Mr. J. G. Best (chairman), Messrs. William Lowe, A. Lomas, and Charles E. Linaker (clerk).. The accounts of the Board for the year ended the 29th September, 1892, were examined and signed by the Chairman on behalf of the Board, the cost of maintenance for the year being as under :—Receipts : Annual grant from the Education Department, £B6 13s. 6d. ; fee grants, £5l 10s. ; drawing grant, £1 15s. ; books, &amp;c., sold to children, £4 Os. sd. ; rates, £lBl ; balance from last account, £l2B 18s. 10,1. ; total £453 175.9 d. Payments : Salaries of officers, £3O; legal expenses, £4 13s. Bd. ; salaries and remuneration of teachers, £217 Bs. Bd. ; books, apparatus, and station- ery, £23 13s. Bd. ; fuel, light, cleaning, and repairs, £34 3s. 10d. ; rents, rates, taxes, and insurance, £3 12$. 3d. ; interest on loan, £lO is. 3d. • principal repaid, £10; balance in hand, £l2O 4s. 51. ; total, £453 17s. 9d. JOSEPH PRlTCHARD.Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- at., and 4, Bridge-st., Runcorn.—Advt. THE WIDNES TOWN CLERK LED RATE PROSECUTIONS. At Widnes Petty Sessions on Thursday, before Mr. E. K. Muspratt and Lieu.-Col. Gaskell, Edward Gould, Lugsdale-rd., was ordered to pay arrears of gas, water, and general district rates to the amount of £4 I7s. 3d. within one month. The Town Clerk of the Widnes Cor- poration (Mr. H. P. Oopenheim), prosecuted and Mr. J. W. Knowles defended. At the conclusion of the case the Town Clerk said that he wished to protest emphatically against the remarks made at the last sitting of the court when the case was adjourned with regard to his absence. IHe protested against himself or those by whom he was ' retained, being dictated to as to how, Cr in what man- ner they should act with regard to the recovery of the arrears of rates. He submitted that his presence was not necessary in the case under notice, and only when he could help them in arriving at a decision which would be just to both parties concerned. Mr. Knowles ex- pressed his surprise at the Town Clerk's protest,especially making it in the manner he had done, but stated that he still felt that it was the duty of all salaried officials to appear in person to support all prosecutions, as prose- cutors, on behalf of the Widnes Corporation.</p>
        <p>JOSEPH PRITCHARD IS prepared to wind and keep in repair, private clocks from 10s. per annum.—Advt. DUTTON SCHOOL BOARD. At the monthly meeting, held on Saturday afternoon, the Rev. L. D. Lyon Bennett presiding, the Clerk (Mr. C. E Linaker) produced the accounts of the Board for the past year ending Michaelmas, 1892, for signature by the Chairman from which the following particulars are extracted.—Receipts: Balance brought forward, £lOB 1.55. 4d. ; annual grant from the Education Department, £Bl 7s. 6d.; drawing grant, £1 lie. 6d.; fee grants,' RH 7e. 3d. •, sales to children' Is.; rates, £147; total, £345 9s. 7d. Expendittue : Salaries of officers of the I Board, £3O; legal expenses, £4 is. 4d.; salaries and remuneration of teachers, £lOO 1.35. 9d. ; books, appare- -1 tus, and stationery, £9 17s. ld. ; fuel, light, cleaning, and repairs, £25 Hs. sd. ; rents, rates, taxes, and insur- ance, £1 Bs. 4d. . • other expenses, £1 10s. ; principal of loan repaid, £24Bs. 10d. ; interest, £3l 10s. sd. ; balance, £llB ss. sd. ; total, £345 9s. 7d. The Clerk reported receipt of the fee grant, amounting to £7 2s. 6d. An additional precept was signed upon the overseers of Dutton for the sum of £22. Cheques for £27 is. 2d., for salaries and other accounts, were also signed by the Board. JOSEPH PRITCHARD'S sale shop is 51,1-lign-st., rns workshop 4, Bridge-st.—Advt. A BOATMAN'S DEATH FROM CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Worsley, coroner, on Monday, held an in- quest at Runcorn Police Court, on the body of James Henry Catterall, a boatman, aged 17 years, of Gilbert- st., who died on Friday. Dr. Robinson said he was called in to see the deceased and found him in the last stage of consumption, and on the point of death. In his opinion the youth must have been ill for many months, but he had been working almost up to the time of his death—John Catterall, a boatman, deposed that his brother did not complain of being ill until New Year's Day. For a few years deceased had had a cough in the winter, but had been all right in the summer. He was delicate. He had assisted in working his mother's also witness's boat. In some weather he was kept at home. They did not think it necessary to send for a doctor.—Mary Harrop, an aunt, deposed that the previous witness had been kind to the deceased.—The Coroner said it was evident that the youth had died from cvnsumption, and the question was whether there had been any neglect on the part of the relations in not calling in medical advice earlier. It was a pity the deceased had been allowed to linger so long without a doctor, but they must take into consideration the mode of life of boat people and, their wanner of thinking.—The jury returned a verdict of "natural causes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 14. 1898.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WESTON BIBLE AND MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS. _</head>
        <p>WESTON BIBLE AND MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS. _</p>
        <p>The first meeting for this i-e-a; was held on Monday night last, when an address on" Missionary enterprise" was given by Mr. W.F. Somerville, of Richmond College. Mr. James Ford occupied the chair. There was a good attendance of the members, and at the close a hearty vote of thanks was given to Mr. Somerville for interest- ing address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN CARPENTER AND HIS WIFE.</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN CARPENTER AND HIS WIFE.</p>
        <p>Peter Knight, a carpenter, was brought up at Runcorn -Police Court, on Wednesday, charged with non-compli- ance with a magistrates' order to contribute towards his wife's maintenance In April last. The prisoner, it trans- pired, had not had the order served upon him, but had purposely been wandering about the country to evade service. The arrears on the order amounted to £29 19s. 9d. The prisoner was sentenced to two months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>YOUNG MEN'S BIBLE CLASS, Si'. MARY'S' PARISH CHURCH WIDNES. . _ _</p>
        <p>The quarterly social tea was held on Thursday even- ing, in the Waterloo Schools, the Vicar presiding. About 120 sat down to tea. A very enjoyable evening was afterwards spent, the programme being a varied and in- teresting one. Some chemical experiments, introduced by Mr. John Clibborn and Mr. George Davies, proved a novel and enjoyable departure from the ordinary musi- cal programme. ST. AIiBROSI CUIIIACH BIBLB CLASS'S, WIDNES.,</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting in connection with these classes was held on Tuesday evening at the above mis- sion room, when upwards of 70 sat down to an excel- lent tea, which had been prepared by a number of the members of Mrs. Riley's class. After ,the tables mere cleared the remainder of the evening was devoted to songs, Sm., which proved very enjoyable. The Rev. G. A. Le Bert, M.A., presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>RUNCORN LIBEBAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBEBAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>The third annual supper in connection with the billiard players of this club was held on Wednesday evening in Mr. Riley's Cafe, Bridge-st., and a splendid spread was set before the large company. After the cloth had been cleared Mr. W. Wright took the chair, and the loyal and other toasts were proposed and hon- oured, amongst them being " Success to the Liberal Club" and "Long life to the Grand Old Man" and the health of Mr. Brunner, M.P., the president of the Club. An interesting programme of songs, recitations &amp;c , was gone through by Messrs. J. Nickson, Woods, H. Wright, R. Moores, E. Johnson, Stanway, Parker, and Mr. Commissioner Lea. Mr. Clifford Le Cantem was the accompanist. Both Purrmisures Shope are under personal superYlsion.aad austumers may rely upon reeelviugeyeryattentiou.—dfdat. DOMESTIC INFELICITY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday,:at the Widnes Petty Sessions before Mr. E. L. Muspratt, and Lieutenant-Colonel Gaskell, James Duzbury, Padiham, again appeared to answer a sum- mons taken out by his wife for deserting her. The case was before the court a fortnight ago, and was then ad- journed fer complainant to prove whether defendant had a home provided for her at Padiham, as he alleged. Ellen Whiteley, mother of the complainant, said she had been to Padiham and from inquiries made she had dis- covered that defendont had taken a room in a common lodging-house, but had no house. Defendant called William Duxbury, who stated that defendant rented a house adjoining a Wesleyan Chapel in Padiham. He had also obtained employment where he would have a provided. The Bench made an order for defendant to pay ss. per week. OLD PEOPLE'S TREAT AT FIALTON. The 24th annual treat to the old people of Halton took place at the Norton Arms, when about 40 sat down to a most substantial dinner. The Rev. G. D. Wray presided and he was supported by the Rev. J. Hanby, Mr. T. Wright (vice chairman), Mr. Jos. Stokes, Mr. Rowe, Mr. Nixon, and Mr. Haddock. After the meal had been thoroughly enjoyed, a number of toasts were honoured. Mr. Stokes proposed that of the Bishop, clergy, and ministers of all denominations. Be referred to the good work that was being accomplished 'in the parish by the Vicar and the Rev. J. Hanby. Both of these gentlemen responded.—The Chairman submitted the health of Mr. T. Wright, who was one of thr founders of the treat, in conjunction with the late Dr. Johnson, 24 years ago. Mr. Wright, in acknowledging the compliment said their thanks were due to the donors of the funds, who were— Sir Richard and Lady Brooke, Mr. Barrow, Mrs. Cox, Dr. Carruthers, Mr. Brunner, M.P., Messrs. Charles Reynolds, James Reynolds, Henry Reynolds. Johnson, Jones, Wright, Haddock, Nixon, Rowe and Lea.</p>
        <p>Go to PRITCHARD'S for the best five guinea watch a work- ingman can have.—Advt. PROMOTION OF A RUNCORN ENGINEER...</p>
        <p>Our readers will learn with much pleasure of the pro- motion of Mr. John Burgess, second engineer of the Bridgewater Canal Undertakings of the Ship Canal Co., to the post of engineer of the Rochdale Canal Co. There were about 200 applications for the appointment,which were eventually reduced to six, and the choice of the directors fell open Mr. Burgess. The successful candi- date who is about 38 years of age, has had a lifelong ac- quaintance with canal work. His grandfather and his father before him held responsible positions in connec- tion with the Bridgewater Canals, and he commenced his career after leaving school in the traffic department of the undertaking 24 years ago. Four years later he was transferred to the engineering office, and for the past fifteen years he has held the responsible office of chief assistant engineer. During his long connection with the firm Mr. Burgess has had a large and varied experience of canal engineering, not only in works of ordinary maintenance, but in the making of deviations, locks, erection of buildings, and such works of an ex- traordinary character. In the construction of the hand- some suite of offices and administrative buildings at Victoria Bridge, at Runcorn, and in the con- struction of the large and important work on the Sprinch, Mr. Burgess acted as Mr. Wiswairs, the en- gineer's, right hand colleague, and he has taken a prominent part in other responsible affairs. Six years ago the engineer's offices were removed from Manchester to Runcorn, and during that time Mr. Burgess has re- sided in the ancient Cheshire town, and lie has made troops of friends and acquaintances, and while all will cordially congratulate him upon his promotion, they will be heartily sorry to lose him from their midst. ' "</p>
        <p>_ d 08EPH PRITCHARD has the best stock of ladies' silver and gold watches in the town.—Advt. DEATH AND FUNERAL OF DR. IVlcCemnninoH, OF WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Dr. McCambridge, of Widnes, succumbed to a pro- longed attack of bronchitis, on Saturday morning, and the news of his death was received with feelings of deep regret by all classes of the townspeople. For many years he had suffered from a weak chest, and it had been his custom to take a voyage to a warmer clime in the winter months, but he could not be prevailed upon to do so this year, and, contracting a cold, acute bron- chitis was developed, with the painful result mentioned. The deceased gentleman was the son of a contractor at Randlestown. Ireland, and entering Dublin University at an early age, he had a brilliant career there,and obtained the degrees of Doctor of Medicine and Bachelor of Sur- gery. About ten years age he came to Widnes as the partner of the late Dr. McDonnell, and together they acquired a large practice, to which Dr. McCambridge's skill and genial character greatly contributed. The mortal remains were conveyed to St. Patrick's Church, where th7y laid in state during Sunday, and on Monday a solemn Requeim Mass was conducted by the Rev. Dean Finegan, assisted by the Rev. Father Chapman and the Rev. Father Curran. At the conclusion of the service, the coffin,which was hidden beneath numerous beautiful wreaths, was borne to the London and North-Western Railway Station on the shoulders of a number of young men, viz., Messrs. J. Cain,T. Caldwell, W. Pennington, P. Appleton, C. Broome, J. Smith, J. Brady. and F. Wright. There was a long procession of mourners, in- cluding the whole of the members of the medical faculty in the district,and many tradesmen and personal friends. There was a large crowd of spectators assem- bled at the railway station, and the blinds of all the establishments in the vicinity were drawn. On Tuesday the body was interred in the family vault at Randles- town. The deceased gentleman had only attained the age of 33 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>WIDNES PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. E. IC-Mu-s-prrt- tit- ai;crLieutenant-Colonel Gaskell.</p>
        <p>DRIINLEINESB.—WIIIiam Nazzey, Water-st. ; William Hersee, Bold ; Michael McDermott, Mary-st. ; and Wm. Beresford, liwell-st-, were each fined for being drunk. John Bonny, carter, Ditton, were charged with having been drunk in charge of a horse and cart, and was fined ss. and costs, or seven days in default. BREACH OF TEN PEAca.—John Atkinson, Pleasant- at., pleaded guilty to a charge of committing a breach of the peace, and was bwild over to keep the peace for three months. REFORMATORY ACT.—Jas. Hopkins, Margaret-st., was summoned under the Reformatory School Act, to show cause why he should not contribute towards the main- tenance of his son, who is confined in Clarence Reforma- tory School.—Superintendent Keighley said he had been instructed by the Inspector of Reformatory Schools to apply for an order of is. 6d. per week towards the maintenace of his son.—P.C. Worsfold proved that de- fendant's wages had varied from £2 16s. V) £1 its. 6d. during the past month.—The rder was made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. C. Reynolds and Dr. Steele. TEMPORARY AUTHORITY.—Mrs. Hancock, widow of Frederick Hancock, was granted permission to sell at the Camden Hotel under the license held by her late husband. A DOG CASE.—John Morris, fish dealer, was summoned for keeping a dog without license and also for allow- ing the animal to be at large, in contravention ef the Rabies Muzzling Dogs Order.—The defendant contended that the dog belonged to Mr. Finegan, of the Boathouse Inn.—Both summonses were dismissed. DRUNKENNESS.—James Cusick was fined 23.6 d. and costs for being drunk and disorderly. William Brad- bury, of Sutton, was fined 10s. for being drunk while in charge of a horse and lurrv. A BOY THlEF.—Thomas- Collins, aged 15 years, was charged with stealing a tin of preserved salmon worth Bd, the goods of Walter Collier, grocer, Church-street, on Saturday night.—The prisoner pleaded guilty, and he was tined 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held on Tuesday. Present the Mayor (Alderman Gossage), Aldermen Timmins, Sadler, Wareing, and Williamson, Colin. cillors Barlow, Shores,Fitzpatrick, Quinn, Singleton Shuttleworth, llodgkinson, Smith, Bullough, Neil, S. Owens, G. I. Neil,Davies, and Longton,with Messrs.ll.S.Oppenheim(l'own clerk),T.Craik(deputy clerk), S. Boyle (accountant), Dr. O'Keeffe (medical officer),Messre. I. Carr (engineer), J.S. Sinclair (sur- veyor), and W. Lowe (nuisance inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>SEASONABLE WISHES:</head>
        <p>SEASONABLE WISHES:</p>
        <p>The Mayor wished the Council a happy and prosperous New Year.—Alderman Wareing said the members reci- procated His Worship's good wishes, and he was only expressing what must be in the minds of all present when he said they sympa.thi.ed very deeply with him in the sorrow that had recently overshadowed his house.— The Mayor in acknowledging the references remarked that his son was nearly convalescent, and lie hoped he would be able in a short time to take his seat at the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>THI POLICE FORCE,</head>
        <p>THI POLICE FORCE,</p>
        <p>The Twin Clerk submitted a draft agreement, for- warded by the Clerk of the Peace arranging for the continuance of the county police in the positi m they had hitherto held in Widnes.—On the proposition of Alderman Wareing, seconded by Councillor Shores, the matter was referred to the General Purposes Commit- tee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>HIGHWAY commirrEs.</head>
        <p>HIGHWAY commirrEs.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee contained the following resolutions That the Chairman, Deputy-Chairman, Councillors G. I: Neil, Shuttleworth, and Quinn be appointed a sub-com- mittee to report upon the present lighting requirements of the borough. That the Surveyor proceed forthwith with the Lowerhouse-lane improvement works, and that he prepare plans and estimates for the improvement of Peelhouse-lane between Albert-road and Milltield-road and for the sewering of the Appleton district, also that all applications for labour upon such works shall come through individual members of the committee. That the Surveyor prepare plans and estimates for the paving land completion of Oxford-st. and passages, Vine-st. and passages,and Brendan-st., with the view to notices being issued under section 150 of the Public Health Act, 1875. That application be made to the Local Government BGard for its sanction to the borrowing of £15,510 15.6 d., being the amount estimated to be required for the carry- ing out of the following private street works, for which 'notices have been issued under the 150th section of the Public Health Ant, 1875, viz. :—(1) Waterloo-roa3 dis- trict No. 1, streets and passages from Hutchinson-st., to 'Milton-at., £4,886 10s. ; (2) Waterloo-road district N 0.2, ' streets and passages from Milton-st. to Church-street, £8,729 6s. 6d. ; (3) Mill Brow district, Joseph-street, William-st., &amp;c., and passages connected therewith, £3,920 ss. That the Surveyor negotiate with the owners for the improvement of the road at the corner of Albert-road and Page-lane.—Alder- man Sadler moved and Councillor Shuttleworth seconded the adoption of the minutes.—Alderman Timmis inquired whether the Committee would receive a deputation from West Bsnk, with reference to the light- ing of that district, and also whether the reference to workmen applying through members of the Committee for employment on the improvement works, was not a misprint.—Ccuncillor F. Neil asked was it intended to increase the lighting only in streets already adopted,and also in the streets not adopted.—Alderman Sadler said the Committee would be only too glad to receive assist- ance from any deputation as to the ' lighting require- ments. The Committee did not propose to light un- adopted streets. As to the employment of men, the resolution was not a misprint. Recently when ultra- labour had been employed, many men who had only come into the town overnight had been employed by the surveyor, and residents in the tow'n had thereby been prevented from receiving, work. If the applications came through the members of the Committee they could recommend bona fide residents to the surveyor.—The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>HEALTH Or THB DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>HEALTH Or THB DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee submitted a resolution recom- mending that Mr. Shawcross, M.R.C.V.S., be appointed veterinary inspector for the borough,at a salaryof £l5 per annum. There were ten patients in the Accident Hospital and five patients in the Infectious Diseases Hospital. The Medical Officer reported that during December there were 80 births and 30 deaths, being at the respective rates of 42.68 and 16.00. Nineteen of the death, occur- red amongst children under five years. Zymotic dis- eases caused four deaths, 21 notifications of infectious disease had been received, of which 2 were typhoid fever, 8 croup, and 7 scarlet fever. The Town Clerk and surieyor had been instructed to obtain two suitable sites for night soil depots.—Councillor G. I. Neil moved and Councillor flodgkinson seconded the adoption of the minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>DANCING AT THE ACCIDENT HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>DANCING AT THE ACCIDENT HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>CouncilroTh—ough said it was reported that at the Christmas treat to the inmates of the Accident Hospital, dancing was carried on. He thought a place of suffer- ing like that was not a fit place for dancing until the early hours of the morning. Then as to the visiting hours at the hospital, the rules should be strictly adhered to or else taken away altogether.—Councillor Neil replied that the treat was given entirely out of subscriptions. He was not aware there was any dancing until the early hours of the morning. He was there himself, and cer- tainly there was a little dancing indulged in, and he did not see any harm in it. The rules should have attention. —The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>TTIE DITT-ON SCHOOL DIFFICULTY.</head>
        <p>TTIE DITT-ON SCHOOL DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>The Gas and Water Committee had received a letter, on the 10th December, 1892, from the Vicar and ward- ens of Ditton Church, upon the subject of the proposed enlargement of Ditton Church Schools,which are at pre- sent insufficient for the children in attendance, and stating that the matter was one calling for consideration, inasmuch as should the managers fail to interest owners in the present educational need, the Education Depart- ment would thrust a School Board upon the township. The managers suggested that all owners of property should agree to contribute (if necessary) at the rate of say 9d. in the £, with a view to causing the expense to fall as lightly as possible upon the owners and ratepayers, and the Clerk was instructed to reply that the Corporation were not in a position to accede to the sug- gestion. - - -</p>
        <p>PRICE OF OAS AND WATER.</p>
        <p>The Gas and Water Committee had received a letter from the United Alkali Co., Limited, asking the atten- tion of the Committee to the subject of the price of gas and water with a view to a reduction. It was stated in the letter that if the Committee could see its way to reduce the prices there was every prubability that their works would use increased quantities of both gas and water, and if the reduction in water were sufficient, the pumping from private wells might be discontinued. Resolved that the engineer report fully upon the matter at the next meeting. .</p>
        <p>_ The minutes were approved on the motion of Alder- man Timmis.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee submitted standing orders for approval. The order of most general interest was that granting strangers the privilege of attending the meetings of the Council and Library Committee and to report and publish their proceedings. It was resolved that the fittings in the council chamber be in accord- ance with a plan proposed by the surveyor on the lines of the council chamber at "Nelson. Alderman Wareing said it was intended only to arrange the chamber tem- porarily,until it was seen what course the Council would take in regard to the extention of the Town Hall.— Carried.</p>
        <p>LOANS. The Accountant reported that mortgages amounting to £lO,OOO on Gas Works accounts, And £17,600 on Water Works accounts expired on the 25th March next. As a large proportion of these amounts would no doubt be offered for renewal, the accountant asked for instruc- tions as to the terms upon which they may be renewed. The present rate of interest on £5,000 of the amount fallinz due was 4 per cent., on £6,000, 31 per cent., and on £lB,OOO, 3i per cent. The Committee resolved that the loans be renewed for further periods at 3 per cent., or subject to six months' notice at 3 per cent. ,or paid off and re-borrowed at the rate of interest stated. Accounts amounting to £4,220 Is. 2d.were passed for payment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TRH FINANCILL POSITION.</head>
        <p>TRH FINANCILL POSITION.</p>
        <p>The Accountant also submitted to the Finance Com- mittee a statement of assets and liabilities, each showing a total of £331,418 7s. id. A statement of borrowing powers showed that £312,484 had been borrowed, amount now owing. £260,649 193. 2d., and the unex- hausted borrowing bowers £25,682 10s. 2d. In moving the adoption of the minutes, Councillor Owen said the Committee would be able to save £llO on the renewal of the loans mentioned by the accountant. Referring to the statement of assets, &amp;c., Councillor Owens said they had a very large indebtedness, but it was gratifying to know that it was more than covered, and that there was something like £37,350 of a surplus. They might congratulate themselves on having such an excellent balance sheet.—Alderman Williamson seconded the adoption of the minutes, and they were carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>TECHNICAL INSTECTION CO3IMI TTF:E.</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTECTION CO3IMI TTF:E.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of this committee (Mr.T.Wade-Deacon) reported that the deputation appointed at the last meet- ing had met representatives from the various Lancashire towns interested in building technical schools, at Man- chester, on the 16th inst., when the matter of apprc.ach- Mg the County Council was fully discussed, and a joint deputation, of which he was appointed chairman, con- sisting of representatives from the towas of Widnes, Nelson, Accrington, Heywood, Leigh, and Atherton, was appointed to wait upon the County Council. The de- putation subsequently attended before the Technical Instruction Committee of the County Council on the 19th inst., and laid its views before that body,and urged that grants might be made in aid of buildings. The Chair- man further reported that he had received a letter dated , 22nd December, 1892, fican Mr. J. A. Bennion, director of Technical Instruction, stating that after a lengthened discussion, the. committee passed a resolution declining to recommend the County Council to make any grants in aid of buildings. Letter, 23rd December, 1892, from Mr. J. A. Bennion, in reply to an application from the Town Clerk, and stating that the County Technical Instruction Committee was prepared to consider appli-</p>
        <p>cations for grants in aid of apparatus and appliances, but would want a detailed estimate of the articles re- quired befcre such application could be taken into con- sideration,read.—The committee resolved that the letter be referred to the Classes Sub-Committee, to make such an application.—lt was further resolved, on the sugges- tion of Dr. Campbell Brown, that the railway fares of local students attending lectures at University,Liverpool, be paid by the committee during the current year.— Councillor F. Neil moved the adoption of the minutes, and said the committee regretted very much that they had been unable to induce the County Council to give a grant in aid of the building. The committee had under consideration the desirability of commencing classes in carpentry and engineering, which would require expen- sive appliances,and they were under the impression that it they did not secure the whole of the sum required, they would, at least, obtain a portion of jt. —Alderman Wareing objected to the principle of paying the railway fares of students, it was opening a very wide door.—Mr. Neil said it was done to enceurag,,e technical instruction in every way possible ; only the fees of those students who could not afford were paid.—The Mayor said up to the time of the adoption of the Technical Inatruction Act he had paid the fares, but the Corpora- tion now had the power. It was a great encouragement So these young men to be ablo to attend the classes at the University, and it was for the good of Widnes,and he personally thought it was a right thing to do.— rhe cost he believed was not more than 30s. a year.—The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>CEMETERY 4IIRSTION.</head>
        <p>THE CEMETERY 4IIRSTION.</p>
        <p>Councillor Flodgkinson gave- notice that at the next meeting he would move that the Town Clerk be instrua- ed to obtain a return from the various burial authorities in Widnes as to the number of interments that had taken place during the last three years, and as to the space at present unoccupied in their various burial grounds in the borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>HOSPITALITY.</head>
        <p>HOSPITALITY.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the Mayor the members of the Council adjourned into his parlour, which has been tastefully furnished and decorated, and partook of a glass of wine. Good Wishes appropriate to the seas on, were exchanged.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY AFFRAY BETWEEN BOYS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary combat between boys, des- cribed by an eye witness is a pitched battle, took place at Runcorn on Tuesday night. About 8 o'clock two gangs, one known as the " Duke's Field gang "and the other as the " Cooper-street gang" met in Lord-street, and a serious conflict ensued. Sticks, belts, and all kinds of weapons were used, besides fists and feet, and the disturbance continued for a considerable time. While the fight was at its height a boy named Arthur Boyle, aged ten, ran into the chipped potatoe shop of Mr. Fearnett in Lord-street. He was bleeding profusely, his clothing being saturated with blood and an examination revealed a large gash in the lads left side, just below the heart. Dr. MacLennan was sent for and was promptly in attendance, and he con- sidered the case so serious that he had Dr. MacKenzie in consultation, came to the conclusion that it was advis- able that the injured boy's depositions should be taken. Between twelve and one o'clock, Mr. Brundrit accom- panied by Mr. F. Johnson, deputy magistrates clerk, visited the boy's home in Blantyre-at., and took his deposition. He stated that a boy named Thomas Fisher, 13 years of age, of 2 Queen-st., rushed at him and struck him on the side with a knife. Inspector Cooper brought several boys to his bedside, and the in- jured lad without hesitation selected Fisher as his assail- ant. Dr. MacLennan gave it as his opinion that great force must have been used in dealing the blow. On Wednesday morning Fisher was brought before Mr. J. Brundrit and Mr. G. L. Davies at Runcorn Police Court, on a charge of unlawfully wounding Boyle, with intent to do him grievous bodily harm, and on the application of Superintendent Atherton, the prisoner was remanded I for a week. He was, however, released on bail, in two sureties of £5 each. During Wednesday Royle's life was despaired of, but on Thursday his condition had considerably improved, and with good attention and quietness, there is some hope of his ultimate recovery. The poor boy had lost a very large quantity of blood, and he would have speedily bled to death, but for the prompt attention of the doctor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>B UNCO RN WATER BILL</head>
        <p>B UNCO RN WATER BILL</p>
        <p>THE COMPANY'S VIEW OF THE TRANSACTION. On Wednesday afternoon an extraordinary meeting of the shareholders of Runcorn, Weston, and Halton Waterworks, Co., was held in the Pub- lic Hall, for the purpose of considering the terms and conditions, if any, upon which the Company shall sell, and the Runcorn Improvement Commis- sioners shall purchase the undertaking of the Co., and to approve or otherwise of the Bill proposed to be introduced into Parliament. Mr. J. Brundrit, J.P., chairman of directors, presided, and there were present Messrs. J. Simpson, W. Hayes, W. Whittaker, J. Collier, T. Wright, R. Foulkes, A. Shaw. H. Ackerley, Thos. Blinston, W. Jones, J. Ashton (solicitor to the Co.), W. F. Salkeld (secretary), and others. Mr. Ashton read the clauses of the Bill affecting the Waterworks Co., from which it appeared that the capi- tal of the Co. consisted of £4.000 in preference shares at 5 per cent. ; £13,500 in debenture shares at 4± per cent., and £2,900 in preference shares at 4 per cent., £30,000 in ordinary shares, and a bonded debt of £2,ooo.—Mr. Ashton also read the agreement proposed to be entered into with the Commissioners in which the price was set down at £67,500,t0 be paid on transfer day, September, 30th, 1893, subject to a satisfactory examin- ation of the books and works of the Co. .</p>
        <p>..„ The Chairman said it would be necessary, in order that the shareholders might know to what they were giving their sanction,for him to call the minds of those pre- sent back to the time when the company was first started. The company was formed in the year 1885. and they began to sell in the year 1870. The census of 1871 gave the population of the three townships which they sup- plied at 14,770 ; now, in the year 1891, twenty years afterwards, the population was 2,944, or an increase of close upon ten thousand in that time. It would be at once apparent to them that the provision made at that time for these three townships. which was then more than ample, might not necessarily be found sufficient 20 years later, with ten thousand more people to be sup- plied. And it was in connection with the Manchester Ship Canal coming through the place, and the probabi- lity of large works some time being established on the banks of tit:a canal, and a larger increase in population, that the directors found it necessary to consult their engineer in London, Mr. Ha.wksley—than whom there was no better authority on water—as to the advisability of providing a further supply. Mr. Ilawksley at once pointed out that they would have to go to Parliament for powers to obtain the further supply it would be necessary for them to have in a short time, and he (Mr. Brundrit) mentioned this fact in his remarks at the last meeting of the company in August last. It was shortly after the report of what he had said appeared in the newspapers that the directors were approached in an informal way by local Commissioners as to whether they would be inclined to sell in the event of the Board of Commissioners thinking it necessary to pur- chase for the benefit of the town. There were several consultations in one way or another, and some negotia- tions passed, .and at last finding the time had nearly expired, within which a bill must be lodged in the Houses of Parliament, if it has to come into effect dur- ing the present year ; the directors arranged with the Commissioners to promote a Bill, but that Bill was of , no use at all unless it received the sanction on the one part of the shareholders of the Company, and of the , ratepayers of Runcorn, on the other part. The Bill was thus promoted to save time. That meeting was held that day in corrsequence of the Standing Orders of the Houma of Commons requiring a statutory meeting before the 16th of January and the questions to be asked then were: Did they approve of the Bill now before them; and were they satisfied with the agreement? He might as well tell the shareholders that going to Parliament and getting further powers to supply water would require a large increase in the capital of the Company, and there being a concensus of opinion that water and gas should belong to local authorities—wth gas they had nothing to do—the directors on being approached by the Com- missioners felt that if they could get fair and reasonable terms it would be to the advantage of both the town and the Company, and with the approval of the share- holders the directors would assist all they could in the promotion of the Bill. He moved "That the meeting of the proprietors of the Runcorn, Weston, and Halton Waterworks Co. do hereby approve of the Runcorn Water Bill now introduced in Parliament by the Run- corn Improvement Commissioners.—Mr. W. Hayes seconded. Carried unanimously ; no remarks being made. The Chairman then moved, "That this meeting of prc- prietors of the Run Water- work, Weston, and Halton Company, lierehe ratify the agreement dated the first day December, 1892, and made between the Com- pany on one part and Runcorn Improvement Commis- sioners on the other part ; and hereby authorise the directors to vary the terms in thr said agreement in such a manner as they think fit, subject to the same being confirmed by a meeting of the proprietors.' The Chairman explained that the reason for the latter part of the resolution was, that the directors had hoped to have had the negotiations favourably concluded by that meeting in regard to the sale and purchase, but affairs had not arrived at that happy conclusion. They hoped, however, that they should reach that consummation shortly, therefore they asked the permission of the shareholders to ratify the agreement, and call a further meeting. They did not ask them to agree to those terms, in case they might be varied for one reason or an- other, but they would be called together again before the Bill was brought forward in Parliament, and state to them what the exact terms were.—Mr. Collier seconded the motion, which was also carried unanimouslv.—A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the prOceed- ings.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>I see that on Wednesday, in the High Court, the first case under the Act promoted by the Irwell and Mersey Rivers Pollution Commission was dis- posed of. The Bury Corporation had been con- victed by the magistrates on a charge of polluting the rivers Irwell and Roach, and appea;ed, but the conviction was sustained. I am not sorry that something practical is being done in order to show that the rivers in this part of tlie country are not to be created into open cesspools and sewers, with the result that the atmosphere, as well as the water, is filled with poisonous elements. I should certainly like to see some stir made to remedy the condition of the Mersey, as it is in Warrington. The fact is that the river here does emit the most abominable smells occasionally, and one is not sur- prised when one looks down at it from the bridge sometimes and sees the murky blackness it assumes, though this is occasionally diversified by the white frothy-looking liquid which floats on its bosom.</p>
        <p>The whiteness, I may say, is not caused by any impurity, but is simply due to the churning agita- tion of Messrs. Fairclough's water-wheels, aud no harm is done by this, while it presents additional subject-matter for the aquatic studies of the multi- tudinous loafers who delight in congregating about the river-side, contemplating the waters below. But the blackness is quite another element, and a classic artist might find realistic inspiration in it for a conception of Acheron. This blackness is caused by the sewage and manufacturing refuse of Man- chester. How long is this state of things to con- tinue?</p>
        <p>I believe that a deputation is about to wait on the Home Secretary, in order to urge upon him the desirability of some reform of the burial laws. I think the present imbroglio in which the Warring- ton Union Rural Sanitary Authority, are involved as to the Rixton-with-Glazebrook Cemetery ques- tion, is a fair example of the necessity of revision of the burial laws. The Sanitary Authority have ' obtained sanction to borrow a sum of money for the provision of the cemetery, for which they have secured ground, and since then they have been called upon to erect a mortuary chapel at the cost. of £2OO on the ground, for the use of the Church of England. Now, it happens that there in an episco- pal Church close to the cemetery site, with only a narrow lane intervening, but though the Church ;of England people themselves say that their present building will ansvi er their purpose for burial ser- vices, and neither the inhabitants nor the Sanitary Authority desire the mortuary chapel, the Local Government Board nevertheless declare that it must be built.</p>
        <p>The lane is too broad.and I suppose the minds of the Local Government Board are not broad enough to admit of the proposed editicebeing dispensed with. We are accustomed to hear ot ecclesiastical bigotry as opposed to lay liberality in burial matters, but. here is a case of exactly the reverse, for while the church clergyman and the bishop are content to allow this unhallowed country lane to intervene be- tween the consecrated cemetery and the consecr- ated building they are desirous of using as a mortuary, the lay Local Government Board says this must not be. The circumstances form a curious and not very edifying anomaly for the pub- lic to exercise its mind upon.</p>
        <p>I see that the front page of " The Indepandent and Nonconformist," of this week, contains an ad- mirable portrait of the Rev. J. Yonge, the pastor of Wycliffe. Along with the portrait is giv9,n a sketch of the history of Congregationalism in War-, rington, and some biographical details of Mr Yonge's career. Among other things it is pointed out that when Mr. Yonge took up the pastorate of Wycliffe, sixteen years ago, the membership was 168, while now it is 313.</p>
        <p>MERLIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>HELSB F.</head>
        <p>HELSB F.</p>
        <p>In the Wesleyan new Schoolroom, the Rev. E. Lloyd Jones, of Rhyl, delivered an eloquent lecture on " The disestablishment of the public-house," to a large and delighted audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>FRODSRAM,</head>
        <p>FRODSRAM,</p>
        <p>MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLess.—The opening of the second part of the session took place on Wednesday eveving, and was in the nature of a miscellaneous entertainment, comprising pianoforte soles by Mr. C. H. Hibbert, choruses by the choir, and readings by Mrs, R. Holland. Songs were also rendered by Mrs. J. G. Davies, Miss Agnes Wrench, and Messrs. J. F. Wright, and Percy Robinson. Mr. John Garner's string band was also in attendance, and gave several selections. Miss B. Lewis accompanied oaths piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LI. MM.</head>
        <p>LI. MM.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT ASSOCIATION.—A social gathering in connection with the above was held on Monday evening. There was a good attendance, and a very pleasant evening was spent in a programme of instrumental and vocal music.</p>
        <p>THE Slur CANAL WORKS.—With the temporary de- parture of the frost, a large number of men were given employment in the excavations on the Ship Canal, the operations having been delayed for a space of about three weeks owing to the remarkably keen weather which has been experienced. THE SMALLPDX EPIDEMIC —The authorities at Lvmm have still been unable to stamp out the disease. For a period of 14 days prior to Monday there had been no fresh outbreak, and it was generally hoped that the dis- ease had been stamped out, but on Monday, a fresh case was detected in the house where a patient had been , previously smitten, and on the following day, Tuesday, two further eases were found. All three patients were promptly moved to the temporary hospital. It is a remarkable coincidence that on the past three occasions when outbreaks of the disease have been discovered cases were not detected until a period of 14 days had elapsed between each detection. Very few meetings have , recently taken place owing to the sparse attendances which have characterized the gatherings which have been held. Dr. Fox emphasizes the fact that all the cases of smallpox which have occurred in the town have been confined to one locality outside the village of Lvmm, and he has caused placards to be posted to this effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday "evening a free tea was given in the Wesleyan school, to the members of the various society classes in connection with the chapel. including Mrs. Walker's, Mrs. Wigfield's, Mrs. R. Cook's, Miss Routledge's, Miss Hobson's, and Miss E. Hughes's classes, also Mr. R. Garnett's and Mr.s Wilson's. The tea was very well provided by Mesdames J. Jackson, P. Jackson, Cook, Ireland and Miss Once. After tea a service was held in the chopel at which speeches were given by the Revs. J. Gunnell (resident minister), A. Freeman, T. Rose, and J. Malan, Mr. R. Garnett and Mr. J. Atherton. Mtrrusi, IMPUOVNMENT Assocismori. On Wednesday night the weekly meeting of the above, took place in the Wesleyan schoolroom. The lecturer was Mr. A. Bennett, of Padgate, and hia subject was, "The possibilities of Federation." Mr. G. Randall was in the chair. The subject was most interesting and the lecturer quite fulfilled the ex- pectations of the large audience. The following commented on the lecture : Messrs. W. H. Butler, A. White, C. E. Parker, and the chairman.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A RUNCORN FOOTBALLER.. Football enthusiasts in Runcorn, will learn with regret of the death of Mr. John Stubbs, of Dale-st., Runcorn, which took place on Wednesday evening, after a long and painful illness. The deceased for many seasons was a prominent forward in the Run- corn football team. Five years ago. however, he sustained a serious injuries to his knee,which neces- sitated his retirement from the football field.</p>
        <p>PROSPECTUS. We publish this week the Prospectus of the "Persil Flexible Wheel Tyre Syndicate Limited. This company was formed in August last with a capital of £170,000 in .£1 shares to acquire a new invention in the constructions of wheels and tyres. It is claimed that it is applicable to bicycles, tricycles, broughams, victories, omnibuses, carts, perambulators, and bath chairs &amp;c., It is stated that the shares have been dealt in as high as 425. or over 100 per cent. premium. It will be seen that the present issue is only the balance at the working capital, via.. £16,000 at a premium of i ss. Reports are published n the prospectus from many well known bicycle champions. The pros- pectus also states that orders are being received from all parts for the tyrts and wheels, and that the application list for shares closes on or before Wednesday next, Jan, 15th."</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PROCLAMATION.</head>
        <p>PROCLAMATION.</p>
        <p>£2O Reward.-2.lThereas it has come to our know- ledge that certain unscrupulous person or persons have lately been offering for sale and passing off Quinine Bitters not ot our manufacture, and have endeavoured when Grrucynt EvaNs' QUININE Smarts has been ssked for, to substitute and pass off other preparations.</p>
        <p>The above Reward will be paid to anyone who will give such information as will enable us to ensure conviction in any case of passing off or substitut- ing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>WARNING!</head>
        <p>WARNING!</p>
        <p>We also desire to warn the Public to be on their guard,and see the name " GWILYSI EVANS' Smalls " on each bottleand label.</p>
        <p>For fgrther PROTECTION each bottle has the Government Stamp, witi the name " GWILYM EVANS, F.C.S., M.P.S., LLANELLY," engraved thereon,—to imitate which is FORGERY.</p>
        <p>Signed, QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING</p>
        <p>CO., LD., LLANELLY.</p>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (Alderman Greenall), and Messrs. F. Monks, J. W. Hayward, W. Pierpoint, and</p>
        <p>S. Wright. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY .—Charles and Mary Fudge,on the 31st ult., in Fennell-st., were discharged with a caution. DRUNK IN CHARGE OF HORSES.—John Edwards was charged with beidg drunk in charge of two horses and a 'bus in Bridge-st.--The Chief Constable said the man had not been driving since. The Watch Committee had bis case before them, and were waiting for the decision of the court. Be was fined ss. _ . _</p>
        <p>TIIESDAY.—Before Messrs. R. Garnett (in the chair), and</p>
        <p>E. J. Guest.</p>
        <p>AN OBSCENE DIANAI:2-Yw—o-m—an named Diana Bar- nett, of Golden Grove-st., was fined ss. and costs for using obscene language on the 3rd inst. SHOPPING AT OTHER PEOPLE'S EXPENSE.—A domestic servant named Elizabeth Rotherford, of Brick-st., was charged with obtaining a parcel of groceries, the pro- perty of Messrs. Milling and Co., by false pretences. The evidence showed that the prisoner, who had formerly been a servant in the employ of Mr. Thomas Fletcher, Grappenhall, went to Millingrs shop on Satur- day night with a note, purporting to come from Mr. Fletcher, and asked for articles to the value of Bs. to be sent by the bearer. The articles were given to the prisoner, and the account was sent to Mr. Fletcher. He went down to the shop and denied the purchase, where- upon the matter was reported to the police, and on Monday the woman was taken into custody by D.S. Monaghan.—lt was stated that some months previously she had been before the magistrates for stealing £lB from a deaf and dumb brother. The prisoner, who cried bitterly in the dock, pleaded for leniency; but the Chairman said they could inflict no smaller penalty upon her than a month's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>FElDAY.—Before Alderman Wright (in the chair), Dr _ _ -</p>
        <p>Gomel' and Councillor- H. 'Roberts. APPRASING HUNGER WITH SCRAP IRON.—Three boys, Arthur Lomax, John Yltes,and John Allen, all of Cross- Jey-st., were charged with stealing, from an enclosure</p>
        <p>4W4.041</p>
        <p>in Battersby-lane, 1031b3 of scrap iron, the property of the Cheshire Lines Railway Company. Mr. P. Davies appeared to prosecute on behalf of the Railway Com- pany. He described how the previous afternoon, an employe of the company observed the three youths to climb into the enclosure and commence to put the iron in the sacks. He fetched two of the company's police- men from Warrington station, and they arrested the prisoners as they were about to leave the enclosure with their spoil. The prisoners said they were hungry, and had no food at home. The Bench ordered them to be detained in the cells at the Police Station for 24 hours. A NEGLECT OF CHILDREN CASE.—John Fiddler and his wife Sarah Ann Fiddler,appeared to an adjourned charge of having neglected their children. The case was ad- journed a month ago to give the defendants an oppor- tunity of bettering their own and their children's condi- tion. Poverty had then been pleaded in extenuation of their offence.—lnspector Uphill stated that he had watched the case during the past four weeks, and had seen great improvement—so much so that he asked to be allowed to withdraw the summons.—The summons was accordingly withdrawn. A I)IFM:it:TING APERFINTICE.—An adjourned summons was heard against a girl named Alice Bowers, appren- tice to Mr. Mills, fustian cutter, for unlawfully absent- ing herself from work. The girl has several times been up for the same offence. The Bench considered her incorrigable and committed her to jail for 14 days.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>BEECHAM'S PILLS</head>
        <p>BEECHAM'S PILLS</p>
        <p>BEECHAM'S PILLS.</p>
        <p>BEECHAIVI'S PILLS.</p>
        <p>BE. CHAIVI'S PILLS.</p>
        <p>'AECHAM'S PILLS:</p>
        <p>BEECHAM'S PILLS.</p>
        <p>BEECHAM'S PILLS.</p>
        <p>T '"( CHAM'S</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 14, 1893.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
