      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUGBY.</head>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS v. TorrartdroN.—At St. Helens, before very few spectators. The ground had been protected by straw; and this was turned over the snow, and an ordinary game was played. St. Helens won the toss, and played with a strong wind at their backs. The visitors kicked off. Cross received, and passed to Doherty, who ran along touch, but was collared by Simcock, and shortly afterwards a minor was forced. Even play followed, and Dearden, receiving near centre passed to Cross, who in turn gave to Sudlow, the latter scoring under the posts. Foulkes took the kick, but the ball struck the crossbar and rebounded. The Saints still pressed, and some good passing took place between the backs. Ainsworth relieved with a long kick, and this was further improved by a good dribble, transfer- ring play to the Saints' 25. Foulkes returned to centre. Half-time :—St. Helens 1 try, Tottington nil. Final result :—St. Helens 2 tries, Tottington nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT CUP COMPETITION. WARRINGTON V. LIVERPOOL PRINTERS.—The Warring- ton Association met the Live/pool Printers at Arpley,on Saturday, in the third round of the Liverpool and Dis- trict Cup Competition. Snow covered the field to the extent of three or four inches. There were a good num- ber of spectators present. The visitors made a protest against the state of the ground, and Warrington started the ball against the wind. For the first five minutes play was of an even character, both goals being visited in turn, but it was at once obvious that the Warrington forwards were far too light for the ground, whilst their most sturdy and older opponents were quite at home in the deep snow. The visitors were not long in putting to their credit a goal, and in much shorter time added still aaother goal. Warrington now seemed to realise that they were really playing a cup tie, and the ball rolled merrily towards the visitors' goal, where Pierpoint,from an easy position, made a good attempt to score, Forbes immediately afterwards heading the ball into the goal mouth, the visitors' custodian clearing without diffi- culty. Half time arrived with the score—Printers, two goals ; Warrington, nil. Shortly after restarting opera- tions, the homesters' left half back. Bannon, had his leg injured, and was prevented from further partaking in the game. After this somewhat uulucky occurrence the Warrington players seemed to lose heart, and though the backs played a strong game, the forwards could never do anything with the ball, missing chance after chance. During the latter part of the game the visiting forwards managed to pile on five more goals, the result being Liverpool Printers, seven goals ; Warrington nil. The Warringtonians w/ere obviously on account of the ground, but they cannot feel dissatisfied on the whole with the year's performances, as they never before had the pleasure of getting over the first round of the cup competition. The backs played a splendid game, and the result falls solely on the forwards' shoulders, who were completely " lost." Davies at full back was a tower of strength, and Jones, at goal, is back again in his old form. Had it not been for the latter's defence the defeat of the Warrington lads would have been heavier than it was.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>MINERS' CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>MINERS' CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>A special Conference of miners' delegates, represent- ing all the mining districts in Great Britain, assembled at Birmingham on Monday, to discuss the Minors' Eight Hours Bill, the Employers' Liability Bill, and other measures. Mr. S. Woods, M.P., was elected president, and Mr. Cowey, a Yorkshire delegate, vice president of the Conference. Credentials were, presented by 64 dele- gates, representing about 270,000 miners in union. The Confarence will extend over Wednesday, and will be fol- lowed by the meeting of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain.</p>
        <p>After a heated discussion on Wednesday, the Confer- ence passed a resolution in favour of supporting the Eight Hours (Miners) Bill in Parliament. Mr. Wilson and Mr. Johnson, representing 50,000 miners of the 270,000 represented at the conference, were alone in support of the acquirement of an eight hours day by organisation. A resolution in fovonr of abolishing the clause in the Employers' Liability Bill giving power to contract out of the Act was carried. Resolutions were adopted in favour of the appointment of a Government inspector for every 10,000 miners, of compulsory report- in of inspectors of weights and measures, and of prac- tical miners serving on juries.</p>
