      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>IN THE SHEILA) COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Nine or ten figures were seen to enter the 2.10 train to Birmingham, at Bank Quay station, on Saturday. From the agile manner in which they conducted themselves in throwing apparatus into the luggage van, almost anyone would have guessed that they were not rheumatic undertakers, but athletes. And indeed they were, for they com- prised the selected " eight " from the Gymnasium, who were going to meet Dolobran Athletic Club, Birmingham, in the first round of N.P.R.S. shield competition. The famous town of boxers and iron- ware was reached at five o'clock, and after having tea the party set out for the Masonic , Ball, which they , reached at seven o'clock. There was a good number of spectators present, but as they were all Birmingham people Warrington did not receive much applause from them ; and it never ran to a cheer. After waiting some time for the judge, Mr. F. J. Harvey, to make an appearance, they received a tele- gram from him stating that his train had broken down at Northampton. He concluded the telegram by stating that they could go .on with the jumping if they liked. They did so. But as the competition went on, after the judge had arrived,the Warrington " eight " became very discontented with their lot. They were giving a display cf 14 different, exercises against a variety of eight on the part of their opponents. In the high jumping the visi- tors were much the best team. Their average would be about 12 marks each, to an average of 8 each on the part of their opponents. Warrington's total for this was 89, and Dolobran's 69. A. L. Fitt was the best jumper for Dolobran, whilst D. Walsh could claim a similar position, in the Warrington eight. Next came the dumb- bell exercise. It was this that annoyed Warrington. Dolobran exhibited an old discarded exercise which Warrington had scorned to practice, and they were also prompted by command from their conductor. There were only about three features of variety in the exercise,whilst the display of Warrington embraced three times as many features. The results of this exercise were : Dolobran 140 marks, Warrington 130 marks. Parallel bars come next, and Warrington, very legitimately objected to their opponents' performances in this department. Dolobran scored 110 points in this, and Warrington • received 98; but the latter insist that the scores were unfair, inasmuch as they performed ,equally on the parallel and horizon- tal bars, whilst their opponents stuck almost exclusively to exercises on the parallel bar. The War- rington men, and many others who witnessed the dis- play, considered that Warrington were by far the most advanced all round team. This is much to their own credit, and that of their conductor, Mr. Kishy. The fol- lowing are the marks obtained : •</p>
