      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>NINETEEN MINERS DROWNED.</head>
        <p>NINETEEN MINERS DROWNED.</p>
        <p>A Penzance correspondent telegraphs that on Tuesday morning the Wheal Owles Mine, St. Just, became flooded and many lives were lost. The Wheal Owles is situated about a mile north of St. Just, on the borders of the cliffs overlooking the Bristol Channel, under the bed of which some of the mine's workings extend. The thine is drained by edits, whose mouths discharge into the sea at a depth of about twenty-five fathoms. There are many old workings in the Wheal Owles, and several sets have of late years been discontinued, and have become full of water. The catastrophe arose through some hundreds of tons of water in the old workings having been tapped. The inundation was wholly unexpected. Scores of narrow levels and winzespqured a concentrated stream down one of the shafts, forrifing such a fearful torrent that no miner could climb the ladder-way against it. The water rushing in soon filled the lower levels and places of temporary refuge above them and many miners, men and lads, were speedily drowned. There were forty men and boys below. These, according to the survivors, heard a terrible roar, followed by a rush of wind which blew all the lights out. The men rushed for the ladders, finding teat it was a case of life or death. Some of the most experienced and daring reached the surface, though oftentimes losing their foothold and hand-grasp in the ladderways. One young miner named Grenfell fell three fathoms, but sustained only a few scratches. He regained the ladder, and escaped. In an hour and a half the lower part of the mine was corn- pletely flooded, and formed a watery grave for those poor fellows who had been unable to escape. Skips lighted with candles were lowered to the service of the water, so that any man at the junction of the levels and shafts above the water might, if alive, know that assist- ance was at hand, and signal their own safety ; tut up to one o'clock on Tuesday afternoon there was no response, and the solemn silence in the mine gave rise to the worst fears.</p>
        <p>The following are the names of the miners reported drowned :—Edward White, Joseph Eddy, James Rowe, William Roberts, William Devey, James Edwards, Thomas Ellis, James Williams, Peter Dale, James Thomas, John Olds, Thomas Olds, Thomas Allen, Rich- ard Williams, Charles Hitchens, John Taylor, Mark Taylor, and John Grose and Thomas Grose, father and son.</p>
        <p>There is no doubt that with the present machinery no bodies can be recovered for the next two months. Several of the bodies are in thirty fathoms of water, and others in fifty fathoms. Captain Newton, one of the mine agents, went to the West Wheal Owles shaft, in the hope that some of the men might have escaped in that direction, but it was soon seen that the poor fel- lows could not have got out at that pomt, and that all below were beyond reach of human aid. James Hall, who has been working in the Wheal Owles Mine for seventeen years, states :—We were down in the fifty-five fathom level, thirteen of us. and all went to their work. Presently a collapse occurred, and I heard a shout and cries for a light. I jumped in- to the level, but found that no one could carry a light, and I ran back in the dark towards the sound. When I got back there was one of the boys in the ladderway, and him I rescued and teok up into a safe place on the ladder road. I was then told that there was another man missing, and I went back and rescued him, bring- ing him from the fifty-five fathom level. I then struck a light, and tried to rescue the others, who were con- tinually crying out, " Bring us a light." Their cries were heartrending. They called again and again for light, but I could not help them, and that is the last I heard of them. I followed on until I got to the forty fathom level, and then the water was within twenty fathoms of myself.</p>
        <p>A later telegram says there seems to be no possibility of any of the entombed men being alive. The accident happened either at the sixty-five or seventy-five fathom level. It is the opinion of Captain Tregear, the senior agent and assistant manager, that one of the men must have blasted a hole, and struck the old workings, which were not shown on any of the plans of the mine in exis- tenets. The plans of the mine do not show any workings for many fathoms away from the levels mentioned, but the hole created must have tapped the whole of the old workings, which extend for a mile or more. As a conse- quence of the accident, there has been great subsidence of land in the locality. The whole mine is flooded to the thirty fathom level, and all the working portion of the mine is thus stopped. To add to the general disaster, the only engine available for pumping out the water has become choked,and pumping operations have altogether ceased ; but even if it were not choked, it would be quite useless to cope with the vast amount of water in the mine, and if the bodies are to be recovered, an ad- ditional engine will have to be erected. This will be the work of a considerable time—some weeks at least. Four of those drowned were new men, and went under- ground on Tuesday for the first time. One of them, named Devey, was never in a tin mine before.</p>
        <p>THE RECENT COLLIERY FIRE AT ST.</p>
        <p>HELENS. DELAY IN FINDING THE HYDRANTS.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the St. Helens Water Committee on Wednesday, Alderman lul.'Bryde pre- siding, some discussion took place as to the delay in getting to the hydrants at the Phoenix Colliery fire on the 22nd ult. _ .</p>
        <p>The report of Superintendent Birchall showed that the alarm was received at ten minutes to nine. The hose reel and steam engine immediately turned out, but on arrival the hydrant was found to be buried in three inches of slag, and a delay of ten minutes occurred before the water could be turned on. _</p>
        <p>Councillor Urmson, one of the owners of the colliery, said he had been told the delay was three-quarters of an hour. _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor F. R. Dixon-Nuttall (ex-Mayer) said he had it on good authority that the fire brigade got to the scene of the fire in good time from the Town Flail, but a delay of more than half an hour—he would not put it higher than that—occurred before the water was turned on. Then there was scarcely any pressure, and someone was sent to the Town Hall to put the pressure on. If they had got to the hydrant at once, and there had been good pressure, there would not have been more than £lO or £l5 worth of damage done. When the brigade arrived the headgear was not on fire, but the staging was. The firemen were running about like wild men, and were looking for the hydrant 120 yards from where it actually was. They had no more idea than the Man in the Moon where the hydrants was, and when they did find it, it was covered with slag and mud. His opinion was that a sub-committee ought to be appointed, and an inquiry instituted. He maintained that these hydrants should be marked in a prominent place. This particular hydrant was marked, but in a place where it could not be seen. . .</p>
        <p>Alderman Cook raoved that a sub-committee consisting of the ex-Mayor, Alderman J. C. Gamble, and Council- lors Burchall and Martin, be appointed to investigate and report to that committee. _ . _ . .</p>
        <p>The Engineer: It is a piece of ridiculous nonsense to say that a man was sent down to the Town Hall to put on the pressure. Alderman Cook pointed out that one of the accounts was for cab hire from Brownedge to the Town Hall and Glover's fire.</p>
        <p>The Engineer said that OR that day the Brownedge Reservoir was being cleaned out. Superintendent Birch. all was his foreman, and he had Ids assistance at that time. When he heard of the fire, he sent to Whitta- more's for a cab (the Corporation horse having been out all day) to bring Birchall down. They arrived at•the fire at a quarter-past ten, barely an hour after the alarm had been received, and there was sufficient pressure then to go over the headgear and the wheels of the pulley. Councillor Dixon-Nuttall : When the water was turned on, or a considerable time, extending over a number of minutes, the water would not squirt ten yards, until it was said, someone went and had the pressure turned on. I maintain there was something wrong. The Engineer: There was nothing wrong. The Sut- ton-road engine was pumping into the main direct to that spot. Councillor Dixon-Nuttall : Then what was the cause of the delay ? The Chairman: It should be properly inquired into. The Engineer: I may say that a very important hydrant was covered the other day with four inches of slag, and the steam roller passing over it. It was decided to appoint the gentlemen named as a sub-committee to inquire into the matter fully.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
