      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>PANAMA SCANDALS.</head>
        <p>THE PANAMA SCANDALS.</p>
        <p>STUPENDOUS BRIBERY. M. Fontane, in the course of his examination on Wednesday as a defendant in the Panama trials said that until 1885 he had charge of the company's relations with the Press, the exactions of which, however, had compelled him to give up those duties. Up to 1885 the sum he had distributed among the Press did not exceed 60,000 francs, in drafts payable to bearer. M. Eiffel, under strong pressure from the judge, confessed to hav- ing cashed a cheque for twelve million francs which was given to him for the purchase of machinery. As a matter of fact, however, he devoted only one and a half millions to that purpose. two million francs of the remainder being given to M. Hebrard for bribing officials in order to secure contracts, and two million francs were given to Baron Reinach, in order that he might financially influence the members of the Govern- ment. An engineering expert named Flory gave evi- dence that M. Eiffel paid sixty-nine million francs for certain contracts, and made by the transaction a per- sonal profit of no less than thirty-three million francs. M. Arton, a naturalised British subject, is reported to have declared that the recipient of the famous cheque for 500,000 f., which has been sought for so long, was a foreign savant, who occupied an important diplomatic post in Paris.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>REVISION OF _RELIEF.</head>
        <p>THE REVISION OF _RELIEF.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board, congratulating the Guardians on the steps they proposed to take so as to ensure more careful adminis- tration of out relief.</p>
        <p>A POOR LAW PROBLEM. The Clerk read some correspondence from the Local Government Board as to the case of two youths suffer- ing from typhoid fever, who had been admitted to the hospital belonging to the Runcorn Improvement Com- missioners, and enclosing a copy of a letter received from the Commissioners. The Commissioners claimed from the Guardians payment for the maintenance of the youths in the hospital as paupers, but the Guardians con- tended that they were not liable, as the youths were not paupers, having not been admitted on a medical order from a relieving officer. The Local Government Board in reply to the inquiry of the clerk to the guardians, now pointed out that if they were admitted on such an order they were paupers, and must ,be paid for by the Guardians, or if the lads had no means to pay for their own maintenanse, the Guardians could pay for it.—Mr. Taylor thought that if the youths had no means, the Guardians might as well pay the amount.—Mr. Brandreth dissented from this view. He mentioned that a case had been decided lately, which appliea to the present circumstances, and according to which, if a per- son suffering from an infectious disease,were confined by the sanitary authority of a district to prevent the spread of the disease, the Guardians were not liable for the maintenance.—The Chairman said this case only proved more strongly the necessity for providing a hospital I for the whole Union. FINANCE COmMITTEE.-RUNCORN AND FREE EDUCATION. I Mr. Brandreth moved the adoption of the minutes of the Finance Committee, which recommended payments of £2,930 35.4 d., including £2,308 16s. Td. to the County Council, and reported that the balance in Nand was £4,091 138. 2d.—Mr. Brandreth referred to an item of £2 14s. sd. among the payments, as a sum charged by the Runcorn School Board to the Guardians for the edu- cation of children. He expressed the opinion that in these days of free education the Guardians ought not to be paying this money.—Mr. laylor said he understood from the papers that parents could demand free educa- tion, and if that were the case the Guardians ought not 1 to be required to pay these fees; and he presumed that applied both to Board schools and denominational schools—The Fins nceCommittee's minutes were adopted, audit was resolved, on the motion of Mr. Brandreth, se- conded by Mr. Taylor, that the Clerk consult the Local Government Board and the Education Department on the matter discussed.—This was all the business of im- portance.</p>
