      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>A RAILWAY OFFICIAL'S DEFALCA- TIONS. -- POLICE PROCEEDINGS AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>PAINFUL CASE.</p>
        <p>Frederick Edward James, for eleven years chief parcel agent and booking clerk at the St. Helens Station of the London and North-Western Railway Company, surrendered to his bail at St. Helens Police Court on Wednesday. A week ago Mr.Fenna, for the company, stated that the whole Of Mr. James's deficiencies amounted to .£622 Os. 9d., but the warrant had been issued on one specific charge of embezzling £3O 10s. On Wednesday Mr. F. M. Preston, (instructed by Mr. Fenna) appeared to prosecute, and Mr. J. 0. Swift defended. The magistrates on the bench Were the Mayor (Mr. Arthur Sinclair) and Alderman Harrison. Bfr, Preston opened the proceedings by stating that the duties of the prisoner, who had been in the service of the company for eighteen years, were to receive all Moneys paid to the company at the St. Helens Passenger tsetion. He had control of all the moneys, and received , aniounts from the booking clerks, the parcels clerks,the excess fares, and from contracts. There was what was called a clerks' settlemeht book, in which the under Clerks entered their amounts, and when received by James he initialled them. In this book their ought to be entries of all'moneys received in various ways by the Prisoner. In addition to that he bad to make remit- f. to the district cashier at Liverpool, and that was " done by entering the amounts in gold, silver, and cheques on a transmit note, after which he put the cash . eed note into a bag, which was sent to Lime-st.' The amount of the note should also be: entered on the : counterfoil and initialled by James. At the end of the Month there wee sent up a sort of balance sheet, which g,eve the whole of the cash received during the month, e'en:use's operations seemed to have been as follow :- . 'seginning with July 13. when his accounts were audited by Mr. Scrymgeour, of Lime-street, nothing was !eund wrong, but the defalcations commenced almost Iretnediately after the audit. From the examination of • "Ile hooks it was found that during August he made under-remittances to the amount of £ll2 14s. 6d. He 'would enter on different days certain amounts in the transmit note which were less than the amount he had 4etually received during the day, but the counterfoil 'contained the correct amounts. He kept the counterfoils but lock and key, so that nobody could get at them, nut it was eventually found that he had under-trans- netted in that month £ll2 14s. 6d. At the end of the Ituonth it was his duty to send in a balance-sheet, and as ' ,here were other ways of checking the amounts if noth- ing further were done, his defalcations would be found out then. James went to a Mr. Nickson, a fish dealer, teul induced Nickson to give him a signed cheque in lank, and he filed in £l2O 10s 6d. so as to cover the eelliount of the defalcations. He then took current re- '.;ciPts from the station and gave them to Nickson Le pay Into the bank to meet the cheque. This tided him over "tnßust. In September the same thing went on. He Uteler-rernitted £9B 10s. 9d., and at the end of that kontla his defalcations were £2ll ss. 3d. He then oh- title ned another cheque from Nickson,and filled it in for izlol us. 2d. and a cheque from Mr. Norbury, fruiterer, "Ler £95 Is. ed. There were also contracts for £25 Bs. 3d. e"initing a total of £222 3s. lid. The same thing went tit in October, during which his defalcations amounted N,(,) 1-24 1.55. ed., making altogether £336 Os. 9d. That, e.as met in the same way as before, and another cheque Fres got from Nickson for £193 38. 2d., and a cheque eetn Norbury for £97 15s. 4d. He then apparently 'Aid back a Portion by inserting in the transmit note a 3r.eater amount of cash than he had received, and that emfferetice was £39 10s. and £5 13s. 6d., leaving defici- ilcies £336 Os. 9d. That took him to the beginning of eilernber, and in that month his defalcations were 18. 1.35. 641.-an increasing quantity-making £559 3(1. At the end of November he obtained a blank eueque from Nickson, dated 30th November, 1892, and e"ecl it in for £559 18s. sd. This cheque was dis- teiineured. He knew the amount was too largo for bins teerneet, and knowing the auditor might Caine at any 06:7 he was afraid of the consequences, and on the 2nd e 29 scernber he absconded, taking cash £3O 10s., and bolielves• That, together with £1 16s. on a railway ectee, , accounted for the whole of the defalcations- tiee`e Os. 6d. He absconded, but on the 13th he sent following letter to Mr. James Shaw, the super- tetendent b• eatbeat Sie-F'or the wrong I have committed whilst in the tree, 3' of the railway company I am deAply pained and true' f "rrY, and I feel it my duty to inform you fully the with t, of my trouble brought on by folly, and created foolish- desire to improve my income, having by rash and anseeauseeculation lost the whole of my own mmey, PriateaS to £4OO, and in a moment of weakness appro. Mg my 108 Moneys of the company, in the hope of retriev- Pate, ee "88, and replacing what I laud so wrongfully used. Into proved was ejltersd adverse to me, as every transaction I n proved a ilia set, one failure, and increased my hoe P. until finally! was compelled to admit the sourse I ihst.;B•Ken, as the money I expected to receive on the 2nd 0 meet it was not fotthconsing. The thought of betrayed the confidence you reposed in me, and after w•-nving so many kindnesses in a variety of ways, together „ 'ta the disgrace and distress it has caused my dear wife V' children, causes me deep and bitter pain, and distracts ta brain. Why I should have acted thus I cannot compre- jeS M. The exact tot il wif my deficit is £62.1 Os. 3d., and triBhall be glad if yen will inform my dear wife, whose ',!ress is 78, Chapel-street, St. Helens, that if the money to "nrlbllreed to the company, whether you would or could re• .go any proceedings that you may already have Instituted lekeinst Me. I have no wish to be dishonest, and would ithal? make reparation for my errors, if it can be arranged. With,'Y Write her early. If the proceedings cannot now be Tawn, I will return and undergo the punishment I de- ,, s. * relying upon you to consider my wrongs in as lenient se,, *RS you can, and trusting you will not be too unduly to, ere in this, the greatest trouble it is possible for anyone whiculaclergo,-I remain, dear sir, through circumstances have proved adverse to me in all my dealings. a Yo , u, unfortunate, and ruined man. Therefore I pray clea,^lll consider my position favourably, for the sake of my Wife and six young children.-Yours truly, FREDERICK IP. JAMES. 110 a lajit nswer was sent-to the letter, and prisoner gave ereeBelf up. The company now charged him with ezzling, on the 4th October. £8 Is. received from a Rep nileY in respect of a contract; on the Ist December 1)0 'vs, which was under-remitted; and on the 3rd Iv:M.l'o3er £29 1.65., also under-remitted. The money th: tint apparently taken under a sudden impulse, but eati practice had been carried on for months, the defal- els p i e°lls being very eat, and carried eut nan extra eel" Manner. lea'r• Matthias Bailey, butcher, St. Helens, said one of , Coescies Was studying as a medical student at University eeeeeee, Liverpool, and he came back each night by the eeteft: and North-Western Railway. On the 4th of tite'eer the prisoner came to his shop about the season and asked for the amount of the new ticket. sheen. lie had not sufficient cash, and gave prisoner a lee and made it payable to himself. pethr• Swift said that if the prisoner was to be charged he those three amounts, or any amounts up to £o2l. InseVan ready to plead guilty. Subsequently the ee f'cution had opened the case as the prisoner Ltuttea p • leest• reston said that under the circumstances the lea sa.tisfactory way would bo to prove the embezzle- borlilt. Charles Gerard, district cashier, Liverpool, corro- teaated the opening statement as to the manner in which A1e%4.2.11 Was transmitted. There was no cheque from isee7alley on the transmit note of the sth October, lithe should have a correct list of the money received 14,2 e 4th. He checked the cash received on the 2nd chse',Thee, which amounted to £6l 10s. ed., and the note"" were detailed at the bottom of the transmit tree- On the 3rd December he received the cash and lecniit note, the amount being £35 15s. 6d. axl'erbert Edwards, a clerk in Parr's Banking Company ' leeetile Alliance Bank at St. Helens, said the cheque easeuced was presented on the 4th October, and he ed it. Joh o,, '3l3ect crynsgeour, the railway company's audit in- teas er neseizi,cluded said for the last five years it had been his for the Liverpool district, in which St. Helens etse .0 audit" the prisoner's accounts. In July last the beeerts balanced. He had got two clerks' settlement terse • The amounts received by the clerks were en- teas- were different times during the day. The defalca- Ie were £622 03. 9d. !ePly to Mr. Swift, he said Ur. James had £30,090 kleccr IlkSsing through his hands, and his salary was The amount had largely increased during the to-rly Years Mr. James had been in charge. Witness 1/riat "illy had no reason to think Mr. James had appro- _ Joh any money. 11 Henry Nickson, fishmonger, said he signed blank hetiebnes at the request of the prisoner, who first said he 'Sc unexpectedly called upon to pay a deposit the i"8 shares. Witness had no money to lose. When the 'rst cheque became due James met his wife with trio The nnY, and they took it into the bank together. Jbamstrates intimated that they were prepared to ttevith the case summarily, and Nte:l B.,wlft, made an earnest appeal for a lenient sent- 4ble • The prisoner had already suffered very consider- -1:1 ts• own conscience, and be Witnessing the u()• re-I ''gß of his wife and family. Mr. James had to xl Ivas' n rret. 0. ney at large rates of interest, and when he ntibculties like a fool he attempted to make his ta,•,:on better by investing small sums of money on • Magistrates consulted, and then the Mayor said /.s4 • eePIY regretted that after such a career of many 8 edee nourable service and his Own personal know- 0f Mr. James a most gainful duty was placed up- Pose j. 4 Under all circumstances, they could only dis- Nileft, "e ease by passing the full sentence the law prili them to do, which was six months' hard labour. Vvaitoeute,irtoili:asthenremoved below, and afterwards to in a cab.</p>
        <p>-kOVAL OF 'UNIONIST OFFICIALS. toil% l'rancis Macnaghten, Lord Lieutenant of the othc,J; Mr. Victor Coates, High Sheriff; and three 431,,,,ni0n1ts have been removed from County Anrim Board, their places being filled by rive Nation-</p>
