      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>THE SOUTH-EASTERN RAILWAY DIVIDEND. '</p>
        <p>The South-Eastern Railway divided for the past half year will be at the rate 6i per cent, per annum on un- divided stock, 114 per cent, on preferred ordinary stock, and 2 per cent. on deferred ordinary stock, carrying for- ward the balance of £l,OOO.</p>
        <p>REPRESENTATION OF BURNLEY'.</p>
        <p>The Socialists of Burnley have decided net to bring out a candidate, but to support the Gladstonian candi- date, the Hon. Philip Stanhope, who has been in Burnley for the last two or three days. The Conservatives and Unionists have not ye; secured a candidate, and it is considered doubtful whether one will enter the field.</p>
        <p>THE COTTON STRIKE.</p>
        <p>REJECTION OF WORKMEN'S OFFER.</p>
        <p>At the conference, on Thursday, between the master cotton spinners and operatiVes the latter proposed that there should be short time, with a reduction of 5 per mat., and an adjournment was made in order to enable the masters to consider the offer. The masters refused the offer, and the strike will go on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>£2OO FOR A BROKEN LEG.</head>
        <p>£2OO FOR A BROKEN LEG.</p>
        <p>Mr. Miller, collector for the Midland Railway Com- pany, was awarded £2OO damages by the jury in the Queen's Bench, London, on Thursday, in action brought against a Mr. Randcock. owner of premises in the city. Miller slipped over a worn stair and broke his leg. Handcock denied the resPonsibility for the repair of the stairs, commonly used by all the tenants, and on this point the judge reserved his decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>DEATH OF A PRESTONIAN MILLIONAIRE.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A PRESTONIAN MILLIONAIRE.</p>
        <p>• On Thursday, a telegram was received in Preston from the Continent announcing the death of Mr. John Hunt- ington, a native of Preston, at the age of sixty years. Ths deceased went to the United States when a boy, and after years of steady industry and perseverance became a partner in the Standard Oil Company, of Cleveland, in Ohio, where he has many years resided, and had amassed an immense fortune. He visited Preston in 1878, and presented a font of Carrara marble to St. Paul's Church, as a memorial of his wife's parents, John and Nancy Beck. In Cleveland, he founded and endowed a hospital for decayed persons of both sexes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MR. H. GLADSTONE, M.P., ON AGIRICUL.</head>
        <p>MR. H. GLADSTONE, M.P., ON AGIRICUL.</p>
        <p>TIIRE.</p>
        <p>Speaking at Hawarden on Thursday night, Mr. Her- bert Gladstone, M.P., after stating that his father had returned from the continent in robust health, referred to Lord Winchilsea's panacea fc,r agricultural depression. He declined to believe in the decay of British agriculture, showed that the demand for agricultural produce was increasing, said that butchers and market gardeners were making good profits, and urged that the farmers should combine with the object of getting better prices.</p>
        <p>• Mr. J. Arch, M.P., says that the labourers have as much to hope for from Lord Winchilsea's National Union as a mouse would have of its life if it attempted to lick the cream off a cat's whiskers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>DISPORTING IN ICE AND WATER.</head>
        <p>DISPORTING IN ICE AND WATER.</p>
        <p>HEROIC BATHING.</p>
        <p>A representative of the " Pall Mall Gazette "went to Hyde Park early on Saturday morning in order to ascertain if there were individuals, even in London, who derived enjoyment from a dip in the Serpentine, with the thermometer showing 20 degrees of frost. Sure, enough, he writes, before eight o'clock no less than twelve men put in an appearance. One of them, armed with a good sized hatchet, broke up the ice, and all of them, getting into the water, proceeded to disport them selves as cheerfully as in a warm bath. There was no preliminary shivering on the bank, and each and all of them appeared to be thoroughly accustomed to and to ' enjoy the experience. " Have you indulged in this sort of thing during the whole of the present winter ?" he asked of one elderly gentleman, a member of the Ser- pentine Swimming Club. "Yes,' he replied, "and more than that, I don't think I've missed a dozen morn- ings during the last thirty-five years. It's getting out of bed that's difficult. The water's quite warm when you get into it. I never suffer from colds, or, in fact, anything else." s• None of us ever catch cold," chimed in another of the bathers. " I know if I ever get the snuffles it's from someone else." Asked if he enjoyed throwing the ice about, diving, and standing up to the neck in such cold water, another said : " Of course we do or we wouldn't do it ; but," he added, candidly, "I don't suppose there would be so many come if there was no physical advantage to be gained." The largest atten- dnace is,on Sunday mornings, when as many as thirty youths and men enjoy a morning dip. Of course during the winter months the number greatly diminishes, and it's only the enthusiasts who are at present making use of the twenty square feet of ice-covered water.</p>
