      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>THE CHITRCH AND THE LONDON</p>
        <p>UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>A SAGE DELIVERANCE FROM CANTERBURY'S ARCHBISHOP. The " Times" says that the Archbishop of Canterbury has replied to one of many correspondents in the follow- ing letter:—</p>
        <p>Lambeth, Jan. 8.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—Our Christian philanthrophy seems to me ready this momeLt for either an elastic efficiency or for another large mistake. You and some others charitably reproach me with not being as other men, cardinals. or generals, and the Church with not advertising untried schemes of relief. Yet the unemployed are not a wholly new phenomenon. The genuine unemployed deserve and have our genuine, active sympathy. The class is constantly being helped in sound ways. The difference now is that, through obvious exceptional depressions, the numbers are increasing in an unusual ratio. But I there is no new principle involved. It has happened occasionally in former years that any special develop- ment of want has been met mainly by the process called simple subtraction. New funds have been formed and inflated, and immediately afterwards it has appeared that all the regular associations and funds have suffered loss. There has been little beyond transference and that even of hospital subscriptions, to the philanthropy of the hour. The funds so raised have left small trace. One such great fund is persistently criticised as having done more harm than good. But what does at present leave traces of solid, con- structive power, is the even, steady, ever-increasing, substantial work which is carried on by existing or- ganisations. The best thinkers and students regard the parochial unit of the clergy and their co-workers and committees, if properly used by benevolent people wf any persuasion, as the one effective safeguard against fraud and "overlapping.' The great Metropolitan So- ciety for the Belief of Distress acts through the parish. Mr. Arnold White proposes to act through clergy— rather more motley in the enumeration than is quite practical. An eminent man lately entered the Church's ranks because on the great committees he found the East-end parochial clergy, and them alone, thoroughly acquainted with the cases and characters of their people. No one who knows the neighbourhood of any active parochial mission or church questions the enor- mous remedial and uplifting power of, that organisation. Its work is sound, substantial, lasting, and alsa strong</p>
        <p>to carry new work. To those, then, who are kind enough to give me ad- vice, or to ask for it, my answer, out of past and pre- sent, is—strengthen the existing means of relief, assist- ance, and rehabilitation. Multiply their power of dealing in detail with poverty and want of employment. Work persona 4 where work is wanted in investigation, committee, and correspondence. Make the communica- tion of such bodies with each other real, where the occa- sion arises, rather than artificially complete. It is in individual effort strengthening the existing organisations, whether limited or general, which have long dealt with this subject, and in extending their operation in propor- tion to the need which comes before them, that there will be found least waste and most power.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>ED. CANTUAR.</p>
