      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>HOPE HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE HOPE HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Having had the misfortune to be a patient in the Hope hospital for seven or eight weeks permit me through the columns of your journal, to return my sin- cere thanks to the director and the nurses, for their kindness, and the care and attention they paid to me as well as to the rest of the patients suffering from so loathsome a disease. There have been rumours outside about the indifferent care and attention paid to the patients, but such talk is unfounded and wrong. I have also to thank the Rector' the Rev. Mr. Willis, for his regular visits to us and for the kind and sympathetic feeling he displayed.—Yours truly, WM. FRANCE. . 24, Watkin-st.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ROMANISTS IN POWER.</head>
        <p>ROMANISTS IN POWER.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Sooner or later we are bound to have a conflict on the schools question respecting religious teaching. I myself, am strongly in favour of secular instruction, and would confine all religious teaching to the Sunday school. But all are not of this opinion, and in order to show them how sectarianism works I enclose you a cut- ting from an American paper which I lately came across. Trusting you will be able to find room for the same.—l am, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>A PROTESTANT.</p>
        <p>"Ls Citizen Franco-Americaine " says—There are thousands of people in the East who will be loth to believe the high-handed manner in which the represen- tatives of Rome conduct themselves where they get the opportunity; but the case which has been decided in the Circuit Court here, and which has been appealed to the Supreme Court, will give them authentic information and open their eyes. This town has a very large Roman Catholic population. It is not a large place, having a population of about 1,600, and it is located on the C. and N. W. railway, in Juneau county, about 70 miles west of Madison.</p>
        <p>They have one public school, with four rooms, in charge of four teachers. There are three school commis- sioners, elected by the voters; and last fall two of those elected were Roman Catholics and one a Protestant. They met and elected one of the Romanists president, and the other secretary. At the regular meeting the two Romanists would not attend, so there would be no quorum ' • then they would call a special meeting and not notify the Protestant. They appointed three Roman Catholic teachers, and retained one lady who had loag been teaching there and was a Protestant, though there was a clamor among the Romanists of the town for her removal also ; but they seenled satisfied with 75 per cent. of the teachers. .</p>
        <p>-- Then came the priest of the Roman Catholic Church and ordered the Bible out of the schools ; this was done. Then he ordered that the Roman Catholic catechism should be taught in the schools; the Protestant teacher objected, but she had to submit or resign. Then came the crowning outrage of all. One morning the priest came into the schools and ordered the three Roman Catholic teachers to take all their scholars over to the church at once. He then went into the other room and notified the lady to take all her pupils over to his church, as some in- teresting proceedings were to take place; she protested, but he marched every one of the scholars off, and taking every child over to his church, he publicly " conse- crated" or baptized them into the Roman Catholic faith, Protestants and all, and many of the little folks didn't know what was being done to them. The last indignity was too much for some of the Pro- testant parents, and with the Protestant School Commis- sioner they applied to thc Circuit Court for an injunc- tion to restrain the priests from further interfering with the public school. The court has granted the injunction, and the priest and his backers have appealed the case to the Supreme Court. This is the Supreme Court that last year decided that the holy Bible was a sectarian book, and must not be read in the public schools. The Supreme Court decides such appeals on the briefs submitted, and the Roman Catholics have plenty of money to retain the best attorneys to fight their case, while the Americans have no such powerful organiza- tion.</p>
        <p>la case the Supreme Court decides in favor of the priest, what will he do next? And what will every priest in Wisconsin do, if the people submit ? This is in America, in 1892. The record of the Jackson county, Wis., courts wifl certify particulars as given above. '</p>
        <p>BUDDHISM AND CHRISTIANITY.-SOME POINTS OF RESEMBLANCE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of Saturday last there was an interesting account of the monthly meeting of the Wes- leyan local preachers at which a paper was read by Mr. W. H. Butler, on " Buddhism and Christianity : some points of contrast." Believing the subject to be one likely to interest your readers, I have ventured to give a short sketch showing the remarkable similarity in many respects that exists between this very ancient religion—Buddhis m—and Christianity. The study of the ancient religions of the world is very interesting, and is calculated to dissipate a vast amount of prejudice from the minds of ignorant but well-mean- ing Christian people towards those whom it has been the fashion to stigmatize as heathens and barbarians. It will be found that purity, justice, mercy, humility, and the highest morality are inculcated by these ancient religions, and are not the exclusive monopoly of Christ- ianity. As Emerson says—" What divines had assumed . as the distinctive revelations of Christianity, theologic criticism has matched, by exact parallelisms from the Stoics and Poets of Greece and Rome. Later, when Confucius and the Indian Scriptures were made known, no claim to monopoly of ethical wisdom could be thought of : and the surprising results of the new researches into the history of Egypt have opened to us the deep debt of the Church of Rome and England to the Egyptian hies- ology." Perhaps the most remarkable points of similarity be- tween Buddhism and Christianity are those relating ta the birth and life of Christina, a Buddhist god, and of Christ, the founder of Christianity. Chrishna was be- lieved by the ancient Ffindoos to have been born of a holy virgin ; at his birth angels came down from hea- ven, singing hymns in his praise. The story of the Massacre of the Innocents is related of Chrishna, and the escape by flight, owing to a miraculous warning, and various other particulars, are given which agree al- most word for word with the account given by St. Mathew of Christ. So much alike, indeed, are the two stories, that the learned men amongst the Hiiadoos and the Chinese retort, as a certain Mandarin did to M. Le Huc, the eminent French missionary—" You are a man of prayer. Why do you steal our Boudha,and offer him back to us as your Christ ?" Another feature is the likeness between certain rites of Buddhism and Chris- tianity. Professor Clodd says—" When the Roman Catholic missionaries first met the Buddhist monks, they I were shocked when they saw that their heads were shaven, that they knelt before images, that they worshipped relics, wore strings of beads, used bells and holy water, and had confession of sin. They believed that the devil as the father of all mischief and deceit, had tempted these men to dress themselves in the clothes of Catholics and mock their solemn practices ; whereas it seems likely that if there had been any connection in the past, the younger religion had borrowed from the older.* " Monasteries for men, and nunneries for women still exist; and vast numbers of monks are found; while the huge and now deserted monasteries and temples cut in the solid rock, and of which hundreds exist in India, show how mightily a system which had been thought to belong to Christianity only, formed part of Buddhism two thousand years ago." Buddha, like Christ, taught the law of mercy and charity and loving sacrifice for one's fellow creatures, and the common people were said to have heard him gladly. William Von Humboldt has copied the following ten precepts from the sacred books of the Buddhists: 1. Thou shalt not kill even the smallest creature. 2. Thou shalt not appropriate to thyself what belongs to another. 3. Thou shalt not infringe the laws ef chastity. 4. Thou shalt not lie. 5. Thou shalt not calumniate. 6. Thou shalt not speak of injuries. 7. Thou shalt not excite quarrels by repeating the words of others. 8. Thou shalt ivit hate. 9. Preserve faith in the holy writings. 10. Believe in immortality. I have said enough to show that.Buddhism,so far from deserving the contempt of Christians, merits their warmest regards, for it is in many particulars remark- ably like their own religion—thoup .many.centuries older. And this explains why Christian. missionaries meet with so little success among the teeming millions of India and China.—Yours truly, HARRY ESMOND.</p>
      </div>
