      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PINCLI OF POVERTY AT</head>
        <p>THE PINCLI OF POVERTY AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>HOW THE POOR ARE HELPED. __- A SMALL-PDX PATIENT'S SORRY TALE.</p>
        <p>Although coming from an authentic source, the assurance that the poor are always with us is not at present a comforting one in Warrington. And many people fail to grasp the necessity for per- petuating the pauper. The frost of the past few weeks has had the effect of enlightening many people as to the real extent of povery in Warring- ing, for anyone with a discerning eye can see that the hundreds of people who have been receiving public relief at the soup kitchens, &amp;c., are not alone those who are suffering from a temporary pinch of poverty. The majority of them are the permanent poor of the town, and there are few people who are aware of their serions numbers. Undoubtedly the frost has intensified the hardness of their lot, aud augmeated their numbers con- siderably. But the augmentation has not been beyond 500 or 600 unemployed, and the total num- ber is estimated at 1,500. Therefore it would seem that in our midst we have an average army of 1,000 persons out of employment constantly.</p>
        <p>The announcement that relief would be provided for the most deserving of these cases, by means of public subscriptions, has during the week filled our main streets with pictures of cruellest poverty —pictures which would have otherwise been hid- den from us in the darkness of the hovels and slums ; in the unhealthy dens, the condemned areas, where most of these poor" deserving cases" suffer their hunger and live in their filth perenni- ally.. The work done by the Mayor and Mayoress, in organizing the relief committees is a commend- able piece of practical sympathy. Once having set themselves to the task, our chief magistrate and his wife soon procured funds, and those to collect them.</p>
        <p>They had been moved to take these steps in con- sequence of the many accounts of extreme want, reported by all manner of responsible people in town, especially the clergy. During the whole of last week Alderman Wright, Alderman Sutton, Councillor Smethurst, Mr. Peter Chrimes, and Mr. G. Pilling generously devoted their energies and time to the collecting of funds, and by Friday last week they were able to decide to issue tickets for coal and food to the most need", people, and to open a soup kitchen each day during the present week.</p>
        <p>This soon became known to those people in want, and early on Saturday morning crowds of poor ill- clad women surrounded the relieving depots which consisted of school inspector Riley's office in the park, the Rectory, and other ministers' houses. It was a distressing sight to see them waiting at the doors of these places ; waiting patiently in the cold, clothed in their ragged garments, for as much as an hour. Then, we ere told "many of them were turned away because of their antecedents being of such a character as to preclude them from benefit I" Perhaps the reader will wonder what antecedents. They were not refused because they had not starving bedraggled urchins at their heels ; not because they were not hungry and cold themselves, or because their faces were not pinched by the cruel fingers of want. No ! It was because they were unfortun- ate in their morals, because a great deal of their poverty could be clearly traced to criminality, to drunkenness, and in some cases to laziness. So they were not helped because there was only suffi- cient to assist those who were at least not directly responsible for their own poverty. More than a thousand tickets, each representing a hundred- weight of coal, were distributiad.</p>
        <p>At the soup kitchen, held at the Drill Hall, there has been an p„versge of 700 children fed each day. The dispensation of this food commenced on Mon- day at noon. But for a very long time before those doors at the Drill Hall were opened, an army of poor little urchins were surging before them. There was some grim humour in the excited howl which they set up when the doors were at length opened ; and when two stalwart men walked solemnlythrough their midst carrying a huge earthenware jar full of savoury-smelling soup, it was an excusable piece of industry when some of them tried slily to dive their pannikins into the jar. By-and-by Mr. Whittle, the Town Clerk, and other gentlemen came to them, and forming companies of about 80 at a time, they marched them into the Hall, where they sat on benches at long tables. Soon the ladies and gentle- men who were there to serve out the hot-pot and I soup had placed steaming bowls befo7e them. It was then that the sight was interesting. For in looking at them, was not one observing how a great portion of future Warrington could eat ?</p>
        <p>They were an eager-eyed gathering, all very young; some with meek, white faces, who ate their food silently and quietly, and looked like chil- dren who would grow up to be" steady men." There were those with boisterous voices and healthy- looking faces, who ate their food and then licked their pannikins, a' section who would maybe grow up rougher-dispositioned men. Indeed there were all kinds, probably a few future Town Councillors, for all they or anyone else knew.</p>
        <p>The total number of children fed during the week would be about 4,000. What they consumed would amount to about 1,4641b5. potatoes, 3601b5. of beef, 1801bs. peas, 12 cwts. of bread,72 ham shanks, and 96 gallons of soup. This giTes one ground to suppose that a few " square meals" were adminis- tered. The cost of these and other disbursements which were made, amounted, we believe, to about £6OO.</p>
        <p>Those who attended to the wants of the children at the soup kitchen were the Rev. Fathers Craven and Feeney,the Rev.E. C. E.Carleton, and Rev.ll. Y. Pigot. Mr. Lyon Whittle, Town Clerk, Alderman Wright, Councillor Smethurst,Mr. S. Wilson (deputy Town Clerk), Mr. G. Riley, Mr. C. Lyon, Mr. Parker, Mr. Ashton, Mr. Jones, Mr. Chrimes, Mr. Spinks, Mr. Flood, and the Mayoress (Mrs. E. Greenall), the Misses Fleming, and other ladies. Almost the whole of the food was made by Mr. and Mrs. Powiss,Town Hall attendants.</p>
        <p>The distress extended greatly to the Ship Canal workpeople, many of whom have been unable to work for many weeks. Relief was granted in this direction by Mr. J. Jackson, contractor,who opened a soup kitchen at Latchford, one at Stockton Heath, and another at Acton Grange. To those who were destitute of shelter—and there was a great number 7-tickets for beds at model lodging-hGuses were given.</p>
        <p>Those who have suffered most in the town how- ever, are, as usual, people who have been unexpect- edly reduced to low circumstances, and who are un- used to receiving charity, and consequently back- ward at asking for it. The writer came across an especially distressing case of this kind. He was a plasterer living off Pennel-street, and has recently suffered much misfortune. He is forty years of age, and has one child. Three months ago his wife died. A week or two after that he was stricken down with smallpox, and taken from his child to the hospital. He had been in lodgings, and when he was released from the hospital, weak and unable to work, he hurried to his lodging and found his child had been turned out. He searched for him and found the child in the precincts of the market begging. He took charge of him, but could pro- cure neither shelter nor food because he had no money. He tried to get work, but couldn't. For eight days he and his child have slept in outhouses, and have procured the meagrest food from what the father earned by selling evening papers. When. the writer saw the sad couple, the child was crying with cold ; the father was ragged and pale ; it was ten o'clock at night, and three parts of the man's papers were still on his hands. They had not broken fast that day —it was Tuesday. With a little assistance that was given to him the poor fellow said he would take a bed at Bruchemite's lodging-house, where a generous amount of consideration is given to the distressed by the proprietor. The man's antecedents are good; from what is known of him by the writer he has always been a temperate steady man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>RELIEF OF SHIP CANAL MEN AT LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>RELIEF OF SHIP CANAL MEN AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>For the alleviation of the distress among the unemployed ship canal labourers, the Rev. F. Slater vicar of St. James's, Latchford, and Mr. James Edge, manager of the Old-road, lodging house, at Latchford, last week collected a number of subscriptions from the public, and Mr. Slater himself generously became the largest subscriber. As the result, 40 of the men were provided in the lodging-house with supper there on Sunday night, besides having other provision made for them during the latter day. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 14, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 14, 1893.</p>
        <p>inmates of the lodging-house who are mostly, if not entirely, ship canal labourers and include only about two 14, arrington men,have been provided with food throughout the week out of the subscriptions collected last week, with some other amounts since I subscribed, and, we may add, through the supple- mentary kindness of Mr. Cobley, of Runcorn, pro- prietor of the lodging-house, himself, and Mr. Edge, who paid for two suppers for the men out of his own pocket. Mr. Sampson, of the Norton Arms, provided breakfast for 33 men at the lodging-house on Wednesday morning. Mr. Cobley has generously instructed his manager to provide 160 free beds for the men to be at their disposal until the weather becomes more favourable for their going away in search of employment.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN UNION RURAL SANITARY</p>
        <p>AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Authority was held at the Dutton Union, on Tuesday. There were pre- sent Messrs. Heywood (in the chair), the Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, and Messrs. J. Brandreth, J. M. Wil- liams, T. Lewis, J. Stokes, E. Aaron, T. Baxter and J. Baguley ; Ashton (clerk), G. Ashton (deputy clerk), and Farrington (nuisance inspector.)</p>
        <p>THE FATAL EXPLOSION AT RIXTON.</p>
        <p>NOTLS ON GAMES AD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>RELSLY SEWERAGE.</head>
        <p>RELSLY SEWERAGE.</p>
        <p>A letter from the Local Government Board dated, December 28th, stated that they were advised that a proper system of sewage disposal was urgently re- quired for Helsby, and that an economical and efficient scheme might be arranged on the following lines. An intercepting sewer, constructed of glazed earthenware pipes, should be laid along the south bank of the railway or parallel to it, by which all the sewage outlets now discharging upon that bank might be taken up and diverted to a given point. All the open ditches in the town where used as sewers should be replaced by earthen- ware pipes, proper flushing chambers being constructed at suitable points. An adequate area of suitable land should be provided for the treatment of the sewage, a measure which a long course of experience had con- vinced the Board was absolutely necessary. The Board further suggested the desirability of employing a com- petent engineer for the purpose of preparing the neces- sary plans and estimate for the scheme, and of the [ Sanitary Authority preparing a detailed plan of the I whole town indicating the position of the houses before the scheme was considered.—Mr. Brandreth moved that the letter be referred to the Helsby Committee. Be remarked that the question was one that meant an ex- penditure of £4,000 to £5,000, and he was afraid that it would be years before the scheme would be carried out. —The motion was seconded by Mr. Aaron and adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>VEHICULAR BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE VEHICULAR BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Clerk to the Cheshire County Council, acknowledging receipt of a copy of the resolution adopted by the Authority as to the proposed vehicular bridge across the Mersey, and promising at- tention to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ANTI-CHOLERA PRECAUTIONS.</head>
        <p>ANTI-CHOLERA PRECAUTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a notice of the inquiry to be held at Liverpool, on the 18th inst., with reference to the pre- cautions to be adopted in the port of Liverpool.—lt was resolved on the motion of Mr. Aaron, seconded by Mr. Stokes, that a deputation consisting of Messrs. Williams and Heywood and the Clerk attend the enquiry, and that the Clerk be authorised to pay out of pocket expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>SMALLPDX AND FEVER.</head>
        <p>SMALLPDX AND FEVER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Aaron mentioned that he was not present at the last meeting but while at Birmingham on the following Thursday, he saw in the "Birmingham Post " a state- ment that there were three cases of smallpox in the township of Weston. Who reported this to the Authority ? —The Clerk said it had been mentioned at the meeting that there was an alleged outbreak of smallpox at Weston.—Mr. Aaron made a statement from which it appeared that he had been called upon by someone who informed him that there were such cases at Weston, but subsequently they discovered this to be incorrect. He asked why should such reports get into the papers, and said if the reporters came and reported things in that way he should propose that they stop away. (Laughter.) —Mr. Stokes said he had seen the report in the " Liver- pool Mercury " before last meeting, and at that meeting proposed that the Inspector of Nuisances should inquire as to it, and he did so and found there was no truth in the report.—The Chairman said he did not believe the , report had emanated from that room; and others than reporters might be responsible for the misreport.—The Clerk stated that Mr. Riley had reported to him that there was a case of smallpox on Frodsham Marsh in the ship canal huts. They telegraphed to Mr. Farring- ton, and Dr. Adams to get the case into the Warrington Hospital. He tried his best to do so, but could not manage it. He (the clerk) and Dr. Adams then went to Runcorn to endeavour to get the case into the hospital there. That morning he received a letter from the Com- missioners declining to receive the case.—Dr. Adams reported as follows :—An outbreak of: four cases of scarlet fever, now convalescent, had occurred at Seven Oaks, two at Kingswood, and one at Frodsham. Infection appeared to have been derived from a case in Main-st., Frodsham, reported some time ago. This case gave rise to the Kingswood cases. A case of diptheria had occurred at Five Crosses, and three cases of typhoid . _</p>
        <p>fever at Kingsley, Helsby and Appleton. The Medical I Officer regretted to have to inform the Authority that smallpox had broken out in the Ship Canal huts on Frodsham Marsh. A " tramping navvy" obtained temporary lodging at No. 9 hut, and there developed smallpox. He had attempted to effect his removal to Runcorn or Warrington Hospital, but hitherto without success. He had suggested to the Ship Canal authorities the immediate re-vaccination of every man, woman, and child in these huts, but he greatly feared an extension of the disease. The Medical Officer reported a case of smallpox, that of a woman' at one of the huts at Acton Grange. She was delivered of a child early in the week, so that her removal evenif permission were obtained from Warrington would be a matter for the discretion of her medical advisers.—Mr. Aaron sug- gested that the Ship Canal Company might give the Authority one of their old huts or POl/10 timber to put up a place that might be used as a temporary hospital, and if they refused the Authority might inspect a stone building at Weston which had been mentioned by the Clerk.—Mr. Lewis said that steps should be taken immediately to establish a hospital for infectious dis- eases. He moved that the Infectious Diseases Hospital Committee see the Ship Canal Company as to carry- ing out Mr. Aaron's suggestion.—Mr. Aaron seconded, and the motion was carried.—A letter was read trom the Rev. H. R. Blogg, Frodsham Vicarage, drawing atten- tion to the case of smallpox desiring in the interests of his parishioners the very serious attention of the Autho- rity to the matter. The Clerk was instructed to reply that the matter was being attended to.—Dr. Adams expressed the opin- ion that the best protection against the spread of the disease was re-vaccination, and he had written to Mr. Jackson, agent for the Ship Canal Co., as to the desira- bility of re-vaccination among the inhabitants of the huts.—The Medical Officer was desired to use his endeavours to encourage this.—There was no other business of imp'ortance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>NOTABLE CENTENARIAN.</head>
        <p>A NOTABLE CENTENARIAN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon a number of gentlemen assem- bled at the Marlborough Rooms, Regent-st., to congra- tulate Dr. Severin Wielobycki on having attained the age of 100 years. There were present two other medical abstainers whose ages, combined with that of Dr. Wielobycl:i, reached the aggregate of 278 years. Dr. Norman Kerr, who was in the chair, stated that Dr. Wielobycki was the ion of a judge and born in the Polish province of Volinia, on the judge, of January, 1793. He fought for the independence of his Fatherland in I thirty-six battles. He graduated in 1841 at Edinburgh I University, and practiced first in Nova Scotia for ten years, finally returning twenty-seven years ago to Lon- don. Prior to an attack two years ago of influenza he was an energetic man, walking twelve miles a day. He had always been an ardent supporter of temperance and prohibition, as well as legislative provision for the seclu- sion and treatment of inebriates. He had never used tobacco,and had been an abstainer from all intoxicating drinks for over sixty years. He was a vegetarian for over seventeen years. but since his recent illnesses had taken fish and gravy of meat. He rises regularly at six o'clock. His sight is excellent, needing no assistance from spectacles, and his voice is strong. His only in- firmity is extreme deafness, which he attributes to the exposure he underwent when fighting for Polish indepen- dence in 1830.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>PROPOSED PROPOSED SMALLPDX HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED PROPOSED SMALLPDX HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>AN IMPORTANT SUGGESTION. Dr. Sargeant, Medical Officer of Health for Lan- cashire, had a conference with the Health Commit- tee of Warrington Town Council, on Friday, res- pecting the erection of a permanent Smallpox Hospital for the town. The Committee expressed the view that the County Council ought to provide permanent hospitals in central positions throughout the county so that the burden often noW thrown upon one borough where a hospital is erected, should be equally distri- buted, as the Rural Sanitary Authorities seldom provide hospitals in their areas. Dr. Sargent, it is understood, approved of the idea, and it was after- wards suggested that if the County Council accepted this proposition, they should be advised that a suit- abld district for a hospital in this neighbourhood would be to serve Warrington, St. Helens, Earles- town, Newton, and intermediate townships.</p>
        <p>EMIORATIOW.—Intending eraigran Bto New York, Boston Philadelphia, San Francisco, Canada, China, Japan, Aus- tralia, or New Zealand, should take ti.kets (lowest rates) at the " Examiner " Office, by Onion, American, Australian, Orients]. andf,c,ther lines.—John Walker, agent,"Bsarniner' office, Beweey-st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>SPECTATOR'S DESCRIPTION, -- – THE INQUEST ADJOURNED.</head>
        <p>A SPECTATOR'S DESCRIPTION, -- - THE INQUEST ADJOURNED.</p>
        <p>Another addition to the manyfatalities which have occurred in connection with the Ship Canal work at Rixton, took place on Monday. This was caused by the blowing up of a portion of a fiat, by tonite, a kind of dynamite. The accident happened in the morning, and resulted in the death of one man named e'Robert Green, known as " Lincoln Jack,"! and the mutilation of three other men named Hendicott, the foreman, Palmer,and Bentley. They had been engaged in blasting the bed of the river, at a spot through which the canal passes. Shortly after the acci- dent occurred, the injured men were conveyed to the Latchford hospital, as was also the deceased man. Coroner Yates was communicated with,and he arranged to hold the inquest on "Lincoln Jack" on Wednesday afternoon, at the Latchford hospital. A great crowd ot ship canal men met near the hospital and waited to hear what further developments the inquest would add to the tragedy. The Coroner and the jury were delayed some consid- erable time, and it was understood that they would not hear any evidence, except of identification, at that sitt- ing. During the pause our reporter had a conversation with a navvy who had seen the accident occur, and who is likely to give evidence at the adjourned inquest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>ME AIVY'S NARRATITS.</head>
        <p>ME AIVY'S NARRATITS.</p>
        <p>" Yes," said the canal man, "I have seen some terrible accidents in my time. But I never knew one that made me feel so melancholy as this. Maybe it was because " Lincoln Jack " or Robert Green was a friend of mine and I had spoken to him but a few minutes before great death snatched him away, right before my eyes. He was a gocd fellow was " Lincoln "and it was a pity he " cleared " after only working at that job for a week."</p>
        <p>"But how did it happen." "Well it was this way. Lincoln Jack, Hendicott, the foreman, Palmer, and Bentley were picked for doing the blasting for deepening the river bed, just by Rixton. The canal is to pass through that part of the river and the job had been going on for a few days. You see the river is about 10 feet or more deep just where they were working. The bottom is of solid rock, and a mighty tough jon it is. The blasters got into the centre of the river in a pontoon, and fixing long tubes to the bed, they run down a long hand drill, and bore a hole in the rocky bottom. When one hole is done, they do others and when enough are done they slip a charge of tonite down the tubes, which stand just above the water. Then they " couple" a fuse and levant for the shore as hard as they can get the old pontoon to go. There were about ten of us on the banks on Monday morning. Presently Hendicott gave the tip, and we hauled the pontoon along a few yards towards the bank, when six of the charges slipped off, sending the water into the air ,in spouts. But there was another one, 'cause seven had been charged. But the pontoon had got hard by the bank now, and the fellows began to wonder where that seventh tube was. They peered around in the water but couldn't see nothing. Then somebody yells "Mates, make tracks—there's danger." They all four looked scared, and began to hedge for the bank quick. But it weren't no go ! For just then, while I was leani*g again a jetty post, and I see that 'ere pon- toon just jump clear up on the top of a kind of water spout. The tonite had exploded beneath it. One end of the craft smashed clear off, and lumps of the pon- toon flew around like brick-bats. Water splashed around right up to where I stood. It startled me ; I said "My God, they're killed," and I looked and seed poor Lincoln shoot into the air, and fall with a thud on the sinking craft. His mates were knocked off their feet like ninepins, and there they lay stunned. The smashed craft was sinkirg,and several of my mates and , myself swarmed down ladders from the bank to the pontoon and brought poor Lincoln and his pals to the top. My poor mate lay like a log ; and I knew when I seed his face as my poor pard was a gonner. He lay crunched up like a broken heap with his ribs all smashed ; and he never spoke or looted—his face was white like chalk. We waited till two engines came along, then we placed two of the poor fellows on each. That's about all I know—except that I heard that poor old " Lincoln " died on the way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>AT THE INQUEST.</head>
        <p>AT THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>The jurymen now quickly viewed the body, which ' was lying in a wooden mortuary near the gate. They then proceeded to the ante-room where the coroner was awaiting theme Mr. Griffiths, the agent for the section of the Canal, represented the Ship Canal Company. The Coroner explained that the accident had occurred by the explosion of a tino charge beneath the pontoon or flat, on which the deceased and three others were working. The three other men were then lying in the hospital suffering from serious injuries which they had sustained. As the affair came under the Explosive Acts, he found it necessary to communicate with the some Secretary, who would no doubt send down a Govern- ment Inspector. This would compel him to adjourn the inquiry for a week, so that he could communicate with the Home Secretary, and so that the man who laid the charges of tonite would be able to give evidence. He would merely call the wife of the deceased to give evi- dence of identification.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Elizabeth Green, of Hathersite, Derbyshire, said the deceased W3S her husband. He was 38 years of age, and had been working on the ship canal at Rixton as a carter for some time. He had been living in Cadishead, and she thought he had not had much experience in blasting. .</p>
        <p>This-was all the evidence that was given and the inquest was adjourned until the 19th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>YOUNG MEN'S CHRISTIAN ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>YOUNG MEN'S CHRISTIAN ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>THE PAST YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>On Monday night the annual meeting of the Warrington Y.M.C.A.,w as held in the Lecture Room. 'Phe chair was occupied by Mr. C. Joyce. The Com- mittee presented their annual report,in which they stated that good and lasting work had been done by many who had shown great interest in the work of the association. Their membership roll had in- creased in a very satisfactory manner. Since the eommencement of the winter season 50 new mem- bers and associates who had joined the branch of the Committee, had every reason to believe the step they took in going to their new premises at Friar's Green House-was a beneficial one, as the rooms were well adapted for the present work, with the exception of the largest room which was sadly too small for concerts and other meetings. The Committee had during the year also furnished several rooms, chiefly bedrooms, and they found they had been able to render valuable assistance to young men coming from other towns ss strangers, by being in a position to accommodate them on the premises. In the report were several instances of the valuable benefits conferred in this manner. The Committee were pleased to be able to state that they had been able to make good terms with the Warringtcn Gymnasium, some twenty-five members having taken advantage of the privilogeoffered. They also expected to place before the members a similar advantage respecting the swim- ming baths, by the kindness of the Warrington Baths Committee. Throughout the year,continued the report, the members had shown great interest in the classes formed for their special benefit. It made allusion to the Bible class conducted by Mr. F. R. Roberts, M.A., a Greek class conducted by the same gentleman ; and the S. John's Ambulance class established by Dr. Starkey Smith had been very successful, both in the examination of the members and the work they had been able to do. The attendances at Mr. Brown's shorthand class showed an increase; the Tonic Solfa Choir conducted by the Rev. E. Moore, was so far advanced that they expected presently to have the pleasure of turning their efforts to practical results. Mr. W. H. Butler had successfully conducted a Christian evidence class, and they regretted that they were soon to loose him byhis departure to America. About £6 had been spent upon the new tennis class. The Committee also drew attention to the increase in the number of minor physical and social recreative clubs and classes. They had just completed their arrangements for the P.S.A. classes. They trusted that the work would be successful. The treasurer submitted his report, which showed that the balance from last year amounted to 19s. The receipts for , the past year had been £228. After the expenses of the Association had been met a balance of ZI Os 94d. re- mained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>BLICTION OF OFFICHRS.</head>
        <p>BLICTION OF OFFICHRS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. C. Parr was re-elected president. The follow mg vice-presidents were also elected :—Revs. E. C. E. Carleton, J. Kirtlan, E. Moore, J. Warnock, T. P. Wil- liamson, J. Yonge, E. T. Reed, D. W. Roberts ; Messrs. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., Z. Armitage, J. Burgess, S. Chandlev, J. Crosfield, Joseph Davies, John Fairclough, Robert Garnett, C. Lister, F. Monks, W. Owen, J. C. Ridgway, T. H. Sutton, J. E. Wright, and T. Morris.— Mr. Farrisobr moved that the best thanks of the meeting be given to Mr. S. B. Stringer for the able way in which he had carried out the duties of treasurer, and also that he be re-elected treasurer for the ensuing year.—Mr. WHILE!' seconded the motion, which was supported by Mr. A. E. FIORDHN and carried unanimously. The audi- tors, Messrs. J. P. Jones and Critchley, were re-elected, and a vote of thanks was passed to them, on the motion of Mr. HORDEN, seconded by Mr. STRINGHR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO LORD R. CHURCHILL'S SON.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO LORD R. CHURCHILL'S SON.</p>
        <p>The eldest son of Lord and Lady' Randolph Churchill, who is staying with his mother and the Dowager Duchess of Marlborough at Branksome Dene, Bourne- mouth, met with an accident on Tuesday afternoon. a e was climbing a tree when a branch on which he was standing broke, and he was precipitated to the ground. No hones were broken, but he was much shaken and , bruised. He is in his nineteenth year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Keen frost and heavy snow have been too much for the generality of footballers again in Warrington and most other places. Many matches which ought to have taken place did not take place ; and many, many heads were battered, in matches which took place' but which ought not to have been played. Save the daring and adventurous spirits of the Association Club, no local " men of the leather" indulged in the game on Satur- day.</p>
        <p>Possibly as an illustration of the effect of this the gentleman who sent our P.D. the laudanum for a Christ- mas box, has sent " an original football story " to us far insertion. Had our journal been connected with " Ebony-eyed, the skull splitter," or " Weird haired James the fluke garroter," series, we might have used it. But we fear to hurt the morals of our football readers and will seek to publish at some remote period, when only Sunday school superintendents read thes e notts. But I will give an outline of the" story" with- out the details.</p>
        <p>It is headed thus : " A GRIM TALE of Wilderspool. Football MURDERED WITHOUT A MURMUR."</p>
        <p>The author states how for some thne the absence of football brought on a deep melancholy; it was the result of the lack of a certain nervous stimulus—now necessary to the nerves—only induced by the excitement of a foot- ball match. The doctor told him this and said in six weeks he would be a raving idiot if he didn't find a sub- stitute for that stimulus. He tried most things to induce excitement. He swallowed his food down the" wrcng " way. But that didn't act. He roused the excitable passions of his bosom next, by allowing his six months old son to fall into the river. It didn't do ; the only result being that when he had walked thigh deep for the youngster the inherited inborn devilry—or lunacy—of his offshoot showed itself. The boy refused to leave that liquid mire of the Mersey ; and looking into his fathers face with " a firm aspect in his voice " he said : "Father —FATHER ! I can never, never leave this spot until from the bowels of this quagmire I have delved my missing chewing gum."</p>
        <p>Six weeks slipped by; and he felt the hand of lunacy growing stronger, stronger, ever stronger, on his brain. He found himself trying to roost on the back yard gate and cluckering like a fowl. He was once a mad horse, leapinz over chairs in the house, and seemed to like it until his wife knocked pim endways with a broom. He turned himself into a brass band ; but cut his mouth badly with the kettle spout, down which he tried to play " The Lost Chord." Then suddenly, he says " I went mad, mad ! mad !" Here the manuscript became marked with tear drops that smelled like sour beer.</p>
        <p>"I went to a match between the Warrington team— playing in their long night shirts on the snow-covered ground—and 15 members of the Warrington Town Council. I stood amongst the audience and knew I was mad ; but no one else did. I wanted to strangle a nice little boy close by ; but found more fun shying pieces of clay at tall Webster and plucking hairs out of the head of an ex-alderman who stood in front.</p>
        <p>"Then all at once I jumped off the grand stand, iiito the field. There had been foul play. One councillor, who generally sits by the window, said, "I want to know if that lamp at—" Another civic gentleman said, " Councillor—you are out of order, sit down !" Then the author states how he commenced to kick the footballers through their night shirts ; a melee arose ; they kicked him all over the field, and were drowning him when he says, " I awoke with a jump. My head was hanging over the side of our bed. My wife was lamming me with a pillow, and shmuting " Herterey, the children want washing for Sunday School !"</p>
        <p>The non-playing of football matches has other effects too. It has caused a great row between Newport and Swinton, The match between them was postponed be- cause the Newport men considered the Swinton field was unfit to play upon. Some thousands of spectators had assembled on the field, and much indignation was ex- pressed.</p>
        <p>We are told that the loss to the Swinton Club in a monetary sense will be large—something near £soo— the loss of the honour of meeting such belauded antagonists was equally great. The Swinton Committee have resolved to return the money to those who are unable to use the tickets for any other match at Swin- ton, and as a consequence of this offer the secretary is receiving batches ot letters from all parts of the coun- try.</p>
        <p>But there are teams which have been able to keep their ground in order. St. Helens for instance have played most of their fixtures lately. They met Batley on Monday and gave them a good game, after they (Batley) had been frozen out at Warrington and Wigan. This was arranged. simply by covering the ground with a layer of straw covered with snow. Mark this Warrington.</p>
        <p>Wales and England met in then. 10th annual fixture on Saturday, at Cardiff. Last week it was feared that the ground would be too hard for play ; but by a process of heating (!) which hag been illustrated in the" Graphic," and described in other journals, the field was kept in a thawed condition. The process alluded to, consisted of keeping a large number of semi-beacon fires ablaze for two days and nights.</p>
        <p>The match was witnessed by quite 20,000 persons. Both teams were as originally selected, with the exe,ep- tion of Allport, the English forward, whose place was taken by Bradshaw, of Bramley. Taylor, the North- umbrian back, came down but did not supercede De Winton. The game throughout was a fast one, Eng- land being on the aggressive most of the game. During the latter portion of the play the Welshmen showed ex- traordinary staying powers; they commenced the aggressive and they won by two goals and two tries, to one goal and three tries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Association team on Saturday en- countered the Liverpool printers in the third round of the Liverpool and district cup competition. It will be remembered that the ntatch was played on the printers' ground at Liverpool, but the Warrington club, who were defeated, forwarded a protest to the Liverpool Associa- tion, and that association ordered the match to be re- played at Warrington. It would seem that the worthy clerk of the weather has a bit of spite against the Warrington lads, for he generally manages on any im- portant occasion, such as last Saturday, to render the field in such a condition that it requires a very heavy team to make any decent exhibition of football on it. A visit to the ground on Saturday morning would have induced any common-sensed person to believe that it was not exactly the ground for a cup competition, there being four or five inches of snow.</p>
        <p>The visitors made a protest against the ground before the game started. But it was all in their favour ; the ground suited them, and they piled on the scores until they had got seven goals ; then they stopped, whilst Warrington men were able to start.</p>
        <p>The tall scoring cannot for a moment be blamed OR the Warrington goal keeper, whose marvellous defence under trying circumstances made him quite a favourite with the spectators. The left full back, Davies, like- wise played a sterling game, and one that would bring credit to any amateur footballer. HiA partner Kineand was considerably' off " and the way the visitors' left outside wing skipped round him time after time was surprising. It was on his side that the majority of the goals were obtained. Philips and Mather, especially the former, were also in very good form, although it was not Mather's day by "long chalks."</p>
        <p>Amongst the forwards Norman was the best, and that as not saving much for him. Of course three of the others are all under nine stone weights, and are in fact mere boys, so not much could be expected from them on such a day. It would not be a foolish step by any means if the Warrington committee were to try some of their reserve forwards and give one or two of the first team a rest. There is at least one forward in reserve who would make a creditable exhibition in the frent rank of the first team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>MINER ASSAULTED BY A POLICEMAN.</head>
        <p>A MINER ASSAULTED BY A POLICEMAN.</p>
        <p>VERDICT FOR £3O DAMAGES.</p>
        <p>_ At Wakefield County Court on Tuesday, a miner named William Webster, living at Floekton, near Wake- field, sued Constalsle Shaw, the village policeman, for compensation for assault. The evidence went to show that on September 12th there were public rejoicings at Flockton on the occasion of the turning of he new water supply. Plaintiff was returning home at night with his daughter, when the policeman, who had a grudge against him, followed them, flashed his lamp in their faces, and then struck plaintiff two violent blows with his truncheon on the head, rendering him uncon- scious. Plaintiff had been unable to work for seventeen weeks. Verdict for plaintiff for £3O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>REPRESENTATION REPRESENTATION OF BARROW.</head>
        <p>THE REPRESENTATION REPRESENTATION OF BARROW.</p>
        <p>ADDRESS BY THE LABOUR CANDIDATE.</p>
        <p>Oh Monday night, Mr. Peter Curran, Parliamentary Labour candidate for Barrow, addressed a crowded</p>
        <p>meeting in the Central Hall. He said he did not address them as a party politician, but as a thorough-going Labour representative. He was in favour of the secret ballot, adult suffrage, Home Rule, not only for Ireland but England, Scotland, and Wales, payment of members, and payment of election expenses out of the rates. He was also in favour of the legislative eight hours day for all staple industries. He vigorously attacked ground rents and royalties, stating that " three noble lords, of Devonshire, Buccleuch, and Muncaster," took in royal- ties, Sze., from the Barrow Steel Company £126,000 a year, while only £63,000 were paid in wages.—A resolu- tion of confidence was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>SIR C. RUSSELL ON THE HOME RULE</head>
        <p>SIR C. RUSSELL ON THE HOME RULE</p>
        <p>BILL. THE MINISTERIAL PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Sir Charles Russell, delivering his presidential address to the Cambridge Liberal Club, on Thursday night, said the expectations of the country not only with respect to legislative reforms, but a sympathetic administration of the existing laws, would be fulfilled by the present Government. Early in the Session Mr. Gladstone would introduce the Home Rule Bill, but after it was read a first time a considerable interval would elapse before its second reading, in order to give opportunity for its quiet consideration, and that interval would be utilised in pressing on such measures as the reform of the law of registration and the affirmation of the principle of one man one vote. The Home Rule Bill would differ from the bill of 1886 in one important particular—the Cabinet would bow to the judgment of the nation in retaining the Irish members at Westminster. While giving ade- quate powers of self-government to the Irish people, it would contain also adequate provision for safeguarding the rights of minorities and the greater interests of the Empire. The chief danger was that it should enter into the minds of the Legislature to confer narrow and grudg- ing powers of self-government on Ireland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-01-14_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>RUNCORN SAVINGS BANK.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SAVINGS BANK.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of trustees and managers was held at the Bank in High-st., on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. T. Brundrit, J.P. the President, oc- cupied the chair, and there were present the Rev. Dr. Preston, Messrs. John Simpson, W. Whittaker, J. Handley, J.P., W. Hankinson, W. E. Lea, Thos. Wright, R. Morris, and W. Handley (actuary). The Actuary submitted the auditors' report as to the correctness of the accounts, and the certificate of the National Debt Commissioners, and read the fol- lowing report showing the usefulness of the Bank in inducing habits of thrift and provi- dence. On Nov. 20th, 1891, the number of open ledger accounts was 1065 ; during the year ended November 20th, 1892,167 accounts were opened and 145 closed, a net gain of 22, the total thus numbering 1087. The total number of transactions amounted to 3,172, consisting of 2,184 deposits and 988 withdrawals, of which number 196 deposits and 113 withdrawals were made on behalf of friendly and charitable societies. Excluding these, the average amount of deposit was £2 18s. 2d. and of withdrawal £7 2s. 7d. The total cash received and paid over the counter amounted to £16,110 4s. 7d. As further evidencing the steady pro- gress of the Bank, attention may appropriately be drawn to the yearly increase in the number of open accounts. At the close of 1885 there were 741 ; 1886, 837 ; 1887, 856 ; 1888, 889 ; 1889, 1,001; 1890, 1,029 ; 1891, 1,065 ; 1892, 1,087. The ledger balances in 1885 amounted to £21,113, and in 1892 to £30,263, an increase in accounts of 346 and amount to £6,150,in addition to which the Bank has purchased and holds of depositors £1,050 in consolidated stock. It is also a satisfactory feature in- dicative of the provident habits of Runcorn that £5,793 or more than one-sixth of the total ledger balances be- long to the various Friendly Societies of the town and district. The cost of management for the year had been 6s. id. or a little over a quarter per cent on the capital of the Bank, and after providing for this there was an increase in the Bank surplus of £7 Os. 9d. the total surplus now standing at £lOl Bs. 7d. The whole of the money received from the depositors with the exception of the amount absolutely required by the treasurer to meet current withdrawals, is invested with the National Debt Commissioners which is attested by their certifi- cate now produced. The attention and solicitous watch- fulness of the treasurer is shown by the fact that the Bank , has not been called upon to pay any charges for coin- mission on the manipulation of the turnover, bu'; has received the sum of £1 lls. 10d.' as interest on the com- paratively small amount in his hands. By the recent Savings Banks Act this limit of total deposit has been raised from £l5O to £2OO, exclusive of interest ;and any sum withdrawn during the year may be replaced in addition to the ordinary limit of £3O in any one year. Depositors are increasingly availing themselves of the advantage of investing in consolidated stock. By this means a depositor can have to his credit a total of £5OO i.e. £2OO on deposit account, and £3OO in consols, this being an absolutely safe investment, yielding at current prices about 3 per cent.—The Chairman moved the adoption of the report, which was so thorough in its character that he was relieved of the responsibility of giving the meeting any information. For some time it had been his wish that the deposits should reach the sum of £30,000 and he was glad that that amount had at last been attained. This fact showed that the in- terest in the bank had not flagged during the forty years it had been in existence, that it had gone on improv- ing, and that habits of thrift amongst the people had been increasing. His interest in the bank had never flagged, and he was proud to be in the position of pre- sident of so admiralale an institution. (Applause.) Mr. R. Morris seconded the motion, which was carried unanimously. Mr. W. Whittaker proposed a vote of thanks to the , treasurer, Mr. J. Simpson, who had filled the p)sition ' for t very long time to the satisfaction of everybody.— Mr. W. Hankinson seconded the motion. Carried.—ln reply, Mr. Simpson mentioned that the bank allowed depositors to have their money when they wanted it, and every one seemed satisfied. (Applause.) Mr. J. Handley proposed a vote of thanks to the auditors (Messrs. J. H. Salkeld, E. Marwood, William Williamson and C. Pownall), and that their remuneration be fixed at £3 each, which was an increase of 10s. Mr. Handley explained that increased duties would devolve upon the auditors in consequence of a new Act of Parliament.— Mr. W. E. Lea seconded the motion. Carried.—The Rev. Dr. Preston proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Brundrit for his continued interest in the bank, as its president and chairman.—Dr. Preston said he was not going to enter into fulsome laudation of Mr. Brundrit, because they all knew what he was, and they would agree with him that he was a sound, solid gentleman, full of philanthropy, and taking a deep interest in any- thing that concerned Runcorn. He sincerely trusted, and he was sure that he was only echoing the feeling of all of them, that he might long be spared to fill the honoured position of president of the bank. The speaker said the trustees and managers might fittingly congratulate themselves on the satisfactory position, and growing powers of the bank. While other institu- tions were gying to pieces through mismanagement or defalcations, their bank was increasing in usefulness and power, and it ought to be more widely known that those who deposited money with them could always get their money without waiting until a given notice had expired.—Mr. Wright seconded the motion, which was carried.—The Chairman, in acknowledging the vote, said he hoped, to continue his interest in the Bank for many years to come. He was glad to see the prosperous condition in which it was now. Alluding to the surplus in the Bank, he said he thought they might increase the salary of their actuary, because he was a valuable servant, for a great deal was due to him for the efficient management, and for their accounts being so well kept that the auditors expressed themselves so well satisfied with the general state of affairs. He thought they might fairly and reasonably offer him £lO a year more ; and he had pleasure in moving a resolution to that effect.— Dr. Preston endorsed the Chairman's remarks, and seconded the motion with pleasure, and asked if the surplus could not be used in increasing the interest paid to depositors.—The Actuary said the Government did not allow them to pay more than 2i per cent. on their deposits.—The resolution was carried unanimously. The Actuary, in thanking the managers, said it was a source of great pleasure to him that his work had been appreciated,and he should do his best in the future,as he had in the past.—The meeting then concluded.—The statement of accounts appears in our advertising columns.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>A wedding which excited some interest in the neighbourhood of Hesketh Park, Southport, was solemnised at All Saints' Church on Tuesday, when Miss Annie Louise Robinson, of Wynberg. 9, Park- mad, was united in the bonds of matrimony to Mr. Finlay Dun, solicitor, of Liverpool. The bride is a daughter of the late Mr. Peter Robinson, glass manufacturer, of Warrington, whilst the briegroom is a son of Mr. John Dun, of Rock Villa, Latchford, Warrington, and managing director of Parr's and the Alliance Bank. As the guests were assembling in the church, the organist, Mr, A. J. J. Beaty, played an appropriate voluntary, and upon the arrival of the bride, under the escort of her brother' Mr. Wm. Robinson, he played the " Bridal March," from Lohengrin. The service was impressively conducted by the Vicar, the Rev. Dr. Porter, and as the bridal party left the church, the joyous strains of Mendelssohn's " Wedding March" pealed forth from the organ. The bride, who was the object of generally expressed admira- tion, wore a gown of white satin brocade, trimmed with chiffon, and at her throat was a diamond crescent brooch, the gift of the bridegroom's mother. Her hat was of white chip, trimmed with ostrich feathers, and the whole costume was most effective. The dresses of the bridesmaids, Miss Dora Rylands, of Highfield, Thel- • wall, Cheshire, and Miss Louise Dun, of Northampton. cousin of the bride,were composed of white corduroy,with sleeves and trimmings of green velvet, and hats to match. The duties of best man were efficiently dio. charged by Mr. J. A. Dun, brother of the brideiroom, and the bride was given away by her brother. After the ceremony the wedding breakfast and reception were held at Wvnberg. Amongst the guests present were Mr. and Mrs. John Dun (Latchford), Mr. Finlay Dun (Edin- burgh), Mr. and Mrs. N. Mathieson, Mr. and Mrs. John Murray (Frodsharn), Mr. and Mrs. Maples (Liverpool),. Mr. and Mrs. John White (Latchford), Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Edelsten (Warrington), Mr. and Mrs. T. Mathie- son, Rev. Dr. Porter, Mr. and Mrs. Robert Morris, Mr. J. A. Dun, Mr. R. H. Dun, Mr. T. B. Rylands, Miss Flutch- ings, Miss Lewis, &amp;c., &amp;c. After the luncheon a receP- tion was held when a large number of friends assembled. The bride and bridegroom started for London at 4-25 amidst the congratulations of their friends and a plentiful sprinkling of rice. The following is a list of the presents : Bridegroom to bride, ruby and diamond ring ; Bride to bridegrcom, gold watch chain ; Mrs. Robinson, house- hold linen; Mr. William Robinson, coin; le e se-vice of glass, china breakfast service, and Crown Derby tea ser- vice; Mr. Dun to bride, cheque, dressing bag with silver and ivory fittings, and fur-lined cloak ; Mr. Dun to tride• groom, fur-lined travelling coat ; Mrs. Dun to bride, dia- mond new moon brooch; Mrs. Dun to br:slegroom, writing table ; Miss Robinson, drawing room chairs and afternoon tea tray • Mr. and Mrs. Murray, Worcester v,ses ; Mr. and Mrs. T. Mathieson, dining room clock and ornaments ; Mr. J. A. Dun, case of silver fruit spoons ; Mr. R. H. Dun, carvers ' • the Misses Dun, silver biscuit box, salver, and silver wine strainer ; Mr. and Mrs. J. A. Edelsten silver csndlesticks and lamp ; Mr Finlay Dun (Bdinburgh) breakfast dish ; the Rev. James and Mrs. Dun,: butter dish Mr. and Mrs. Mathieson, silver tea service ; Mr. and Mrs. Maples, artist proof print. after Rosa Bonheur ; Mr. and 55 ii. T. G. Rylands, fish knives and forks ; Mr. and Mrs. Malin Edelsten, silver tea service; Mr. and Mrs. Murphy. Crown. Derby tea service; Mr. and Mrs. C. Hutchings, two silver spoons ; Mrs. Morland Crosfield, table (poker work) •, Mr. and Mrs. E. M. Crosfield, chair (poker work) • Mrs. Peter Rylands, carvers ; Mr. and Mrs. R. Morris, fruit stand ; Mr. and Miss Sauerlander, gong : Miss Hutchings, Wor- cester vase ; Miss G. Rylands, butter dish ; Dr. and Mrs. Davies. silver salts " • Miss Reid (Belfast), after' noon tea cloth; Mr. and